var L=[
{t:"What is cryogenics?",lead:"The science and engineering of very low temperatures.",
h:"<p>Cryogenics deals with temperatures below about <b>120 K</b> (−153 °C). That threshold is a convention: it separates the gases that liquefy only at very low temperatures, such as nitrogen, hydrogen, helium and oxygen, from refrigerants used in ordinary cooling.</p><div class=fig><canvas id=fx width=640 height=200></canvas><label for=fxs>Temperature: <b id=fxv></b></label><input type=range id=fxs min=4 max=300 value=300><div class=cap>Animation: molecules move more slowly as the temperature falls.</div></div><h3>The Kelvin scale</h3><p>Cryogenic work uses <b>kelvin</b>, an absolute scale where 0 K is the lowest possible temperature. To convert, K = °C + 273.15. Room temperature is about 293 K, liquid nitrogen boils at 77 K and liquid helium at 4.2 K.</p><h3>Why go cold?</h3><p>Cooling lets us liquefy gases for compact storage and transport, makes some materials superconduct, reduces thermal noise in sensitive detectors, and preserves biological samples.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Cryogenic range: below roughly 120 K</li><li>Use kelvin, where K = °C + 273.15</li><li>Main motives: liquefaction, superconductivity, low noise, preservation</li></ul></div>",
q:"Liquid nitrogen boils at 77 K. What is that in °C?",o:["−77 °C","−196 °C","−350 °C"],a:1,e:"77 − 273.15 ≈ −196 °C."},
{t:"Cryogenic fluids",lead:"The common liquids and their properties.",
h:"<p>Each cryogen has a normal boiling point, the temperature at which it boils at atmospheric pressure. The lower it is, the harder the fluid is to liquefy and keep cold.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Fluid</th><th>Boiling point (K)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Helium-4</td><td>4.22</td><td>Superconducting magnets, very low temperature research</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrogen</td><td>20.3</td><td>Fuel; flammable over a wide range</td></tr><tr><td>Neon</td><td>27.1</td><td>Specialty cooling</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>77.4</td><td>Cheap, inert, widely used</td></tr><tr><td>Argon</td><td>87.3</td><td>Industrial gas</td></tr><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>90.2</td><td>Oxidiser; fire hazard</td></tr><tr><td>Methane (LNG)</td><td>111.7</td><td>Natural gas transport</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=fig><canvas id=fx width=640 height=220></canvas><label for=fxs>Heat leak: <b id=fxv></b></label><input type=range id=fxs min=1 max=10 value=3><div class=cap>Animation: more heat leak means more bubbling and faster boil-off.</div></div><h3>Large expansion ratio</h3><p>When a cryogenic liquid warms and vaporises, it expands enormously. Liquid nitrogen expands to about 694 times its volume as gas at room temperature. In a sealed container this creates dangerous pressure, which is why cryogenic vessels need relief devices.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Lower boiling point means harder to liquefy and store</li><li>Nitrogen is the workhorse; helium reaches the lowest temperatures</li><li>Vaporisation causes huge expansion and pressure rise</li></ul></div>",
q:"Which cryogen has the lowest boiling point?",o:["Nitrogen","Helium","Oxygen"],a:1,e:"Helium-4 boils at about 4.2 K, lower than any other common fluid."},
{t:"Refrigeration and liquefaction",lead:"How heat is pumped out at low temperatures.",
h:"<p>A refrigerator moves heat from a cold space to a warmer one, and that takes work. The best possible performance is set by the <b>Carnot limit</b>: the minimum work per unit of heat removed is</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>W / Q<sub>c</sub> = (T<sub>h</sub> − T<sub>c</sub>) / T<sub>c</sub></b></p><p>Try it below. The colder you go, the more work each watt of cooling costs, and real machines need several times the Carnot minimum.</p><div class=calc><label for=tc><b>Cold temperature T<sub>c</sub>: <span id=tcv>77</span> K</b> (hot side fixed at 300 K)</label><input type=range id=tc min=2 max=290 value=77><div class=res><div><b id=cw>–</b><small>W per W of cooling (ideal)</small></div><div><b id=cc>–</b><small>Ideal COP</small></div></div></div><h3>Main methods</h3><ul><li><b>Joule–Thomson expansion:</b> a gas cools when it expands through a valve, but only below its inversion temperature (helium about 43 K, hydrogen about 200 K, nitrogen about 620 K).</li><li><b>Linde and Claude cycles:</b> liquefy gas using heat exchangers, with Claude adding an expansion engine for extra cooling.</li><li><b>Brayton and Stirling cryocoolers:</b> compact machines for small cooling loads.</li></ul><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Carnot work grows rapidly as T<sub>c</sub> falls</li><li>Hydrogen and helium must be pre-cooled before Joule–Thomson cooling works</li><li>Cryocoolers suit small loads, liquefiers suit large ones</li></ul></div>",
q:"Why does cooling to 4 K cost far more work per watt than cooling to 77 K?",o:["Helium is expensive","The Carnot work is (Th − Tc)/Tc, which grows as Tc falls","Cold machines have more friction"],a:1,e:"The ideal work per watt rises steeply as the cold temperature drops, about 70 W/W at 4.2 K against about 3.9 W/W at 77 K."},
{t:"Heat transfer and insulation",lead:"Every cryogenic machine is a fight against heat. Here are the three ways heat gets in, and how to block each one.",h:`<p>Heat always flows from hot to cold, and a cryogenic system is a very cold object in a very warm room. Between a 300 K laboratory and liquid nitrogen at 77 K there is a temperature difference of 223 K, and between the room and liquid helium it is nearly 296 K. Every watt of heat that leaks in boils away liquid, or has to be pumped out by a refrigerator at a high cost. So the whole art of cryogenic design is to keep heat out. Heat travels in three ways. Watch them below, and use the slider to change the temperature difference.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=bhf viewBox='0 0 640 290' role=img aria-label='Three animated panels: conduction through a metal bar, convection in a fluid layer, and radiation between two plates'></svg><label for=bhd><b>Temperature difference = <span id=bhdv>200</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=bhd min=10 max=300 step=5 value=200><div><button class=btn id=bhs>Add a radiation shield</button></div><div class=cap>Left: atoms in the hot end vibrate harder and pass the energy along. Middle: warm fluid rises and cold fluid sinks, carrying heat. Right: warm surfaces send out infrared radiation; a shield between the plates cuts the flow.</div></div><h3>1. Conduction: heat passing through a solid</h3><p>In a solid, atoms vibrate. The hotter end vibrates harder, and the vibration is handed on from atom to atom until the energy reaches the cold end. In a metal, free electrons carry heat too, which is why metals conduct so well. The rate of heat flow is given by Fourier's law:</p><p><b>Q = k × A × ΔT / L</b></p><p>Here Q is the heat flow in watts, k is the thermal conductivity of the material, A is the cross-section area, ΔT is the temperature difference and L is the length. To keep heat out you want a <b>small A</b>, a <b>long L</b> and a <b>low k</b>. That is why a cryostat hangs from a thin-walled stainless-steel tube rather than a copper rod: stainless steel conducts roughly 25 times less than copper at room temperature. A glass-fibre composite such as G-10 conducts even less.</p><h3>2. Convection: heat carried by moving fluid</h3><p>When a gas or liquid is warmed, it expands and becomes lighter, so it rises; when cooled, it sinks. This sets up a circulation that carries heat from the hot surface to the cold one far faster than conduction alone. The rate is Q = h × A × ΔT, where h is the heat transfer coefficient. The cure is simple: take the fluid away. The space between the inner and outer walls of a cryogenic vessel is pumped to a high vacuum, so there is no gas left to carry heat. Convection also matters inside the liquid itself, and in the gas flowing out of a vent, which we study later.</p><h3>3. Radiation: heat as light you cannot see</h3><p>Every surface sends out infrared radiation, and the amount rises steeply with the temperature, as the fourth power of the absolute temperature. For two surfaces, Q = σ × ε × A × (T₁⁴ − T₂⁴), where σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W/m²·K⁴ and ε is the emissivity of the surface, between 0 and 1. Radiation needs no matter at all, so a vacuum does not stop it. Two things reduce it. A bright polished metal has a low emissivity, so it radiates and absorbs little. And <b>shields</b> placed between the warm and cold surfaces absorb the radiation and re-emit part of it: each extra shield cuts the flow roughly in proportion. Many thin, shiny layers together make <b>multilayer insulation (MLI)</b>.</p><h3>How much heat reaches the liquid?</h3><p>Try your own vessel. The vessel is cooled by liquid nitrogen at 77 K and sits in a 300 K room. Choose the vacuum quality, the radiation shielding and the supporting neck, and read off the heat load and how much liquid it boils away. The numbers are simple estimates (a stainless-steel neck with an average conductivity of about 12 W/m·K between 77 K and 300 K), good enough to compare designs.</p><div class=calc><div class=res id=bhr></div><label for=bhv><b>Insulating vacuum</b></label><select id=bhv><option value=0>High vacuum (10⁻⁵ mbar)</option><option value=1>Poor vacuum (10⁻² mbar)</option><option value=2>No vacuum (air at atmospheric pressure, 1 cm gap)</option></select><label for=bha><b>Vessel surface area = <span id=bhav>0.2</span> m²</b></label><input type=range id=bha min=0.05 max=1 step=0.01 value=0.2><label for=bhe><b>Surface emissivity = <span id=bhev>0.10</span></b></label><input type=range id=bhe min=0.02 max=0.9 step=0.01 value=0.1><label for=bhn><b>Layers of shielding (MLI) = <span id=bhnv>0</span></b></label><input type=range id=bhn min=0 max=30 step=1 value=0><label for=bhl><b>Neck length = <span id=bhlv>20</span> cm</b></label><input type=range id=bhl min=5 max=50 step=1 value=20><label for=bhc><b>Neck cross-section area = <span id=bhcv>0.5</span> cm²</b></label><input type=range id=bhc min=0.1 max=3 step=0.1 value=0.5><label for=bhw><b>Liquid volume = <span id=bhwv>10</span> L</b></label><input type=range id=bhw min=1 max=100 step=1 value=10><div class=cap id=bhn2></div></div><p>Experiment with it. With no vacuum, the gas dominates and the vessel loses liquid in hours. With a good vacuum, radiation takes over; adding shield layers cuts it by about the number of layers plus one. In the end the neck, which you cannot make infinitely long or thin, sets the floor. This three-way balance is the basis of every cryostat and storage tank, and later lessons explore each route in detail.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Heat gets in by conduction, convection and radiation; all three matter</li><li>Conduction: small area, long length, low conductivity (Q = kAΔT/L)</li><li>Convection: take the gas away with a vacuum</li><li>Radiation: low emissivity and many shields (σεA(T₁⁴ − T₂⁴))</li><li>Heat leak shows up as boil-off: every watt boils off liquid</li></ul></div>`,
q:"What does the vacuum space in a cryogenic vessel mainly stop?",o:["Conduction and convection through gas","Pressure build-up inside the liquid","Chemical reactions"],a:0,e:"Removing gas removes convection and nearly all gas conduction. MLI then handles radiation."},
{t:"Materials at low temperature",lead:"Why some materials fail in the cold.",
h:"<p>Cooling changes how materials behave. Many steels become brittle and can crack suddenly, a change called the <b>ductile-to-brittle transition</b>.</p><h3>What works</h3><ul><li><b>Austenitic stainless steels</b> (such as 304 and 316) stay tough.</li><li><b>Aluminium alloys</b> and <b>copper</b> keep their ductility.</li><li><b>9% nickel steel</b> is used for LNG tanks.</li></ul><h3>What to avoid</h3><ul><li>Plain carbon steels and many plastics, which can turn brittle.</li></ul><h3>Thermal contraction</h3><p>Materials shrink as they cool. Stainless steel contracts roughly 0.3% between room temperature and 77 K, so a 10 m pipe shortens by about 3 cm. Designs need bellows, loops or sliding supports to absorb this.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Choose materials that stay ductile when cold</li><li>Austenitic stainless, aluminium and copper are common choices</li><li>Plan for contraction in piping and supports</li></ul></div>",
q:"A 10 m stainless pipe is cooled to 77 K. Roughly how much does it shorten?",o:["About 3 mm","About 3 cm","About 30 cm"],a:1,e:"About 0.3% of 10 m is roughly 3 cm."},
{t:"Applications",lead:"Where cryogenics is used.",
h:"<ul><li><b>Medical imaging:</b> MRI scanners use superconducting magnets cooled by liquid helium.</li><li><b>Energy:</b> LNG is cooled to about 112 K so it takes up roughly 600 times less volume.</li><li><b>Space:</b> liquid hydrogen and oxygen are high-performance rocket propellants.</li><li><b>Fusion and physics:</b> superconducting magnets and cryogenic pumping at facilities such as particle accelerators and fusion devices.</li><li><b>Biology:</b> cryopreservation of cells and tissue in liquid nitrogen.</li><li><b>Electronics and sensing:</b> cooled detectors and quantum devices.</li></ul><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Superconductivity is the biggest driver for the lowest temperatures</li><li>Liquefaction makes energy fuels practical to store and ship</li></ul></div>",
q:"Why is natural gas liquefied for shipping?",o:["To make it burn faster","To shrink its volume about 600 times","To remove impurities only"],a:1,e:"Liquefaction cuts the volume by roughly 600, so far more fits in a ship."},
{t:"Safety",lead:"The hazards you must respect.",
h:"<ul><li><b>Asphyxiation:</b> boiling cryogens displace air. Work in ventilated spaces and use oxygen monitors in enclosed rooms.</li><li><b>Cold burns and frostbite:</b> contact with the liquid or cold surfaces damages skin and eyes. Wear insulated gloves, a face shield and closed footwear.</li><li><b>Over-pressure:</b> trapped liquid expanding in a closed section can burst equipment. Never seal a cryogenic line without a relief device.</li><li><b>Oxygen enrichment:</b> liquid nitrogen can condense oxygen from air, creating a fire risk. Liquid oxygen itself strongly supports combustion.</li><li><b>Flammable fluids:</b> hydrogen and methane need ignition control and good ventilation.</li></ul><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Ventilate, monitor oxygen, protect skin and eyes</li><li>Always provide pressure relief</li><li>Follow your institution’s procedures and training</li></ul></div>",
q:"Why must a closed section of cryogenic pipe always have a relief device?",o:["Trapped liquid that warms expands hugely and can burst it","It reduces heat leak","It prevents contraction"],a:0,e:"Vaporisation can raise pressure enormously in a sealed space."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryogens and their properties",lead:"Uses, history, T–s diagrams, and what a watt of heat leak does to each liquid.",h:`
<h3>Where cryogenics is used</h3>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Sector</th><th>Examples</th><th>Typical cryogen</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Space</td><td>Rocket propulsion, cooling infrared detectors, space-simulation chambers</td><td>LH<sub>2</sub> and LOX; LN<sub>2</sub> for cold walls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mechanical</td><td>Magnetic separation, cryogenic treatment of tools and dies, recycling rubber and plastics</td><td>Superconducting magnets; LN<sub>2</sub></td></tr>
<tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Cryosurgery, preserving cells, blood, organs and food</td><td>LN<sub>2</sub></td></tr>
<tr><td>Gas industry</td><td>Liquefying, separating, storing and shipping gases; oxygen for steelmaking; inert atmospheres for welding</td><td>LOX, LN<sub>2</sub>, liquid argon, LNG</td></tr>
<tr><td>Superconductivity</td><td>NMR and MRI magnets, maglev trains, transformers and generators</td><td>Liquid helium, LN<sub>2</sub></td></tr>
<tr><td>High-energy physics</td><td>A 27 km accelerator ring with magnets at 1.9 K inside a 77 K liquid nitrogen shield; the ITER fusion magnets cooled by helium gas</td><td>Helium, LN<sub>2</sub></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>A short chronology</h3>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1877</td><td>Cailletet and Pictet liquefy oxygen</td></tr>
<tr><td>1879</td><td>Linde founds Linde Eismaschinen AG</td></tr>
<tr><td>1892</td><td>Dewar develops the vacuum-insulated storage vessel</td></tr>
<tr><td>1895</td><td>Onnes establishes the Leiden laboratory</td></tr>
<tr><td>1902</td><td>Claude establishes l'Air Liquide and develops an air-liquefaction system</td></tr>
<tr><td>1908</td><td>Onnes liquefies helium</td></tr>
<tr><td>1911</td><td>Onnes discovers superconductivity</td></tr>
<tr><td>1926</td><td>Goddard test-fires the first cryogenically fuelled rocket</td></tr>
<tr><td>1934</td><td>Kapitza designs the first turbo-expansion engine</td></tr>
<tr><td>1952</td><td>The Cryogenic Engineering Laboratory is set up at NIST, USA</td></tr>
<tr><td>1966</td><td>The dilution refrigerator is developed</td></tr>
<tr><td>1975</td><td>Record superconducting transition temperature of 23 K</td></tr>
<tr><td>1994</td><td>Matsubara develops a 4 K cryocooler</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>What counts as a cryogen</h3>
<p>The course defines cryogenics as the science and technology of temperatures below 123 K, and a cryogen as a fluid whose normal boiling point is below that. Ordinary refrigerants such as R134a (about 247 K) or propane (231 K) boil far above it.</p>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Fluid</th><th>Boiling point (K)</th><th>Triple point (K)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Methane</td><td>111.67</td><td>90.69</td></tr><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>90.19</td><td>54.36</td></tr><tr><td>Argon</td><td>87.30</td><td>83.81</td></tr><tr><td>Air</td><td>78.6</td><td>59.75</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>77.36</td><td>63.15</td></tr><tr><td>Normal hydrogen</td><td>20.39</td><td>13.96</td></tr><tr><td>Helium-4</td><td>4.230</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>Helium-3</td><td>3.191</td><td>none</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Reading a T–s diagram</h3>
<div class=fig><svg class=sv viewBox='0 0 640 260' role=img aria-label='Schematic T-s diagram with the liquid-vapour dome and a one atmosphere isobar'><path class=o d='M60,235 V20 M60,235 H620' style='stroke-width:1.5' /><text x=68 y=24>Temperature</text><text x=618 y=252 text-anchor='end'>Entropy</text><path class=o d='M110,215 Q200,90 320,55 Q440,100 540,215' /><circle cx=320 cy=55 r=4 style='fill:var(--text)' /><text x=320 y=42 text-anchor='middle'>critical point</text><path class=c d='M590,40 Q520,80 477,150 L167,150 Q152,160 138,205' /><text x=598 y=30 text-anchor='end'>gas at 300 K, 1 atm</text><text x=330 y=200 text-anchor='middle'>liquid + vapour</text><text x=176 y=168>normal boiling point</text><text x=100 y=110>liquid</text><text x=560 y=100>vapour</text><circle class=mv r=6 style='fill:#d9534f'><animateMotion dur='7s' repeatCount='indefinite' path='M590,40 Q520,80 477,150 L167,150 Q152,160 138,205' /></circle><text x=320 y=250 text-anchor='middle' style='font-size:11px'>Schematic only, not to scale</text></svg><div class=cap>Animation: a gas cooled at constant pressure (1 atm) condenses along the flat part of the isobar, then cools further as a liquid.</div></div>
<p>Vertical lines on a T–s diagram are lines of constant entropy, so an ideal expansion is a vertical drop. The region under the dome holds liquid and vapour together. The lecture gives these critical points: nitrogen 33.9 atm at 126 K, hydrogen 13.15 atm at 33.19 K, and helium-4 2.27 atm at 5.25 K.</p>
<h3>Properties of the common cryogens</h3>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Saturated liquid at 1 atm</th><th>LHe</th><th>LH<sub>2</sub></th><th>LN<sub>2</sub></th><th>Liquid air</th><th>LOX</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Boiling point (K)</td><td>4.214</td><td>20.27</td><td>77.36</td><td>78.8</td><td>90.18</td></tr>
<tr><td>Critical pressure (MPa)</td><td>0.229</td><td>1.315</td><td>3.39</td><td>3.92</td><td>5.08</td></tr>
<tr><td>Density (kg/m³)</td><td>124.8</td><td>70.79</td><td>807.3</td><td>874</td><td>1141</td></tr>
<tr><td>Latent heat (kJ/kg)</td><td>20.90</td><td>443</td><td>199.3</td><td>205</td><td>213</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class=cap>Other slides in the same course give slightly different helium values (for example a latent heat of 20.28 kJ/kg), so expect small differences between sources.</p>
<h3>What does a watt of heat leak do?</h3>
<p>The heat needed to boil one litre is density times latent heat. Pick a liquid and a heat leak to see how quickly it boils away.</p>
<div class=calc><label for=bos><b>Cryogen</b></label><select id=bos></select><label for=bol style='margin-top:10px'><b>Heat leak: <span id=bov>1.0</span> W</b></label><input type=range id=bol min=0.1 max=20 step=0.1 value=1><div class=res id=boo></div><div id=bob style='margin-top:12px'></div><div class=cap id=bon></div></div>
<p>One watt boils about 1.4 litres of helium per hour but only about 22 millilitres of nitrogen. That factor of roughly 60 is why helium vessels are wrapped in a liquid nitrogen shield.</p>
<div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>A cryogen boils below 123 K; the dome on a T–s diagram is the liquid + vapour region</li><li>Boil-off depends on density times latent heat: about 2.6 kJ per litre for helium, 161 kJ for nitrogen</li><li>Helium and hydrogen are the hardest to hold because of their low latent heat per litre</li></ul></div>`,q:"For the same heat leak, which cryogen boils away fastest per litre?",o:["Liquid nitrogen","Liquid helium","Liquid oxygen"],a:1,e:"Helium absorbs only about 2.6 kJ per litre as it boils, against about 161 kJ for nitrogen and 243 kJ for oxygen."},
{t:"Advanced: Hydrogen and helium",lead:"Ortho and para hydrogen, the phase diagram of helium, and the strange behaviour of superfluid He II.",h:`
<h3>Hydrogen</h3>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Normal boiling point</td><td>20.27 K</td></tr><tr><td>Normal freezing point</td><td>13.95 K</td></tr><tr><td>Critical point</td><td>33.19 K, 1.315 MPa</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid density</td><td>70.79 kg/m³ (vapour at 1 atm: 1.33 kg/m³)</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat</td><td>443 kJ/kg</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Hydrogen has three isotopes: ordinary hydrogen, deuterium (one proton and one neutron) and tritium, which is very rare and radioactive with a half-life of 12.5 years.</p>
<h3>Ortho and para hydrogen</h3>
<div class=fig><svg class=sv viewBox='0 0 640 180' role=img aria-label='Ortho hydrogen with parallel nuclear spins and para hydrogen with opposite spins'><circle class=o cx=130 cy=95 r=18 /><circle class=o cx=190 cy=95 r=18 /><path class=c d='M130,92 V38 M130,38 l-6,10 M130,38 l6,10' /><path class=c d='M190,92 V38 M190,38 l-6,10 M190,38 l6,10' /><text x=160 y=150 text-anchor='middle'>Ortho: spins parallel</text><circle class=o cx=450 cy=95 r=18 /><circle class=o cx=510 cy=95 r=18 /><path class=c d='M450,92 V38 M450,38 l-6,10 M450,38 l6,10' /><path class=h d='M510,98 V152 M510,152 l-6,-10 M510,152 l6,-10' /><text x=480 y=172 text-anchor='middle'>Para: spins opposite (lower energy)</text></svg><div class=cap>Arrows show the direction of the nuclear spin of each proton in the H<sub>2</sub> molecule.</div></div>
<p>If the two proton spins point the same way the molecule is ortho hydrogen; if they point opposite ways it is para hydrogen. Para is the low-energy form. At 300 K the mix is 75% ortho and 25% para (normal hydrogen), but at 20 K the equilibrium is almost entirely para, 99.8%.</p>
<p>The conversion from ortho to para releases heat and is very slow, so catalysts are used to speed it up. Without conversion during liquefaction, the heat released later in storage would evaporate about 70% of the liquid originally made. That makes complete conversion an essential part of making and storing liquid hydrogen.</p>
<div class=calc><label for=ops><b>Temperature: <span id=opv>77</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=ops min=20 max=300 step=1 value=77><div class=res id=opo></div><svg class=sv id=opg viewBox='0 0 640 230' style='margin-top:12px'></svg><div class=cap>Equilibrium composition calculated from the rotational energy levels and nuclear-spin weights of H<sub>2</sub> and D<sub>2</sub>. Hollow circles are the lecture's values at 300 K and 20 K. Deuterium behaves the opposite way: it becomes mostly ortho when cold.</div></div>
<h3>Helium</h3>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Helium-4</th><th>Helium-3</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Normal boiling point (K)</td><td>about 4.2</td><td>3.19</td></tr><tr><td>Critical temperature (K)</td><td>5.25</td><td>3.32</td></tr><tr><td>Critical pressure (MPa)</td><td>0.227</td><td>0.117</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid density (kg/m³)</td><td>124.8</td><td>58.9</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat (kJ/kg)</td><td>20.28</td><td>8.49</td></tr><tr><td>Natural abundance</td><td>about 100%</td><td>1.3 × 10⁻⁴ %</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Helium-3 is rare and hard to separate from helium-4; it was first liquefied in 1948. Helium-4 is a monatomic inert gas, first noticed as a spectral line in sunlight in 1868 and liquefied by Onnes in 1908.</p>
<h3>The phase diagram of helium-4</h3>
<div class=fig><svg class=sv viewBox='0 0 640 250' role=img aria-label='Schematic phase diagram of helium-4 showing solid, He I, He II, vapour, the lambda line and the critical point'><path class=o d='M60,220 V20 M60,220 H620' style='stroke-width:1.5' /><text x=68 y=24>Pressure</text><text x=618 y=240 text-anchor='end'>Temperature (K)</text><path class=o d='M60,74 Q250,68 520,30' /><path class=o d='M100,218 Q330,214 461,185' /><path class=h d='M227,214 L246,72' /><circle cx=461 cy=185 r=4 style='fill:var(--text)' /><text x=470 y=182>critical point 5.25 K</text><text x=120 y=50>solid (above about 25 bar)</text><text x=262 y=130>He I (normal liquid)</text><text x=70 y=150>He II</text><text x=70 y=166>(superfluid)</text><text x=300 y=208>vapour</text><text x=234 y=60 style='fill:#d9534f'>lambda line, 2.17 K at the saturated liquid line</text><text x=320 y=244 text-anchor='middle' style='font-size:11px'>Schematic only, not to scale</text></svg></div>
<p>Helium has no triple point: it has no pressure and temperature at which solid, liquid and vapour coexist, and the liquid has to be compressed to about 25.3 bar to solidify. When liquid helium is cooled below 2.17 K (the lambda point) a second liquid appears. The boundary is the lambda line, the normal liquid above it is He I, and the superfluid below it is He II.</p>
<h3>Superfluid helium: the Rollin film</h3>
<div class=fig><canvas id=hec width=640 height=260></canvas><button id=heb class=tgl>Liquid at 1.8 K (He II): switch to 2.5 K</button><div class=cap>Animation: a cup of liquid helium lifted out of the bath. As He II it empties itself through a thin creeping film; as He I it stays put.</div></div>
<p>He II shows zero viscosity when flowing through narrow channels, very high thermal conductivity, and it flows through slits and powders that stop ordinary liquids. It also clings to walls as a film about 30 nanometres thick, which creeps up and over container walls. The flow rate does not depend on the height of the barrier, it is zero at the lambda point and becomes constant below about 1.5 K. A He II container must therefore be designed so the film cannot creep into warm regions and evaporate.</p>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Effect</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Rollin film (1937)</td><td>A thin film creeps over walls against gravity, filling or emptying vessels</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thermomechanical effect (1938)</td><td>Heating liquid inside a container joined by a narrow channel raises its pressure head, because superfluid flows in towards the heat but normal fluid cannot flow out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mechanocaloric effect (1939)</td><td>Superfluid flows easily through fine powder, leaving normal fluid behind, so the flask warms up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fountain effect</td><td>Heating a powder-filled tube in a He II bath draws superfluid in and squirts it from a capillary at the top</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Three kinds of sound</h3>
<ul><li><b>First sound:</b> ordinary pressure and density waves, above and below the lambda point.</li><li><b>Second sound:</b> below the lambda point the normal and superfluid components can oscillate against each other, giving a temperature wave. Its speed is zero at the lambda point.</li><li><b>Third sound:</b> in thin films only the superfluid moves, seen as waves in the film thickness, with a speed of about 0.5 m/s according to the lecture.</li></ul>
<p class=cap>The lecture quotes up to 239 m/s for second sound near 0 K. That is close to the speed of ordinary first sound in helium, and references usually give second sound as much slower, so check this value in a standard source before relying on it.</p>
<div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Hydrogen must be converted from ortho to para during liquefaction, or storage losses are huge</li><li>Helium has no triple point; below 2.17 K liquid helium becomes superfluid He II</li><li>He II creeps, conducts heat extremely well and flows without viscosity through fine channels</li></ul></div>`,q:"A cup of liquid helium held at 1.8 K is lifted above the bath. What happens?",o:["Nothing, the liquid stays in the cup","It slowly empties as a thin film creeps over the wall","It boils violently and shatters the cup"],a:1,e:"Below the lambda point He II forms a thin creeping film, so the cup drains. At 2.5 K (He I) this does not happen."},
{t:"Advanced: Superfluid helium (He II)",lead:"The liquid that flows without friction, carries heat faster than copper and cools the world's biggest accelerators.",h:`<h3>A liquid that refuses to freeze</h3><p>Helium has no triple point. Under its own vapour pressure it stays liquid all the way down to absolute zero, and it only solidifies if you squeeze it to about 25 bar. Quantum effects take over below roughly 20 K, and the two isotopes behave in completely different ways: helium-4 follows Bose–Einstein statistics (nuclear spin 0) and helium-3 follows Fermi–Dirac statistics (spin ½). Ordinary helium is almost all helium-4; only about 0.1 ppm of it is helium-3, which is why helium-3 is rare and expensive and is used to reach millikelvin temperatures.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>⁴He</th><th>³He</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Normal boiling point</td><td>4.22 K</td><td>3.19 K</td></tr><tr><td>Critical temperature</td><td>5.19 K</td><td>3.324 K</td></tr><tr><td>Critical pressure</td><td>2.27 bar</td><td>1.15 bar</td></tr><tr><td>Critical density</td><td>69.6 kg/m³</td><td>41.3 kg/m³</td></tr><tr><td>Superfluid transition</td><td>2.17 K</td><td>2.6 mK</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>The lambda transition</h3><p>Pump on a bath of normal liquid helium (He I) and it cools by evaporation. At <b>T<sub>λ</sub> = 2.176 K</b> (at saturated vapour pressure) the liquid changes into a second liquid, <b>He II</b>. The transition is named after the Greek letter because a plot of specific heat against temperature has the shape of a λ. It is a second-order transition: there is no latent heat, and He I and He II never coexist. Higher pressure lowers the transition temperature, to 1.76 K at 25 bar.</p><ul><li><b>Density:</b> about 145 kg/m³ below T<sub>λ</sub>, against about 125 kg/m³ for saturated He I at 4.2 K. The density peaks a few millikelvin above T<sub>λ</sub> and the thermal expansion coefficient turns negative below it, so isentropic compression can even cool He II.</li><li><b>Specific heat</b> (fits quoted in the lectures, in J/kg·K): C ≈ 117 T<sup>5.6</sup> from 1.1 K to T<sub>λ</sub>, 108 T<sup>6.7</sup> from 0.6 K to 1.1 K and 20.4 T³ below 0.6 K.</li><li><b>Why engineers care:</b> He II is the coolant for large superconducting magnets and accelerator radio-frequency cavities, because it is colder than He I and conducts heat extraordinarily well.</li></ul><h3>Three things nobody expected</h3><ul><li><b>Rollin film.</b> A test tube lowered part-way into a bath of He II slowly fills, because a thin film of liquid climbs the outside wall without friction and spills inwards.</li><li><b>Thermomechanical (fountain) effect.</b> Join two containers by a tube so fine that no ordinary fluid can pass. Heat one container and the liquid level there rises: the pressure difference is ΔP = ρ s ΔT, where s is the entropy per unit mass. This is London's fountain-pressure equation.</li><li><b>Two different viscosities.</b> A torsion pendulum oscillating in He II slows as if the liquid had a viscosity about a tenth that of air, yet the same liquid flows through a very fine capillary with no pressure drop at all. Andronikashvili's stack of closely spaced rotating discs measured the part that does drag, and it falls as the temperature drops.</li></ul><h3>The two-fluid model</h3><p>The puzzle is solved by treating He II as two interpenetrating fluids (a model, not two literal liquids), proposed independently by Tisza and London and by Landau. Einstein had shown that identical integer-spin particles below a certain temperature pile into the lowest-energy state: a Bose–Einstein condensate. That condensed fraction is the <b>superfluid</b>: no viscosity and <b>no entropy</b>. The thermally excited rest, which Landau described as quantised sound waves (phonons) and rotational excitations (rotons), is the <b>normal fluid</b>: it has viscosity and carries all the entropy. Their proportions depend only on temperature, ρ<sub>s</sub>/ρ = 1 − (T/T<sub>λ</sub>)<sup>5.6</sup>, so the liquid is 100% superfluid as T goes to zero. The disc experiment feels only the normal fluid; the capillary lets only the superfluid through. Drag the temperature slider and watch the two components counterflow in a heated channel.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=h2f viewBox='0 0 640 215' role=img aria-label='Counterflow of normal fluid and superfluid in a channel between a heater and a cold bath'></svg><div class=two><svg class=sv id=h2c viewBox='0 0 330 175' role=img aria-label='Superfluid and normal fluid fractions against temperature'></svg><div><label for=h2t><b>Bath temperature = <span id=h2tv>1.80</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=h2t min=300 max=2300 step=10 value=1800><div class=res id=h2o></div></div></div><div class=cap id=h2n></div></div><p>Notice how the speeds work out. With no net mass flow, ρ<sub>n</sub>v<sub>n</sub> = −ρ<sub>s</sub>v<sub>s</sub>, so when the normal fraction is small it must move fast to balance the superfluid, and the heat flux is q = ρ s T v<sub>n</sub>.</p><h3>Sound, but two kinds</h3><p>He II carries two sound waves. <b>First sound</b> is the usual pressure-density wave. <b>Second sound</b> is a wave of temperature and entropy, where the normal and superfluid components oscillate against each other; Landau predicted it and Peshkov measured it in 1948 with a heater and a thermometer in a glass resonator. Engineers use it today: a small oscillating superleak transducer (OST), a foil with sub-micron holes that only superfluid can pass, detects temperature waves and can triangulate the hot spots on a superconducting cavity or the origin of a magnet quench.</p><h3>Heat transfer: the whole reason for He II</h3><p>Bulk heat moves by <b>internal convection</b>: the fluid shows no net motion, yet carries heat, like conduction through a perfect conductor. In the laminar regime the effective conductivity grows with the square of the channel diameter d, which would suggest unlimited conduction from ever wider channels. But above a critical relative velocity the two fluids interact through quantised vortices (mutual friction), which limits the transport, and the Gorter–Mellink law applies: dT/dx = −f(T,p) q<sup>m</sup> with m ≈ 3. For channels wider than about 1 mm and heat fluxes above about 100 W/m² this is the whole story. The ceiling is the <b>peak heat flux</b> q*, reached when the warm end of the channel reaches T<sub>λ</sub> and the superfluid is lost. For a pressurised channel at 1.8 K and 1 bar the lectures give q* L<sup>1/3</sup> = 7.4 W/cm<sup>5/3</sup>.</p><div class=calc><label for=h2l><b>Channel length L = <span id=h2lv>0.001</span> cm</b></label><input type=range id=h2l min=-3 max=2 step=0.05 value=-3><div class=res id=h2p></div><div class=cap id=h2q></div></div><p>Heat from a solid surface into He II meets another barrier, <b>Kapitza conductance</b>: an interface effect from the mismatch of phonon impedance (density times sound speed) between the solid and the liquid, with no convective boundary layer. It produces the largest temperature jump in non-boiling He II heat transfer. Unlike boiling in ordinary liquids, He II has no stable nucleate boiling: beyond q* a vapour film forms, and a lower recovery flux is needed to restore the Kapitza regime.</p>`,
q:"Why can He II move heat far better than copper?",o:["The normal fluid carries entropy away from the heater while the superfluid flows back with no entropy, so heat moves by counterflow","Superfluid helium is a metal","Its boiling point is higher than nitrogen"],a:0,e:"The two-fluid counterflow transports heat without net mass flow and without viscous loss; the limit is the peak heat flux at which the warm end reaches the lambda temperature."},
{t:"Advanced: Mechanical properties and thermal contraction",lead:"Why some metals stay tough in the cold, and why parts shrink by different amounts.",h:`
<h3>Why properties change</h3>
<p>The lecture opens with two examples. Rubber dipped in liquid nitrogen turns hard and shatters like a brittle solid, while Nb–Ti wire loses all electrical resistance in liquid helium. Design therefore needs strength, ductility, thermal and electrical data at the working temperature, not just at room temperature.</p>
<h3>Crystal structure decides ductility</h3>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Lattice</th><th>Slip planes</th><th>Behaviour when cold</th><th>Typical examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Face-centred cubic (FCC)</td><td>Most</td><td>Stays ductile</td><td>Copper, aluminium, nickel, austenitic stainless steel</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hexagonal close-packed (HCP)</td><td>In between</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Titanium, magnesium, zinc</td></tr>
<tr><td>Body-centred cubic (BCC)</td><td>Fewest</td><td>Prone to brittle failure</td><td>Carbon steel, ferritic steels</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class=cap>The slip-plane ranking comes from the lecture. The examples are standard ones added for illustration.</p>
<p>Slip planes are the directions in which layers of atoms slide over one another. Real crystals contain dislocations, and the number of slip planes governs how easily dislocations move, and with it ductility and impact strength.</p>
<h3>Strength rises, toughness can collapse</h3>
<div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>As temperature falls</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Yield strength</td><td>Rises</td><td>Atoms vibrate less, so dislocations are harder to move</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ultimate strength</td><td>Rises</td><td>The same reason</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fatigue strength</td><td>Rises</td><td>A micro-crack needs a larger stress to grow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Impact strength</td><td>Generally falls; some materials show a sharp ductile-to-brittle transition</td><td>Little energy is absorbed before fracture</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Carbon steels undergo this ductile-to-brittle transition at roughly 80 to 100 K, so they cannot be used for cryogenic service. In the lecture's impact data, 304 stainless steel, 9% nickel steel and 2024-T4 aluminium keep their toughness. For parts under cyclic load, such as the flexure bearings made of beryllium–copper, the working stress is kept below the endurance limit.</p>
<div class=calc><label for=mcs><b>Temperature: <span id=mcv>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=mcs min=20 max=300 step=1 value=300><div class=res id=mco></div><svg class=sv id=mcg viewBox='0 0 640 230' style='margin-top:12px'></svg><div class=cap>Illustrative curves built from the trends described in the lecture, not measured data. The shaded band marks the 80 to 100 K range where carbon steels change from tough to brittle.</div></div>
<h3>Thermal contraction</h3>
<p>The linear coefficient of thermal expansion is λ<sub>t</sub> = (δL/L)/δT, and for an isotropic material the volumetric coefficient is β = 3λ<sub>t</sub>. Materials contract on cooling, and the coefficient itself shrinks as temperature falls, so most of the total contraction has already happened by 80 K. For 304 stainless steel the lecture gives 2.91 mm per metre from 300 K to 80 K, and only 0.13 mm per metre from 80 K to 20 K. Copper contracts 3.11 mm per metre between 300 K and 80 K.</p>
<p>Two metals joined together contract by different amounts, which stresses the joint. Try it below.</p>
<div class=fig><canvas id=tcc width=640 height=200></canvas><label for=tcl>Bar length: <b id=tclv>1.0</b> m</label><input type=range id=tcl min=0.2 max=3 step=0.1 value=1><label for=tcp>Cooling from 300 K to 80 K: <b id=tcpv>0</b>%</label><input type=range id=tcp min=0 max=100 step=1 value=0><button id=tcb class=tgl>Cool down</button><div class=res id=tco></div><div class=cap>The shrinkage is drawn 50 times too large so that it is visible. The slider is simple progress, not an exact temperature scale. Dashed outlines show the original lengths.</div></div>
<div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Slip planes: FCC has the most and stays ductile, BCC the fewest and turns brittle</li><li>Strength rises as temperature falls, but impact strength can fall sharply</li><li>Dissimilar metals contract by different amounts, so joints and long runs need allowance</li></ul></div>`,q:"Which crystal structure has the most slip planes and therefore stays most ductile when cold?",o:["Body-centred cubic","Face-centred cubic","Hexagonal close-packed"],a:1,e:"FCC metals such as copper, aluminium and austenitic stainless steel have the most slip planes and keep their ductility at low temperature."},
{t:"Advanced: Specific heat and thermal conduction",lead:"Why solids need so little energy to cool further, and how to calculate heat flow when conductivity changes with temperature.",h:`
<h3>Specific heat of solids</h3>
<p>At room temperature each lattice point of a solid stores the same energy, which gives the Dulong–Petit result c<sub>v</sub> = 3R, independent of temperature. That fails in the cold. Einstein modelled the solid as identical oscillators. Debye did better by counting every standing wave in the solid, which gives the lattice heat capacity per mole:</p>
<p style='text-align:center'><b>c<sub>v</sub> = 9R (T/θ<sub>D</sub>)³ ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>θ<sub>D</sub>/T</sup> x⁴eˣ / (eˣ − 1)² dx</b></p>
<p>Only the Debye temperature θ<sub>D</sub> changes from one material to another. When T is above 2θ<sub>D</sub>, c<sub>v</sub> approaches 3R. Below θ<sub>D</sub>/12 it follows a cubic law, c<sub>v</sub> = (12π⁴/5) R (T/θ<sub>D</sub>)³, so it collapses very quickly as the temperature drops.</p>
<div class=calc><label for=dbs><b>Material</b></label><select id=dbs></select><label for=dbt style='margin-top:10px'><b>Temperature: <span id=dbv>100</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=dbt min=2 max=400 step=1 value=100><div class=res id=dbo></div><svg class=sv id=dbg viewBox='0 0 640 230' style='margin-top:12px'></svg><div class=cap id=dbn></div></div>
<p class=cap>Debye temperatures are from the lecture's table. This is the lattice contribution only; in metals the electrons add a small extra at very low temperature, and C<sub>p</sub> and C<sub>v</sub> are nearly equal for solids at these temperatures. Worked checks from the lecture: copper at 100 K gives c<sub>v</sub>/R of about 1.93, roughly 253 J/kg·K, and aluminium at 25 K gives about 19 J/kg·K.</p>
<h3>Thermal conductivity in solids</h3>
<p>Supports and struts inside a cryostat should have low conductivity to cut heat leak, while a cold link that must carry cooling to a load should have high conductivity, which is why copper is used. In general conductivity falls as temperature falls, but pure metals behave differently from alloys.</p>
<ul><li><b>Pure metals:</b> both electrons and lattice vibrations (phonons) carry heat. Above liquid-nitrogen temperature electrons dominate and conductivity stays roughly constant. Below it phonons take over, conductivity rises (varying about as 1/T²) to a peak, and then falls when the electrons' mean free path becomes as large as the specimen.</li><li><b>Impure metals and alloys:</b> electrons and phonons contribute about equally, and imperfections such as grain boundaries and impurities scatter them, so there is no large peak.</li></ul>
<h3>Conductivity integrals</h3>
<p>Fourier's law is Q = −k(T) A dT/dx, but k changes strongly with temperature. Define the thermal conductivity integral relative to a datum, θ(T) = ∫k dT from T<sub>d</sub> (0 or 4.2 K) to T. For a constant cross-section A and length L, the heat flow is</p>
<p style='text-align:center'><b>Q = (A/L) [θ(T<sub>hot</sub>) − θ(T<sub>cold</sub>)]</b></p>
<p>Only the end temperatures are needed, not the temperature profile. Averaging k at the two ends is tempting but can be wrong when k is not linear in T. This demo uses a made-up power law, k = k₃₀₀ (T/300)ⁿ, to show the size of the mistake.</p>
<div class=calc><label for=kcn><b>Exponent n: <span id=kcnv>2.0</span></b> (n = 1 is linear, n = 0 is constant)</label><input type=range id=kcn min=0 max=2.5 step=0.1 value=2><label for=kch style='margin-top:10px'><b>Hot end: <span id=kchv>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=kch min=100 max=300 step=5 value=300><label for=kcc style='margin-top:10px'><b>Cold end: <span id=kccv>80</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=kcc min=4 max=90 step=1 value=80><div class=res id=kco></div><div class=cap>Illustrative model, not a real material: k at 300 K = 10 W/m·K, area 1 cm², length 0.1 m.</div></div>
<div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Debye: c<sub>v</sub> tends to 3R above 2θ<sub>D</sub> and falls as T³ below θ<sub>D</sub>/12</li><li>Pure metals have a conductivity peak at low temperature; alloys do not</li><li>Use the conductivity integral, not an average of k, when k varies strongly</li></ul></div>`,q:"What happens to the lattice specific heat of a solid when it is cooled well below its Debye temperature?",o:["It stays near 3R","It falls roughly as T³","It rises steadily"],a:1,e:"Below about θD/12 the Debye result is cv = (12π⁴/5) R (T/θD)³, so the specific heat falls very steeply with temperature."},
{t:"Advanced: Superconductivity",lead:"Zero resistance, flux expulsion, critical limits, and the race to higher transition temperatures.",h:`
<h3>Resistance that vanishes</h3>
<p>The electrical resistance of a metal falls as it is cooled, because the scattering of electrons by lattice vibrations dies away, while scattering by impurities and dislocations does not depend on temperature. A few materials go further: their resistance drops suddenly to zero at a critical temperature. Onnes saw this in 1911 in mercury, whose resistance fell to zero at about 4.2 K.</p>
<p>Superconductivity exists only inside a region bounded by three critical values: temperature T<sub>c</sub>, current density J<sub>c</sub> and magnetic field H<sub>c</sub>. Exceed any one and the material turns normal again.</p>
<h3>Why electrons stop scattering: BCS theory</h3>
<p>In the superconducting state electrons attract each other weakly through the lattice (an interaction of about 0.1 eV) and form pairs, called Cooper pairs. The second electron follows the first, so the pair meets much less resistance. This was explained by Bardeen, Cooper and Schrieffer in 1957, and they received the Nobel Prize for it in 1972.</p>
<h3>The Meissner effect</h3>
<div class=fig><canvas id=msc width=640 height=250></canvas><label for=mss>Temperature: <b id=msv>10.0</b> K</label><input type=range id=mss min=2 max=12 step=0.1 value=10><div class=cap>Animation: drag the temperature below 7.2 K. A superconductor expels magnetic flux, which is a separate property from having zero resistance.</div></div>
<p>A normal material lets magnetic field lines pass through. The moment it turns superconducting it pushes the field out, which was first observed in 1933. This is also what lets a magnet levitate above a superconductor: the lecture shows YBCO, cooled below about 90 K, turning diamagnetic and floating a vessel above a magnetic track, and the same principle supports maglev trains with no contact and no friction.</p>
<h3>The critical field</h3>
<p>The threshold field falls as the temperature rises: H<sub>T</sub> = H<sub>0</sub> [1 − (T/T<sub>0</sub>)²], where H<sub>0</sub> is the critical field at zero kelvin and T<sub>0</sub> is the critical temperature. Choose a metal, then set a temperature and applied field.</p>
<div class=calc><label for=cfs><b>Metal</b></label><select id=cfs></select><label for=cft style='margin-top:10px'><b>Temperature: <span id=cftv>3.00</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=cft min=0.05 max=8 step=0.05 value=3><label for=cfh style='margin-top:10px'><b>Applied field: <span id=cfhv>30.0</span> mT</b></label><input type=range id=cfh min=0 max=90 step=0.5 value=30><div class=res id=cfo></div><svg class=sv id=cfg viewBox='0 0 640 230' style='margin-top:12px'></svg><div class=cap>T<sub>c</sub> and H<sub>0</sub> are standard textbook values for these pure metals, not taken from the lecture. The region under the curve is superconducting.</div></div>
<h3>Low-Tc and high-Tc materials</h3>
<p>Early on, materials with transition temperatures above 30 K were called high-Tc. Today the term usually means materials that superconduct at liquid-nitrogen temperature, which is far cheaper to reach than liquid helium. The chart shows how the record rose, with a line at 77 K.</p>
<svg class=sv id=tlc viewBox='0 0 640 300'></svg>
<p class=cap>Approximate transition temperatures and discovery years from standard references; the lecture shows the same trend as a graph.</p>
<h3>Applications</h3>
<ul><li><b>NMR and MRI:</b> superconducting magnets from about 10 T to 25 T cooled by liquid helium; measurement accuracy improves with field strength.</li><li><b>Maglev trains:</b> superconducting magnets on the vehicle and coils on the guideway levitate the train.</li><li><b>Transformers and generators:</b> windings kept cold to remove I²R losses.</li><li><b>Particle accelerators and fusion:</b> the 27 km CERN ring at 1.9 K, and the ITER magnets cooled by helium gas.</li></ul>
<div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Resistance drops to zero below T<sub>c</sub>, and current density and field set further limits</li><li>Cooper pairs explain the zero resistance (BCS theory)</li><li>The Meissner effect, flux expulsion, is what makes levitation possible</li></ul></div>`,q:"What does the Meissner effect describe?",o:["Resistance dropping to zero","A superconductor expelling magnetic flux","Electrons forming pairs"],a:1,e:"When a material becomes superconducting it pushes magnetic field lines out of its interior. This is separate from zero resistance and from Cooper pairing."},
{t:"Advanced: Conduction in solids at low temperature",lead:"Why the conductivity of a solid changes by factors of hundreds between 300 K and 4 K, and how to calculate the heat a support or a link really carries.",h:`<p>Conduction is the heat route you control most directly: you choose the material, the cross-section and the length of every support, wire and thermal link. The one complication is that the thermal conductivity k is not a constant. It changes by large factors between room temperature and a few kelvin, and a calculation that uses a single value is often wrong by tens of per cent, or worse. This lesson is general physics, not taken from the lectures. The conductivity values are rounded typical figures for the standard grades; real values vary by 20 to 30 per cent with composition, heat treatment and, for copper, purity, so use handbook data for a real design.</p><h3>Fourier's law when k depends on temperature</h3><p>Locally, the heat flux is q = −k(T) dT/dx. In steady state the same heat Q flows through every cross-section of a bar of area A, so Q dx = −A k(T) dT. Integrating from the cold end to the hot end gives</p><p><b>Q = (A / L) × ∫ k(T) dT</b>, with the integral taken from T<sub>cold</sub> to T<sub>hot</sub>.</p><p>The integral of k over temperature is called the <b>thermal conductivity integral</b>. The heat load depends only on the two end temperatures and the integral; the shape of the bar does not matter beyond A and L (for a bar of constant area). The mean conductivity is simply the integral divided by the temperature difference.</p><h3>Who carries the heat</h3><ul><li><b>Electrons.</b> In a metal, free electrons carry most of the heat. Their conductivity is tied to the electrical resistivity ρ by the <b>Wiedemann–Franz law</b>: k<sub>e</sub> = L<sub>0</sub> T / ρ, with L<sub>0</sub> = 2.44 × 10⁻⁸ W·Ω/K². A good electrical conductor is a good thermal conductor.</li><li><b>Lattice vibrations (phonons).</b> In insulators, and in poor conductors such as alloys, heat is carried by the vibrations of the lattice. The conductivity is roughly (1/3) × (heat capacity) × (sound speed) × (mean free path).</li></ul><p>These two pictures explain the shapes seen in the chart below. In a <b>pure metal</b> the resistivity drops as it cools, so k rises. Near 10 to 30 K it reaches a <b>peak</b>, and below that the electrons run out of freedom to move: collisions with impurities fix the resistivity at a constant value ρ<sub>0</sub>, and k = L<sub>0</sub>T/ρ<sub>0</sub> falls in proportion to T. In an <b>alloy</b> such as stainless steel the electrons and lattice are always strongly scattered, so k simply falls steadily as the temperature falls. In a <b>glass-epoxy composite such as G-10</b>, k is very low everywhere and falls slowly too.</p><h3>Residual resistance ratio, RRR</h3><p>The purity of a metal is described by the <b>residual resistance ratio</b>, RRR = ρ(295 K) / ρ(4 K), the room-temperature resistivity divided by the leftover resistivity at 4 K. The higher it is, the purer or better annealed the metal. For copper, ρ at room temperature is about 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m, so Wiedemann–Franz gives, in the low-temperature region where only impurity scattering matters, <b>k ≈ 5.7 × RRR W/m·K at 4 K</b>, and it falls in proportion to T below that. Ordinary commercial copper may have an RRR of 50 to 100; very pure annealed copper has several hundred; a poorly annealed or alloyed copper may have less than 20. That is why the same copper bar can be a superb or a mediocre thermal link at 4 K.</p><div class=calc><label for=kqrr><b>RRR of the copper = <span id=kqrrv>100</span></b></label><input type=range id=kqrr min=5 max=1000 step=5 value=100><label for=kqrt><b>Temperature = <span id=kqrtv>4</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=kqrt min=1 max=10 step=0.5 value=4><div class=res id=kqro></div><div class=cap>Valid at low temperature, where impurity scattering dominates and the temperature is below the conductivity peak. The relation k = L<sub>0</sub> T/ρ<sub>0</sub> with ρ<sub>0</sub> = 1.7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m / RRR.</div></div><h3>Typical values</h3><p>Rounded typical thermal conductivities in W/m·K. They are estimates, good to about 20 to 30 per cent.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>4 K</th><th>20 K</th><th>77 K</th><th>300 K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stainless steel 304</td><td>0.3</td><td>1.9</td><td>7.9</td><td>14.9</td></tr><tr><td>G-10 glass-epoxy (across the weave)</td><td>0.08</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.3</td><td>0.6</td></tr><tr><td>Copper, RRR 100</td><td>570</td><td>1500</td><td>500</td><td>400</td></tr><tr><td>Aluminium 6061-T6</td><td>5</td><td>35</td><td>96</td><td>155</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>The temperature profile and the heat flow</h3><p>Because k changes along the bar, the temperature profile is not a straight line. Where k is small (the cold end of a stainless-steel tube), a large temperature drop is needed to push the same heat through, so the profile is steep there; where k is large, it is flat. The animation below shows a bar with the hot end at the left and the cold end at the right. The dots carry the heat Q, the same at every point in a steady state. The graph underneath shows the real profile with a full line and, for comparison, the straight line you would get with a constant conductivity. The right-hand chart shows k(T) for all four materials with the chosen one in bold, and the two end temperatures marked.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=kqf viewBox='0 0 640 300' role=img aria-label='Animated bar with heat flow, the temperature profile along it, and the thermal conductivity curves of four materials'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=kqm><b>Material</b></label><select id=kqm><option value=0>Stainless steel 304</option><option value=1>G-10 glass-epoxy</option><option value=2>Copper, RRR 100</option><option value=3>Aluminium 6061-T6</option></select><label for=kqh><b>Hot end = <span id=kqhv>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=kqh min=20 max=300 step=1 value=300><label for=kqc><b>Cold end = <span id=kqcv>4</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=kqc min=4 max=250 step=1 value=4><label for=kql><b>Length = <span id=kqlv>30</span> cm</b></label><input type=range id=kql min=2 max=100 step=1 value=30><label for=kqa><b>Cross-section area = <span id=kqav>0.5</span> cm²</b></label><input type=range id=kqa min=0.01 max=5 step=0.01 value=0.5></div><div class=res id=kqo></div></div><div class=cap id=kqn></div></div><p>Try a few cases. For stainless steel between 300 K and 4 K, the mean conductivity is about 10 W/m·K, noticeably lower than the 14.9 you read off at room temperature, so using the room-temperature value overestimates the heat by about 50 per cent. For pure copper the opposite happens: the mean conductivity between 300 K and 4 K is higher than the room-temperature value, because k rises on cooling, so the room-temperature value underestimates the heat.</p><h3>Why intercepting heat at 77 K pays</h3><p>Look at where the heat is conducted. For a stainless-steel support from 300 K to 4 K, about 90 per cent of the conductivity integral lies in the part of the bar above 77 K. This means almost all of the heat flowing down the support comes from the warm half of its length, not the cold half. If a cooled intermediate stage (a liquid-nitrogen shield or a first cryocooler stage) is connected to the support at 77 K, nearly all of that heat goes into the stage, where removing a watt costs far less than at 4 K, and only about a tenth of it reaches the 4 K stage. This is called <b>heat interception</b> or thermal anchoring. It is the single most important trick in the design of cold supports, wires and pipes, and later lessons use it again.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>k depends on temperature, so use Q = (A/L) ∫k dT, not a single value</li><li>Alloys and composites (stainless steel, G-10) are the good insulating supports; pure metals (copper) are the good thermal links</li><li>Pure metals peak in conductivity near 10 to 30 K; RRR tells you how pure and how good</li><li>At 4 K, copper conducts about 5.7 × RRR W/m·K</li><li>Intercept heat at warmer stages: most of the conducted heat comes from the warm part of a support</li></ul></div>`,
q:"Why is it wrong to estimate the heat conducted along a stainless-steel tube from 300 K to 4 K using the room-temperature conductivity?",o:["The conductivity falls as the tube cools, so the room-temperature value overestimates the heat","Stainless steel does not conduct heat at low temperature","Fourier's law does not apply below 77 K"],a:0,e:"k changes with temperature, so the heat must be calculated from the integral of k over the temperature range. For stainless steel the mean conductivity between 300 K and 4 K is only about two-thirds of the room-temperature value."},
{t:"Advanced: Thermal resistance, joints and thermal links",lead:"How to treat a heat path as a chain of resistances, why a bolted joint can cost more temperature than a metre of copper, and how to build a good cold link.",h:`<p>A real cold stage is joined to its load by a chain of parts: a sample holder, a clamp, a copper strap, another clamp, a cold plate. The heat has to cross each one in turn. This lesson is general engineering, not taken from the lectures. The contact figures are <b>illustrative</b>: real joint conductance varies by orders of magnitude with surface finish, force, plating and temperature, so measure the joints that matter.</p><h3>Thermal resistance</h3><p>Fourier's law can be written like Ohm's law. For a bar of constant conductivity the <b>thermal resistance</b> is R = L / (k A), in kelvin per watt, and Q = ΔT / R. The temperature difference plays the role of voltage, the heat flow that of current. Resistances in <b>series</b> (the heat passes through each in turn) add: R = R₁ + R₂ + …. Resistances in <b>parallel</b> (the heat has several paths) combine as 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + …, so the easiest path carries most of the heat. When k varies with temperature, the resistance depends on the temperatures, and you use the conductivity integral from the previous lesson for each part.</p><h3>Joints: where the heat has to squeeze through</h3><p>Two solid surfaces pressed together do not touch over their whole area. At the microscopic scale, each surface is rough, and contact occurs only at the tips of the high points, the <b>asperities</b>. The heat must squeeze through these small contact spots, so the joint has a resistance of its own, an extra temperature drop at the interface. Pressing harder flattens the asperities and enlarges the spots, so the conductance of the joint rises with the force. Adding a soft layer that fills the gaps (a thin foil of indium, or a thin film of vacuum grease) makes the contact far better at a given force. Gold plating prevents oxide layers on copper, which otherwise act as barriers. In a metal joint at low temperature, the heat is carried by the electrons, so the conductance of the joint falls roughly in proportion to the temperature, which is why joints become the weak point at the coldest stage.</p><p>The model in the animation uses these trends in an illustrative form: the conductance of a joint is G = G<sub>0</sub> × (F / 100 N)<sup>0.8</sup> × (T / 4 K) × (surface factor), with G<sub>0</sub> = 0.05 W/K for a dry bare joint at 100 N and 4 K. The surface factor is 1 for a dry joint, 5 for a greased joint and 20 for an indium foil joint. These numbers show the trends; they are not measurements.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=nwf viewBox='0 0 640 320' role=img aria-label='A chain of sample, joint, copper strap, joint and cold plate with heat flowing through, the temperature at each node, and a close-up of the contact spots in a joint'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=nwq><b>Heat load from the sample = <span id=nwqv>10</span> mW</b></label><input type=range id=nwq min=-1 max=3 step=0.05 value=1><label for=nwp><b>Cold plate temperature</b></label><select id=nwp><option value=4.2>4.2 K (liquid helium)</option><option value=20>20 K</option><option value=77>77 K (liquid nitrogen)</option></select><label for=nwf2><b>Clamp force on each joint = <span id=nwfv>100</span> N</b></label><input type=range id=nwf2 min=10 max=1000 step=10 value=100><label for=nwi><b>Joint surface</b></label><select id=nwi><option value=1>Dry, bare</option><option value=5>Greased</option><option value=20>Indium foil</option></select><label for=nwl><b>Copper strap length = <span id=nwlv>10</span> cm</b></label><input type=range id=nwl min=2 max=50 step=1 value=10><label for=nwa><b>Strap cross-section = <span id=nwav>0.5</span> cm²</b></label><input type=range id=nwa min=0.05 max=3 step=0.05 value=0.5></div><div class=res id=nwo></div></div><div class=cap id=nwn></div></div><p>Experiment. At 4 K with a dry joint and a light force, the two joints take most of the temperature rise, far more than the copper strap. Add grease or indium, or raise the force, and the joints shrink; then the strap dominates, and making it shorter or thicker helps. Raise the plate to 77 K: the joint conductance is larger there, so everything is easier. The lesson is to spend your effort on the weakest link of the chain, and it is very often a joint.</p><h3>Good habits for cold links</h3><ul><li>Use high-purity, annealed copper (high RRR) for the strap, with good gold-plated, flat contact faces.</li><li>Use a bolt, a spring or a Belleville washer to keep the force steady as the parts contract in the cold.</li><li>Use indium foil or a thin film of grease for joints that must carry the most heat.</li><li>Fewer joints are better than many good joints; each one adds resistance.</li><li>Do not forget that the thermometer sits at the end of its own thermal path: its reading tells you the temperature at the sensor, not of the sample.</li></ul><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Series resistances add; parallel paths combine like conductances</li><li>A joint has its own resistance, because heat passes through small contact spots</li><li>Joint conductance rises with force, with a soft interface layer, and with temperature</li><li>At 4 K the joints, not the copper, usually limit a cold link</li></ul></div>`,
q:"Two copper blocks are bolted together at 4 K and the joint is the weakest part of the thermal link. Which change helps most?",o:["Increase the clamp force and add indium foil or grease between the faces","Make the copper blocks heavier","Paint the faces to protect them"],a:0,e:"Heat crosses a joint only at the contact spots. More force, or a soft layer that fills the gaps, enlarges the real contact area and lowers the joint resistance."},
{t:"Advanced: Transient conduction and cooldown",lead:"How a solid cools down in time: the Biot number, the Fourier number, and when you can treat a whole object as one lump.",h:`<p>Steady state tells you what a cryostat does after days of running. But much of an experiment is spent in the transient: the hours when a magnet, a sample holder or a heavy copper plate is cooling down. This lesson is general physics, not taken from the lectures. The model uses constant material properties at their room-temperature values, so it gives a first estimate only: the heat capacity of a solid falls steeply as it gets colder, so the last tens of kelvin go much faster than this model shows.</p><h3>The equation that governs it</h3><p>Heat flows in, heat is stored, and the temperature changes. For one dimension, the heat equation is</p><p><b>∂T/∂t = α ∂²T/∂x²</b>, with <b>α = k / (ρ c)</b></p><p>The <b>thermal diffusivity</b> α, in m²/s, measures how fast a temperature change spreads through a material: a large k carries the heat quickly, a large heat capacity ρc slows it down. Diffusion takes a time of about d²/α to cross a thickness d. Copper has α about 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ m²/s, so a 1 cm thick plate equilibrates in about a second; stainless steel (α about 4 × 10⁻⁶ m²/s) takes about 25 seconds; G-10 (about 3 × 10⁻⁷ m²/s) takes about 5 minutes.</p><h3>Two numbers that tell you what will happen</h3><ul><li>The <b>Biot number</b>, Bi = h d / k, compares the resistance to heat flow inside the solid (d/k) with the resistance at its surface (1/h). Here h is the surface heat transfer coefficient and d the thickness (for a plate cooled on one face and insulated on the other, the full thickness).</li><li>The <b>Fourier number</b>, Fo = α t / d², is the time measured in units of the diffusion time.</li></ul><p>If Bi is small (below about 0.1), the inside of the solid stays almost uniform while it cools, because the heat passes easily through the solid and struggles to get across the surface. Then you can treat the whole object as <b>one lump</b>, and its temperature simply decays exponentially, T − T<sub>bath</sub> = (T<sub>0</sub> − T<sub>bath</sub>) exp(−t/τ), with τ = ρ c d / h for a plate of thickness d cooled on one side. If Bi is large, the surface is quickly at the bath temperature and the inside lags behind; the cooling is controlled by diffusion, with time scale about 4d²/(π²α) for a plate cooled on one face.</p><h3>See it happen</h3><p>The animation cools a plate that starts at 300 K, with its left face in contact with a 77 K bath and its right face insulated. The coloured bar shows the temperature through the thickness; the left graph shows the profiles at different times; the right graph shows the left (surface) and right (far face) temperatures against time, together with the lumped estimate as a dashed line. The solver is a standard implicit finite-difference scheme with 40 cells.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=trf viewBox='0 0 640 310' role=img aria-label='Animated cooling of a slab: temperature through the thickness, profiles at several times, and surface and far-face temperatures against time with the lumped estimate'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=trm><b>Material</b></label><select id=trm><option value=0>Copper</option><option value=1>Aluminium</option><option value=2>Stainless steel</option><option value=3>G-10 glass-epoxy</option></select><label for=trd><b>Thickness = <span id=trdv>10</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=trd min=0 max=2.5 step=0.05 value=1><label for=trh><b>Surface heat transfer coefficient h = <span id=trhv>150</span> W/m²·K</b></label><input type=range id=trh min=1 max=4 step=0.05 value=2.18><button class=btn id=trp>Replay</button></div><div class=res id=tro></div></div><div class=cap id=trn></div></div><p>Try copper at h = 150 W/m²·K, which is a typical order of magnitude for film boiling in liquid nitrogen (a later lesson covers boiling): the Biot number is tiny, the plate cools as one lump, and the dashed line follows the solid curves. Now switch to stainless steel or G-10: the Biot number rises, and the far face lags far behind the surface. Raise h to a few thousand, as in good nucleate boiling, and even copper starts to show gradients. The practical lessons:</p><ul><li>For a metal plate in a poor-heat-transfer environment, the surface limits the cooldown. Spend your effort on better contact with the coolant, not on the thickness.</li><li>For a poor conductor (G-10, stainless steel, a thick epoxy layer), the inside lags, and cooling too fast creates large temperature differences and <b>thermal stress</b>. This is why parts of different material and mass are cooled slowly.</li><li>A part's cooldown time scales with its heat content: a heavy copper block takes longer to cool than a thin one by the ratio of masses, not because copper conducts slowly, but because there is a lot of heat to take out.</li></ul><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Diffusivity α = k/ρc sets how fast temperature changes spread; time to cross d is about d²/α</li><li>Biot number Bi = h d / k: below about 0.1, treat the object as one lump</li><li>Lumped time constant τ = ρ c d / h; diffusion-limited time about 4d²/(π²α)</li><li>Constant-property estimates are rough: heat capacity falls steeply on cooling</li></ul></div>`,
q:"A thick stainless-steel plate is cooled from one face and the Biot number is large. What do you expect?",o:["The cooled face reaches the bath temperature quickly, but the far face lags and gradients and thermal stresses are large","The whole plate cools uniformly as a single lump","The plate does not cool at all"],a:0,e:"With Bi large, the resistance inside the solid exceeds that at the surface, so the surface follows the bath while the inside lags, and the lumped model fails."},
{t:"Advanced: Natural convection and stable stratification",lead:"When a fluid heated from below starts to churn, how much extra heat that carries, and why warm gas on top of cold gas keeps a cryostat neck quiet.",h:`<p>In natural convection nobody pumps the fluid: warm fluid is lighter and rises, cold fluid is heavier and sinks, and the buoyancy drives a circulation. Whether it starts, and how much heat it moves, is decided by one number. This lesson is general fluid and heat transfer physics, not taken from the lectures; the correlation is the standard one for a horizontal layer and is accurate only to about 10 to 20 per cent. (The earlier lesson on convection and boiling gives the vertical-plate case.)</p><h3>The Rayleigh number</h3><p>Consider a horizontal layer of fluid of height H between a warm plate below and a cold plate above. Buoyancy tries to start a circulation; viscosity and heat diffusion try to stop it. The ratio is the <b>Rayleigh number</b>:</p><p><b>Ra = g β ΔT H³ / (ν α)</b></p><p>Here g is the acceleration of gravity, β the thermal expansion coefficient of the fluid (the fractional change of density per kelvin), ΔT the temperature difference across the layer, ν the kinematic viscosity and α the thermal diffusivity. Below a critical value, about <b>Ra = 1708</b> between two rigid plates, the fluid stays still and the heat just conducts across. Above it, the layer breaks into rolls of rising and falling fluid, and the heat flow rises above the conduction value.</p><h3>The Nusselt number</h3><p>The <b>Nusselt number</b> Nu is the actual heat flux divided by what conduction alone would carry, k ΔT / H. So Nu = 1 means no convection at all, and Nu = 10 means convection carries ten times as much heat as conduction. The effective heat transfer coefficient is h = Nu × k / H. A widely used correlation for a horizontal layer heated from below (Hollands) is</p><p><b>Nu = 1 + 1.44 [1 − 1708/Ra]⁺ + [(Ra/5830)^(1/3) − 1]⁺</b></p><p>where [x]⁺ means x if positive and zero otherwise. For large Ra the last term dominates and Nu grows as Ra^(1/3), so that, in the turbulent regime, the heat flux does not depend on the layer height. Watch the rolls form in the animation, and use the controls to choose the fluid, the height and the temperature difference.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=ncf viewBox='0 0 640 300' role=img aria-label='Animated convection rolls in a fluid layer between a warm plate below and a cold plate above, with a chart of Nusselt number against Rayleigh number'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=ncl><b>Fluid</b></label><select id=ncl><option value=0>Liquid nitrogen at 77 K</option><option value=1>Air at 300 K</option><option value=2>Water at 300 K</option></select><label for=nch><b>Layer height = <span id=nchv>2</span> cm</b></label><input type=range id=nch min=-1 max=2 step=0.02 value=0.3><label for=ncd><b>Temperature difference = <span id=ncdv>5</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=ncd min=-1 max=2 step=0.02 value=0.7><label for=nco><b>Heated from</b></label><select id=nco><option value=0>Below (unstable)</option><option value=1>Above (stable)</option></select></div><div class=res id=nco2></div></div><div class=cap id=ncn></div></div><p>Try the settings. In liquid nitrogen, a 2 cm layer with only 1 K across it is already well above the critical value, while a layer of a millimetre needs only about half a kelvin. Air at room temperature in a 2 mm gap does not convect at all, for any temperature difference you will meet in a cryostat. Then switch the heating to <b>above</b>: whatever the numbers, the layer is stable, and the heat moves by conduction alone.</p><h3>Stable stratification: why necks are quiet</h3><p>If the warm fluid sits above the cold fluid, the density decreases upward and nothing wants to move. This <b>stable stratification</b> is the normal state of a gas column in the neck of a dewar: warm at the top, cold at the bottom. The gas conducts and the neck wall conducts, but the gas does not overturn. It is one reason helium storage dewars are built with long, narrow necks. The same idea acts in a liquid tank: heat entering through the side wall warms the liquid next to it, the warm liquid rises along the wall and forms a warm layer at the top, and the bulk below stays cold. The surface layer is warmer than the bulk, so the vapour pressure at the surface, and hence the tank pressure, can rise faster than the boil-off of the whole liquid would suggest. (The next lessons look at thermoacoustic oscillations, which are the one thing that can set a stable gas column in motion.)</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Ra = gβΔT H³/(να) decides whether buoyancy starts a flow; the layer between rigid plates is stable below Ra ≈ 1708</li><li>Nu is the heat flow relative to pure conduction; h = Nu k / H</li><li>In turbulent natural convection Nu grows as Ra^(1/3) and the heat flux no longer depends on the layer height</li><li>Warm fluid above cold fluid is stable: gas in a dewar neck conducts but does not overturn</li></ul></div>`,
q:"A layer of cold gas lies under warm gas at the top of a dewar neck. What do you expect?",o:["It is stable: heat moves by conduction and the gas does not overturn","Strong natural convection transports heat down the neck","The gas rises and leaves through the vent at once"],a:0,e:"Warm gas is lighter, so warm over cold is a stable arrangement. Natural convection needs the warmer fluid to be below the colder fluid."},
{t:"Advanced: Convection and boiling heat transfer",lead:"Dimensionless numbers, the boiling curve and the peak heat flux that decides whether your magnet stays cold.",h:`<h3>Three modes, with a convective twist</h3><p>Heat moves by <b>conduction</b> (Fourier, Q = −kA dT/dx, with the conductivity integral doing the work in cryogenics), <b>convection</b> (Newton, Q = hA ΔT) and <b>radiation</b> (Stefan–Boltzmann). The convection coefficient h is not a property: it depends on the fluid, the speed and the geometry, so engineers correlate it with dimensionless numbers.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Number</th><th>Formula</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reynolds Re</td><td>ρVd/μ</td><td>inertia over viscous force</td></tr><tr><td>Prandtl Pr</td><td>μC<sub>p</sub>/k</td><td>viscous over thermal diffusivity</td></tr><tr><td>Nusselt Nu</td><td>hd/k</td><td>convective over conductive exchange</td></tr><tr><td>Grashof Gr</td><td>βgΔTd³ρ²/μ²</td><td>buoyancy over viscous force</td></tr><tr><td>Rayleigh Ra</td><td>Gr·Pr</td><td>strength of free convection</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Forced convection in a tube</h3><p>For laminar flow (Re below about 2000) the Nusselt number is constant: 3.66 for a constant wall temperature and 4.36 for a constant wall heat flux. For turbulent flow (Re above 10⁴) the <b>Dittus–Boelter</b> correlation, good to about ±15%, is Nu = 0.023 Re<sup>0.8</sup> Pr<sup>n</sup>, with n = 0.4 when the fluid is being heated and 0.3 when cooled, properties evaluated at the film temperature, valid for 0.6 &lt; Pr &lt; 160 and L/D &gt; 10. Sieder–Tate adds a viscosity ratio for strongly varying properties, and Johannes (1972) gives a helium-specific version (within ±8.3%) with a temperature-ratio factor, because helium's properties change a lot between wall and bulk.</p><div class=calc><label for=cbf1><b>Fluid</b></label><select id=cbf1><option value=0 selected>Liquid nitrogen at 77 K</option><option value=1>Helium gas at 20 K</option></select><label for=cbre><b>Reynolds number = <span id=cbrev>100000</span></b></label><input type=range id=cbre min=4 max=6 step=0.05 value=5><label for=cbd><b>Tube diameter = <span id=cbdv>20</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=cbd min=5 max=100 step=1 value=20><div class=res id=cbo1></div><div class=cap>Properties are typical handbook values (liquid nitrogen k = 0.145 W/m·K, Pr = 2.3; helium gas at 20 K k = 0.0098 W/m·K, Pr = 0.68), not values from the lecture. The fluid is taken as being heated (n = 0.4).</div></div><h3>Free convection</h3><p>When there is no forced flow, buoyancy drives the motion near a warm wall. The key number is the Rayleigh number, Ra = gβΔT L³ / (ν D<sub>th</sub>), where D<sub>th</sub> is the thermal diffusivity. For a vertical plate: at very low Ra the result is pure conduction (Nu about 1); for Ra below about 10⁹ the flow is laminar, Nu ≈ 0.59 Ra<sup>1/4</sup>; above that it is turbulent, Nu ≈ 0.1 Ra<sup>1/3</sup>. (The slide's exponents were partly illegible; these are the standard values.)</p><h3>Pool boiling</h3><p>An object immersed in a large pool of cryogen goes through the same stages as a pot on a stove: convective cooling, <b>nucleate boiling</b>, a peak, and <b>film boiling</b>. Nucleate boiling needs a layer of superheated liquid next to the wall (thickness δ = kΔT/q) and surface imperfections as nucleation sites, which set the size of the smallest bubble that can grow (a balance between surface tension and superheat: the larger the superheat, the smaller the critical bubble). It is the principal mechanism for static liquid below the critical heat flux q*; for liquid helium the heat flux rises with the superheat to the power 2.5, so h is not constant. Theory (Kutateladze) predicts the measured curves for nitrogen and helium well. At the critical heat flux the vapour stream becomes unstable and a vapour film covers the surface; because the vapour conducts so poorly the coefficient drops sharply, and the surface temperature jumps.</p><p>The critical (peak) heat flux comes from a balance between surface energy and kinetic energy of the liquid and vapour streams: the Zuber correlation, with the empirical constant 0.16, is</p><p><b>q* ≈ 0.16 h<sub>fg</sub> ρ<sub>v</sub><sup>1/2</sup> [σ g (ρ<sub>l</sub> − ρ<sub>v</sub>)]<sup>1/4</sup></b></p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=cbf viewBox='0 0 640 250' role=img aria-label='Schematic pool boiling curve of heat flux against wall superheat with the operating point'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=cbr><b>Cryogen</b></label><select id=cbr><option value=0>Liquid helium (4.2 K)</option><option value=1>Liquid hydrogen</option><option value=2>Liquid neon</option><option value=3 selected>Liquid nitrogen</option><option value=4>Liquid argon</option><option value=5>Liquid oxygen</option></select><label for=cbp><b>Heater power = <span id=cbpv>10</span> W</b></label><input type=range id=cbp min=-1 max=3.5 step=0.02 value=1><label for=cba><b>Surface area = <span id=cbav>10</span> cm²</b></label><input type=range id=cba min=1 max=1000 step=1 value=10></div><div class=res id=cbo></div></div><div class=cap id=cbn></div></div><p>Peak flux is the safety limit for any boiling-cooled magnet or sensor: exceed it and the temperature of the surface can run away, even if the flux is small compared with what the wall could carry in the nucleate regime. In film boiling the Breen–Westwater correlation describes the (low) heat transfer coefficient, which is why recovery needs a flux well below the peak (hysteresis). The curve in the figure is a dimensionless schematic: nucleate q ∝ ΔT<sup>2.5</sup>, the peak, a transition, and a film regime with a smaller coefficient. The peak heat fluxes it uses come from the Zuber formula and standard property values for each fluid (hydrogen 446 kJ/kg, neon 86, argon 162, oxygen 213, nitrogen 199, helium 20.9; surface tensions from handbook values), so they are estimates for flat plates facing upward, not design data.</p><h3>Flow boiling in channels</h3><p>In a heated tube carrying both liquid and vapour the heat transfer depends on mass flow, orientation, flow regime, quality and void fraction, and is the sum of a convective part and a boiling part. For horizontal flow at moderate Reynolds number the enhanced Nusselt number is correlated as Nu<sub>2φ</sub>/Nu<sub>L</sub> = A χ<sub>tt</sub><sup>−m</sup>, with A about 5.4 and m about 0.385 (de la Harpe), where χ<sub>tt</sub> is the Lockhart–Martinelli parameter of the next lesson on two-phase flow. In a vertical channel the peak heat flux depends on the channel width w and length z: it scales with w for narrow channels, with z<sup>−1/2</sup> when w/z is below about 0.1, and becomes independent of the channel at large w/z, where it approaches pool-boiling behaviour.</p>`,
q:"A boiling-cooled sensor is run at a heat flux above the critical heat flux. What happens?",o:["A vapour film forms, the heat transfer coefficient drops sharply and the surface temperature jumps","Nothing; nucleate boiling just gets stronger","The liquid becomes superfluid"],a:0,e:"Beyond q* the vapour film insulates the surface (film boiling). Recovery needs the flux to fall well below the peak, because of hysteresis."},
{t:"Advanced: Boiling in motion: bubbles, burnout and hysteresis",lead:"Watch what happens to a heated surface in liquid nitrogen as the heat flux rises past the critical value, and why the way back is not the way up.",h:`<p>A magnet, a sensor or a heater immersed in a bath of cryogen is cooled by boiling. This is a superb way to take away heat, until it suddenly is not. The earlier lesson on convection and boiling gave the correlations; here you can drive a heated surface up and down the boiling curve and see the consequences. This lesson is general heat transfer physics, not taken from the lectures. The curve below is an <b>illustrative model</b> built from the usual relations, with rounded round-number parameters, not measured data for any particular surface.</p><h3>Three ways a pool boils</h3><ul><li><b>Convection and nucleate boiling.</b> At small temperature differences, the surface warms the liquid next to it, which rises. When the surface is a few kelvin warmer than the liquid, bubbles form at tiny pits in the surface, rise, and are replaced by liquid. This is very efficient: the heat flux rises steeply with the wall superheat ΔT, roughly as ΔT<sup>2.5</sup> in this model, and each bubble carries away latent heat.</li><li><b>The critical heat flux.</b> As the flux rises, bubbles crowd together until the vapour leaving the surface blocks the liquid from returning. The surface can no longer be wetted. The peak value is estimated by the Zuber formula, q* = 0.16 h<sub>fg</sub> ρ<sub>v</sub><sup>1/2</sup> [σ g (ρ<sub>l</sub> − ρ<sub>v</sub>)]<sup>1/4</sup>. For liquid nitrogen at atmospheric pressure it gives about 20 W/cm² (197 kW/m²), using the empirical constant 0.16.</li><li><b>Film boiling.</b> Beyond the peak a continuous vapour film covers the surface. Vapour conducts heat poorly, so the heat transfer coefficient falls to a small value (taken as 150 W/m²·K here, an illustrative figure). To carry the same flux the surface must run <b>far</b> hotter.</li></ul><h3>Burnout, and why you cannot just turn the power back</h3><p>When the <b>heat flux is imposed</b> (an electrical heater, a magnet quenching, a sensor dissipating power), the surface cannot choose: the flux is fixed by the source. If it is raised past the critical value, the surface has no nucleate-boiling state that can carry the heat, so it flips to film boiling and its temperature jumps by hundreds of kelvin: <b>burnout</b>. Now lower the flux again. The film is stable, and it stays until the flux falls well below the peak, to the <b>minimum film-boiling flux</b> (the Leidenfrost point, estimated with a similar formula with a constant of 0.09, giving about 0.8 W/cm² for liquid nitrogen). Only then does the liquid re-wet the surface and the temperature crashes back. The path up is not the path down: this is <b>hysteresis</b>.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=bof viewBox='0 0 640 330' role=img aria-label='Animated pool boiling on a heated surface with bubbles, and a boiling curve of heat flux against wall superheat showing the jump to film boiling and the hysteresis on the way back'></svg><div><button class=btn id=bog>Run an up and down ramp</button></div><label for=boq><b>Heat flux = <span id=boqv>1</span> W/cm²</b></label><input type=range id=boq min=-1 max=1.7 step=0.01 value=0><div class=res id=boo></div><div class=cap id=bon></div></div><p>Press the button and watch the ramp. As the flux rises, the dot moves up the nucleate branch, with more and more bubbles. At about 20 W/cm² the dot jumps to the right, onto the film branch, and the wall temperature leaps. On the way back the dot stays on the film branch right down to about 0.8 W/cm², then falls back to nucleate boiling. You can also drag the slider yourself: it remembers where you came from.</p><h3>What to do about it</h3><ul><li>Keep the design flux well below the critical heat flux. A safe margin of a factor of two or more is typical, because the real peak depends on the surface, its orientation and the depth of liquid.</li><li>A magnet or sensor that is well cooled in the nucleate regime may be quite uncooled in film boiling, even at the <b>same</b> power. Never assume that because it worked a minute ago, it still works.</li><li>For heat flux above the pool-boiling limit, use forced flow, which raises the critical flux, or a different cooling method.</li></ul><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Nucleate boiling is very efficient, with flux rising roughly as ΔT<sup>2.5</sup></li><li>The critical heat flux (about 20 W/cm² for liquid nitrogen by the Zuber estimate) ends it</li><li>With an imposed flux, passing the peak causes a jump to film boiling and a large temperature rise: burnout</li><li>Recovery needs the flux to fall to the much lower minimum film-boiling flux: hysteresis</li></ul></div>`,
q:"A surface in liquid nitrogen was boiling nucleately, the heat flux is raised above the critical value, and then reduced to just below the critical value. What state is it in?",o:["Still in film boiling, hot, because recovery needs a much lower flux","Back in nucleate boiling, as soon as the flux falls below the peak","Frozen to the liquid"],a:0,e:"Film boiling is stable down to the minimum film-boiling flux, which is far below the peak. That gap between the up and down paths is the hysteresis."},
{t:"Advanced: Gas conduction and thermal radiation",lead:"Why a good vacuum is enough, why radiation depends on the warm wall, and how floating shields cut it by n + 1.",h:`<h3>Conduction through a gas</h3><p>A gas conducts heat in one of two regimes, set by how the molecules' mean free path λ compares with the gap L between the surfaces. The mean free path in SI units (M in kg/mol) is λ = 3.62 (μ/P) √(T/M), where μ is the viscosity, P the pressure and T the mean temperature.</p><ul><li><b>Viscous (continuum) regime, λ ≪ L.</b> Molecules collide with each other many times crossing the gap. The gas obeys Fourier's law with a conductivity k(T) that does not depend on pressure.</li><li><b>Molecular (Knudsen) regime, λ ≫ L.</b> Molecules fly wall to wall. Heat is carried by <i>individual</i> molecules in <b>Kennard's law</b>: Q/A = α (γ+1)/(γ−1) √(R/(8πMT)) P (T<sub>w</sub> − T<sub>c</sub>), with α the accommodation coefficient (between 0 and 1), γ = C<sub>p</sub>/C<sub>v</sub> and T the mean temperature. The heat flow is <i>linear in pressure</i>, which is why a better vacuum helps, and why thermal-conductivity (Pirani) gauges can read pressure.</li></ul><div class=calc><label for=grg><b>Gas</b></label><select id=grg><option value=0 selected>Nitrogen</option><option value=1>Helium</option></select><label for=grw><b>Warm wall = <span id=grwv>290</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=grw min=20 max=300 step=1 value=290><label for=grc><b>Cold wall = <span id=grcv>80</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=grc min=2 max=290 step=1 value=80><label for=grl><b>Gap = <span id=grlv>20</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=grl min=1 max=100 step=1 value=20><label for=grq><b>Pressure = <span id=grqv>1e-4</span> torr</b></label><input type=range id=grq min=-8 max=3 step=0.1 value=-4><label for=gra><b>Accommodation α = <span id=grav>1.00</span></b></label><input type=range id=gra min=0.1 max=1 step=0.05 value=1><div class=res id=gro1></div><div class=cap id=grn1></div></div><p>Viscosity and conductivity here use simple power-law fits (nitrogen μ = 1.78×10⁻⁵ (T/300)^0.7 Pa·s, k = 0.026 (T/300)^0.8 W/m·K; helium μ = 1.99×10⁻⁵ (T/300)^0.68, k = 0.152 (T/300)^0.7), which are handbook-style approximations and not from the lecture. The regimes are blended as two resistances in series; this is an engineering approximation, not a rigorous transition model. With nitrogen between 290 K and 80 K at 10⁻⁴ torr the Kennard heat flux is about 4 W/m², comparable with a layer of poor insulation, and at 10⁻⁶ torr it falls a hundredfold.</p><h3>Thermal radiation</h3><p>Every surface above absolute zero emits electromagnetic radiation. A black body at temperature T radiates σT⁴ (σ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ W/m²K⁴), peaking at a wavelength λ<sub>max</sub> = 2898/T µm (Wien): about 10 µm for a 300 K wall, 36 µm at 80 K, 0.5 µm for the Sun. Real surfaces emit a fraction ε &lt; 1 (the emissivity); an opaque surface reflects what it does not absorb (ρ + α = 1). Between two surfaces the exchange is Q = σ A₁ ε₁₂ (T₁⁴ − T₂⁴), where the effective emissivity ε₁₂ for closely spaced parallel plates is 1/(1/ε₁ + 1/ε₂ − 1), and for a small body inside a large enclosure it is set essentially by the small body's emissivity.</p><p>Two things follow for cryogenics. First, because of the fourth power the heat load is a strong function of the <i>warm</i> temperature and almost independent of the cold one: with black surfaces, 290 K to 80 K gives about 399 W/m², and 290 K to 4.2 K about 401 W/m², while 80 K to 4.2 K gives only 2.3 W/m². That is why thermal intercepts at 80 K are so valuable. Second, you can intercept radiation with <b>floating thermal screens</b>: n screens with no contact between them reduce the radiative flux to σε/(n+1) × (T<sub>w</sub>⁴ − T<sub>c</sub>⁴)/(2 − ε), and each screen settles at a temperature given by T<sub>i</sub>⁴ = T<sub>c</sub>⁴ + (i/(n+1))(T<sub>w</sub>⁴ − T<sub>c</sub>⁴).</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=grf viewBox='0 0 640 215' role=img aria-label='Warm and cold plates with floating radiation shields and the radiative heat flux through them'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=grt><b>Warm wall = <span id=grtv>290</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=grt min=20 max=300 step=1 value=290><label for=grk><b>Cold wall = <span id=grkv>4.2</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=grk min=2 max=100 step=0.1 value=4.2><label for=gre><b>Emissivity ε = <span id=grev>0.05</span></b></label><input type=range id=gre min=0.01 max=1 step=0.01 value=0.05><label for=grs><b>Floating shields n = <span id=grsv>0</span></b></label><input type=range id=grs min=0 max=10 step=1 value=0></div><div class=res id=gro></div></div><div class=cap id=grn></div></div><h3>Multi-layer insulation</h3><p>MLI stacks many low-emittance reflectors (polyester film with a 300 to 400 Å aluminium coating) separated by insulating spacers (paper, silk, polyester net). It behaves like a stack of floating shields: n shields cut the radiation by about n + 1. Heat flow <i>along</i> the layers is about 1000 times larger than through them, so the blanket must be well overlapped and not compressed. An insulating spacer is needed because any metal contact short-circuits a shield.</p><p>For rough estimates the lecture compares the total heat flux of several insulations:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Insulation</th><th>300 K to 77 K (W/m²)</th><th>77 K to 20 K (W/m²)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Polystyrene foam (2 lb/ft³)</td><td>48.3</td><td>5.6</td></tr><tr><td>Gas-filled perlite powder (He)</td><td>184.3</td><td>21.8</td></tr><tr><td>Perlite powder in vacuum</td><td>1.6</td><td>0.07</td></tr><tr><td>High vacuum (10⁻⁶ torr, ε = 0.02)</td><td>9</td><td>0.04</td></tr><tr><td>Opacified powder (Cu flakes in Santocel)</td><td>0.3</td><td>not given</td></tr><tr><td>MLI</td><td>0.03</td><td>0.007</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are ideal figures for rough estimates; installed MLI is considerably worse (typically one to a few watts per square metre from room temperature to 77 K), as the storage lesson notes. Cost and complexity rise down the table.</p>`,
q:"Why is an 80 K thermal shield so effective at cutting radiation to a 4.2 K surface?",o:["Radiated power goes as T⁴, so it is dominated by the warm surface: 80 K to 4.2 K gives only about 2 W/m² against about 400 W/m² from 290 K","Because 80 K is below the boiling point of helium","Because radiation is independent of temperature"],a:0,e:"σ(290⁴ − 4.2⁴) is about 401 W/m² while σ(80⁴ − 4.2⁴) is about 2.3 W/m². Intercepting the warm radiation at 80 K removes almost all of the load."},
{t:"Advanced: Counterflow heat exchangers in motion",lead:"Watch two streams exchange heat along an exchanger, see why counterflow beats parallel flow, and what each lost percent of effectiveness costs.",h:`<p>The heat exchanger is the workhorse of cryogenic plants: it lets the cold gas leaving the cold end cool the warm gas entering. The earlier lesson on heat exchanger effectiveness gave the definitions and the lecture's results; here you see the temperature profiles in motion and compare the arrangements. This lesson is general heat transfer theory, not taken from the lectures, and uses constant specific heats.</p><h3>Two streams, one wall</h3><p>In a <b>counterflow</b> exchanger the two streams run in opposite directions. In a <b>parallel-flow</b> exchanger they run the same way. Each stream has a <b>capacity rate</b> C = ṁ c<sub>p</sub> (mass flow times specific heat), in W/K. A stream with a small C changes temperature a lot for a given heat; one with a large C changes little. Call the smaller capacity rate C<sub>min</sub>, and the ratio C<sub>r</sub> = C<sub>min</sub>/C<sub>max</sub>. The size of the exchanger enters as the <b>number of transfer units</b>, NTU = U A / C<sub>min</sub>, where U is the overall heat transfer coefficient and A the area. The <b>effectiveness</b> ε is the actual heat exchanged divided by the most that could be exchanged, C<sub>min</sub> × (T<sub>hot,in</sub> − T<sub>cold,in</sub>).</p><ul><li>Counterflow: ε = [1 − exp(−NTU(1 − C<sub>r</sub>))] / [1 − C<sub>r</sub> exp(−NTU(1 − C<sub>r</sub>))], and ε = NTU / (1 + NTU) for C<sub>r</sub> = 1.</li><li>Parallel flow: ε = [1 − exp(−NTU(1 + C<sub>r</sub>))] / (1 + C<sub>r</sub>).</li></ul><p>In the animation the warm stream enters on the left and the cold stream, which in a liquefier is the returning low-pressure gas, enters at the other end (counterflow) or the same end (parallel flow). Dots carry the streams, coloured by their local temperature. The graph below shows both temperatures along the exchanger.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=cxf viewBox='0 0 640 330' role=img aria-label='Animated counterflow or parallel-flow heat exchanger with the temperature of the two streams plotted along its length'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=cxa><b>Arrangement</b></label><select id=cxa><option value=0>Counterflow</option><option value=1>Parallel flow</option></select><label for=cxn><b>NTU = <span id=cxnv>5</span></b></label><input type=range id=cxn min=-1 max=2 step=0.02 value=0.7><label for=cxr><b>Capacity-rate ratio C<sub>r</sub> = <span id=cxrv>0.9</span></b></label><input type=range id=cxr min=0.2 max=1 step=0.01 value=0.9><label for=cxs><b>The smaller capacity rate is the</b></label><select id=cxs><option value=0>Cold (returning) stream</option><option value=1>Warm stream</option></select><label for=cxt><b>Warm inlet = <span id=cxtv>300</span> K, cold inlet = <span id=cxcv>80</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=cxt min=150 max=320 step=5 value=300></div><div class=res id=cxo></div></div><div class=cap id=cxx></div></div><p>Things to try:</p><ul><li>Set C<sub>r</sub> = 1 and counterflow: the two temperature lines are parallel, the temperature difference is the same everywhere, and ε = NTU/(1+NTU). To reach ε = 0.95 you need NTU = 19.</li><li>Switch to parallel flow with C<sub>r</sub> = 1: the outlet temperatures can never cross, so ε can not exceed 0.5, however large the exchanger. That is why cryogenic plants use counterflow.</li><li>Reduce C<sub>r</sub> below 1 in counterflow, as happens in a liquefier where part of the flow leaves as liquid and the return stream is smaller: the exchanger can then do better at the same NTU.</li></ul><h3>What lost effectiveness costs</h3><p>The heat that the exchanger fails to recover, (1 − ε) C<sub>min</sub> (T<sub>hot,in</sub> − T<sub>cold,in</sub>), arrives at the cold end as an extra load that the refrigerator or the liquefier must remove. For a flow of 1 g/s of nitrogen gas (C ≈ 1.04 W/K per g/s), a drop in effectiveness from 0.98 to 0.95 between 300 K and 80 K adds about 7 W at the cold end for every gram per second. This is why cryogenic exchangers are built with very high effectiveness, and why the Linde–Hampson cycle needs a very good one.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>ε and NTU describe an exchanger; ε depends on NTU, C<sub>r</sub> and the arrangement</li><li>Counterflow can approach ε = 1; parallel flow cannot go beyond about 0.5 when C<sub>r</sub> = 1</li><li>Unrecovered heat, (1 − ε) C<sub>min</sub> ΔT, lands on the cold end as extra load</li><li>High NTU means large area, which means more cost and pressure drop: a design trade-off</li></ul></div>`,
q:"Why do cryogenic plants use counterflow rather than parallel-flow heat exchangers?",o:["Counterflow can reach effectiveness close to 1; parallel flow is limited to about 0.5 when the capacity rates are equal","Counterflow exchangers are always smaller","Parallel flow cannot work with gases"],a:0,e:"In parallel flow, the outlet temperatures can not cross, which limits ε. In counterflow, the cold stream can leave almost as warm as the hot inlet, so ε approaches 1 as NTU grows."},
{t:"Advanced: Ideal refrigerators and the cost of cold",lead:"Why a watt of cooling at 4 K costs about 75 W from the wall even in a perfect machine, and why ideal cycles cannot be built.",h:`<h3>The cheapest possible cold</h3><p>A cryogenic cycle lifts heat from a cold reservoir and dumps it into the warm surroundings. The second law says that costs work, and the ideal cycle shows the minimum. The ideal <b>isothermal-load refrigerator</b> is a Carnot refrigerator: isothermal compression at the ambient temperature, isentropic expansion, isothermal heat absorption at the cold temperature (the refrigeration load), isentropic compression. The refrigeration power is Q<sub>ref</sub> = T<sub>cold</sub>(s₄ − s₃) and the net work is (T<sub>amb</sub> − T<sub>cold</sub>)(s₄ − s₃), so</p><p><b>COP = T<sub>cold</sub> / (T<sub>amb</sub> − T<sub>cold</sub>)</b></p><p>for an ideal gas it depends only on the two temperatures, not on the fluid or its pressure. For a 4 K load and 300 K surroundings the COP is about 0.013: at least 75 W of mechanical power is needed to remove 1 W at 4 K. Real machines manage a fraction of this (often 10 to 30%), so a 4 K watt often costs 250 to 1000 W at the wall.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=irf viewBox='0 0 640 250' role=img aria-label='Ideal work per watt of cooling against load temperature, with a real-machine curve'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=irt><b>Cold temperature = <span id=irtv>4.2</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=irt min=0 max=2.45 step=0.01 value=0.62><label for=irh><b>Ambient temperature = <span id=irhv>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=irh min=250 max=320 step=1 value=300><label for=irp><b>Real machine reaches <span id=irpv>25</span>% of Carnot</b></label><input type=range id=irp min=3 max=60 step=1 value=25></div><div class=res id=iro></div></div><div class=cap id=irn></div></div><h3>When the load is not at one temperature</h3><p>Most real loads are not isothermal: a stream is cooled from a warm temperature T₂ down to T₁. The ideal cycle then has one isothermal process, two isentropic ones and an isobaric heat absorption. The refrigeration power is the enthalpy change, Q<sub>ref</sub> = h₂ − h₁, and for an ideal gas the COP becomes</p><p><b>COP = (r − 1) / [ (T<sub>amb</sub>/T₁) ln r − (r − 1) ]</b>, with r = T₂/T₁.</p><div class=calc><label for=ir1><b>Coldest temperature T₁ = <span id=ir1v>4.2</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=ir1 min=0 max=2.3 step=0.01 value=0.62><label for=ir2><b>Stream starts at T₂ = <span id=ir2v>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=ir2 min=5 max=300 step=1 value=300><div class=res id=ir3></div><div class=cap>Cooling a stream down is far cheaper per watt than holding the same coldest temperature isothermally, because most of the heat is lifted at warmer temperatures.</div></div><h3>The ideal liquefier</h3><p>An ideal liquefier starts with gas at ambient temperature and pressure, compresses it isothermally, and expands it isentropically to a saturated liquid, with all the gas liquefied (yield one) and make-up gas added at room temperature. The net work per unit mass is W/ṁ = T<sub>amb</sub>(s₁ − s<sub>f</sub>) − (h₁ − h<sub>f</sub>). It is the sum of a series of small Carnot refrigerators between the ambient temperature and the gas temperature as it cools, plus a Carnot refrigerator at the boiling point that removes the latent heat. Helium is nearly an ideal gas (C<sub>p</sub> about 5/2 R = 20.8 J/mol·K, latent heat 84 J/mol at 4.2 K), so the numbers are easy:</p><div class=calc><label for=ilh><b>Ambient temperature = <span id=ilhv>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=ilh min=250 max=320 step=1 value=300><div class=res id=ilo></div><div class=cap>At 300 K this reproduces the lecture: about 26,400 J/mol, which is 6600 J/g or about 0.23 kWh per litre of liquid helium, as the minimum.</div></div><h3>Why ideal cycles cannot be built</h3><ul><li><b>The compressor would have to be absurd.</b> An isothermal compressor must discharge at p<sub>ideal</sub> = p<sub>atm</sub> exp(ΔS/R). The lecture finds: nitrogen about 7.4 × 10⁵ atm (74 GPa), argon about 1.7 × 10⁵ atm (17 GPa), helium-4 about 7.2 × 10⁵ atm (72 GPa), hydrogen about 9.5 × 10⁵ atm, neon about 9.8 × 10⁵ atm. No compressor can do that.</li><li><b>Isentropic expansion ends in liquid.</b> A turbine or piston expander cannot handle a stream that finishes as saturated liquid.</li><li><b>The fluid is a real gas.</b> Enthalpy depends on pressure as well as temperature, which is exactly what a throttle exploits.</li></ul><h3>The practical cycle</h3><p>Real cycles stop compression at an achievable pressure, add a counterflow heat exchanger that pre-cools the forward stream with the cold return stream (so that the expansion starts below the inversion temperature), and replace the isentropic expander with an isenthalpic Joule–Thomson valve near the two-phase dome. The price is that the cycle ends <i>inside</i> the dome: only part of the stream liquefies (yield y &lt; 1) and the isothermal refrigeration available falls from T(s<sub>v</sub> − s<sub>f</sub>) to T(s<sub>v</sub> − s₄). The heat exchange and throttling generate entropy, so the work per unit liquid is larger. Real compressors are described by an isothermal efficiency and a mechanical efficiency (overall is their product): compression is adiabatic followed by constant-pressure heat removal in an aftercooler. Expanders have an adiabatic efficiency, η = (h₁ − h₂)/(h₁ − h₂<sub>s</sub>), and a mechanical efficiency. Heat exchangers are <i>recuperative</i> (continuous steady flows) or <i>regenerative</i> (warm and cold streams use the same channel at different times, with the matrix storing heat). The following lessons build on these pieces.</p>`,
q:"Roughly how much power must a perfect (Carnot) refrigerator draw from a 300 K supply to remove 1 W at 4 K?",o:["About 75 W","About 4 W","About 1 W"],a:0,e:"COP = Tc/(Tamb−Tc) = 4/296 = 0.0135, so about 74 W per watt. Real machines need several times more."},
{t:"Advanced: The Joule–Thomson effect in motion",lead:"Watch gas molecules slow down as they are pulled apart, and see why hydrogen and helium warm up when throttled at room temperature.",h:`<h3>What a throttle does</h3><p>Force a gas through a valve, a porous plug or a narrow orifice and its pressure drops sharply. No work is done on the surroundings, and with good insulation no heat flows in or out, so the <b>enthalpy stays constant</b>: h₁ = h₂. This is the Joule–Thomson (J–T) expansion, and it is the cooling step of every simple liquefier. The temperature change per unit pressure drop is the <b>Joule–Thomson coefficient</b>, μ = (∂T/∂P) at constant enthalpy. Positive μ means the gas cools as the pressure falls; negative μ means it warms.</p><h3>Why a real gas cools</h3><p>For an ideal gas the molecules ignore one another, enthalpy depends on temperature alone, and a throttle changes nothing. Real molecules do two things:</p><ul><li><b>They attract each other</b> at moderate distances. When the gas expands the molecules are pulled apart, which raises their potential energy. With total energy fixed, the kinetic energy falls, so the gas cools.</li><li><b>They have a finite size</b>, so they also repel at close range. In a hot, dense gas this repulsion dominates, and expansion releases energy that heats the gas.</li></ul><p>Which effect wins depends on temperature. Below the <b>inversion temperature</b> attraction wins and the gas cools; above it the gas warms. The simplest model that captures both effects is the van der Waals gas, with attraction constant a and molecular volume b. At low pressure it gives μ ≈ (2a/RT − b)/C<sub>p</sub>, so the inversion temperature is T<sub>i</sub> = 2a/(Rb). Below, drag the sliders and watch the molecules. The speed of each dot is drawn in proportion to √T, and its colour runs from blue (cold) to red (hot).</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=jtf viewBox='0 0 640 235' role=img aria-label='Gas molecules flowing from a high-pressure chamber through a porous plug into a low-pressure chamber, changing speed and colour as the temperature changes'></svg><div class=two><svg class=sv id=jtc viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='Inversion curve for the chosen gas with the start and end states of the throttle'></svg><div><label for=jtg><b>Gas</b></label><select id=jtg><option value=0>Nitrogen</option><option value=1>Oxygen</option><option value=2>Carbon dioxide</option><option value=3>Hydrogen</option><option value=4>Helium</option></select><label for=jtt><b>Inlet temperature T₁ = <span id=jttv>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=jtt min=20 max=700 step=1 value=300><label for=jtp><b>Inlet pressure P₁ = <span id=jtpv>100</span> bar</b></label><input type=range id=jtp min=2 max=250 step=1 value=100><div class=res id=jto></div></div></div><div class=cap id=jtn></div></div><p>The left chamber is at P₁ and the right chamber at 1 bar. A few outlined molecules stream through the plug so you can watch one change from its high-pressure speed to its low-pressure speed. The chart on the left shows the van der Waals inversion curve for the chosen gas, plotted on a logarithmic temperature axis. States inside the shaded dome cool when throttled; states outside warm. The dashed line joins the start and end of your throttle.</p><h3>Inversion temperatures: a model and the real thing</h3><p>The van der Waals model gets the pattern right, and it explains why nitrogen and oxygen cool at room temperature while hydrogen and helium do not. It is not accurate in the numbers: it over-estimates the inversion temperature of the heavier gases badly. The handbook values below are standard figures from outside the lectures.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Gas</th><th>van der Waals, 2a/Rb</th><th>Measured (handbook)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Helium</td><td>35 K</td><td>about 40 K</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrogen</td><td>224 K</td><td>about 202 K</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>852 K</td><td>about 621 K</td></tr><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>1043 K</td><td>about 764 K</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon dioxide</td><td>2053 K</td><td>about 1500 K</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The calculator integrates the low-pressure coefficient down the pressure drop in small steps, so at high pressure it shows the right sign and order of magnitude but not table-grade numbers. Real handbook values should be used for design. When the exit temperature would fall below the normal boiling point, the calculator stops there: the rest of the cooling goes into making liquid, which this simple model does not follow.</p><h3>What this means for liquefiers</h3><ul><li><b>Nitrogen, oxygen, air and argon</b> start at room temperature far below their inversion temperatures, so a simple Linde–Hampson cycle works from the outset.</li><li><b>Hydrogen</b> must be pre-cooled below about 200 K, usually with liquid nitrogen, before a J–T valve helps.</li><li><b>Helium</b> must first be cooled below about 40 K, using an expansion engine or a liquid-hydrogen bath, before a J–T valve can finish the job. That is why every helium liquefier has expanders ahead of the valve.</li></ul><h3>J–T valve versus expansion engine</h3><p>A throttle is irreversible: it wastes the pressure energy of the gas and cools only as a side effect. An expansion engine makes the gas do work and expands it nearly isentropically. It cools every gas, whatever its inversion temperature, and the work can be recovered. The price is moving parts at low temperature, so most plants combine both, with engines for the bulk cooling and a valve for the last step into the liquid.</p>`,
q:"You throttle hydrogen gas from 100 bar to 1 bar, starting at 300 K. What happens to its temperature?",o:["It cools, just as nitrogen does","It warms slightly, because 300 K is above the inversion temperature of hydrogen (about 200 K)","It does not change, because a throttle is isentropic"],a:1,e:"Hydrogen's inversion temperature is about 200 K. Above it, the repulsive part of the molecular interaction dominates and the gas warms on throttling, so hydrogen must be pre-cooled before a J–T valve will liquefy it."},
{t:"Advanced: Linde–Hampson liquefier",lead:"From the energy balance to liquid yield and work, with worked cases.",h:"<h3>Why an ideal liquefier is impractical</h3><p>An ideal cycle compresses all the gas isothermally and then expands it reversibly until it liquefies, so every kilogram compressed becomes liquid. For nitrogen starting at 300 K and 1 bar, the lectures show the pressure needed would exceed 70,000 bar. Real liquefiers instead use a heat exchanger to recover cold and a Joule–Thomson valve to make the liquid.</p><h3>The system</h3><div class=fig><svg class=sv viewBox='0 0 640 190' role=img aria-label='Linde–Hampson liquefier schematic'><circle class=o cx=70 cy=100 r=34 /><text x=70 y=104 text-anchor='middle'>Comp.</text><path class=o d='M70,20 V66' /><text x=70 y=14 text-anchor='middle'>makeup gas</text><rect class=o x=230 y=62 width=170 height=76 rx=6 /><text x=315 y=156 text-anchor='middle'>Counterflow heat exchanger</text><path class='h fl' d='M101,85 H450' /><path class=o d='M450,75 L474,95 L474,75 L450,95 Z' /><text x=462 y=66 text-anchor='middle'>J–T valve</text><path class='h fl' d='M474,85 H520' /><rect class=o x=520 y=55 width=100 height=100 rx=6 /><rect class=liq x=522 y=130 width=96 height=23 /><text x=570 y=95 text-anchor='middle'>Receiver</text><path class='c fl' d='M520,115 H101' /><text x=320 y=182 text-anchor='middle'>Red: warm high-pressure stream. Blue: cold low-pressure return.</text></svg><div class=cap>Animation: gas is compressed, precooled by the cold return stream, expanded through the valve, and the liquid collects in the receiver.</div></div><p>The compressor is assumed isothermal, the exchanger 100% effective with no pressure drops, and the valve isenthalpic. Only part of the gas liquefies, so this is an open cycle: makeup gas replaces what leaves as liquid.</p><h3>Liquid yield</h3><p>An energy balance on the exchanger, valve and receiver gives the fraction liquefied:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>y = m<sub>f</sub> / m = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>) / (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>)</b></p><p>Here state 1 is the low-pressure inlet gas, state 2 is the compressed gas at the same temperature, and f is saturated liquid at the low pressure.</p><h3>Work</h3><p>Combining the first law with the second-law heat rejection of an isothermal compressor gives the work input per unit mass of gas compressed:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>W<sub>c</sub>/m = T<sub>1</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>2</sub>) − (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)</b></p><p>Dividing by y gives the work per unit mass of liquid, the number that really counts.</p><h3>Choosing the compression pressure</h3><p>To maximise y you want h<sub>2</sub> as small as possible. Along the isothermal line, h<sub>2</sub> is lowest where (∂h/∂p)<sub>T</sub> = −μ<sub>JT</sub>·C<sub>p</sub> = 0. Since C<sub>p</sub> is positive, this needs μ<sub>JT</sub> = 0, so state 2 should sit on the inversion curve.</p><h3>Try the worked cases</h3><div class=calc><label for=lhs><b>Operating case</b></label><select id=lhs></select><div class=res id=lho></div><div class=cap id=lhn></div></div><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>y = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>)</li><li>Yield rises with higher compression pressure and lower inlet temperature</li><li>Best yield needs state 2 on the inversion curve</li></ul></div>",q:"Why does maximum liquid yield need state 2 on the inversion curve?",o:["The compressor then needs no work","h2 is smallest there, because (∂h/∂p)T = −μJT·Cp = 0","The heat exchanger becomes 100% effective"],a:1,e:"Along the isothermal line h2 is lowest where μJT = 0, which makes h1 − h2 and so y as large as possible."},
{t:"Advanced: Heat exchanger effectiveness",lead:"Why a Linde–Hampson liquefier lives or dies by its counterflow exchanger.",h:`<h3>What effectiveness means</h3><p>A heat exchanger passes cold from the returning low-pressure stream to the incoming high-pressure stream, at constant pressure. Its <b>effectiveness</b> ε is the heat actually transferred divided by the most that could be transferred in theory:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>ε = Q<sub>actual</sub> / Q<sub>max</sub>, with Q<sub>max</sub> = (ṁC<sub>p</sub>)<sub>min</sub> (T<sub>hot,in</sub> − T<sub>cold,in</sub>)</b></p><p>It is a number between 0 and 1. In the Linde–Hampson cycle it can be written with enthalpies: the returning gas leaves at h<sub>1′</sub> instead of the ideal h<sub>1</sub>, so</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>ε = (h<sub>1′</sub> − h<sub>g</sub>) / (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>g</sub>)</b></p><h3>Effect on liquid yield</h3><p>An energy balance with the imperfect exchanger gives</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>y = [(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>) − (1 − ε)(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>g</sub>)] / [(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>) − (1 − ε)(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>g</sub>)]</b></p><p>The term (1 − ε)(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>g</sub>) is the cold that leaks away at the warm end. It is subtracted from the small cooling effect of the valve, so a modest loss of effectiveness removes most of the yield. Try it with the lecture's air case (1.013 to 202.6 bar, 300 K).</p><div class=calc><label for=hxs><b>Effectiveness ε = <span id=hxv>1.00</span></b></label><input type=range id=hxs min=80 max=100 step=1 value=100><div class=res id=hxo></div><div class=cap id=hxn></div><b>Liquid yield against effectiveness</b><svg class=sv id=hxc viewBox='0 0 320 170'></svg></div><p>The lecture tabulates 1.00 → 0.085, 0.95 → 0.060, 0.90 → 0.034 and 0.85 → 0.006. The curve above reproduces those and shows that yield reaches zero near ε = 0.84. That is why the lecture says effectiveness must be better than about 85% before the cycle makes any liquid at all, and why real liquefiers use very long, carefully designed exchangers.</p><h3>Figure of merit</h3><p>To compare liquefiers fairly, the lecture defines the <b>figure of merit</b>: the ideal work per unit mass liquefied divided by the actual work per unit mass liquefied.</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>FOM = (W<sub>ideal</sub>/m<sub>f</sub>) / (W<sub>actual</sub>/m<sub>f</sub>)</b></p><p>Worked case from the lecture: nitrogen, 1.013 to 202.6 bar, 300 K, ε = 1.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ideal work, T<sub>1</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>f</sub>) − (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>)</td><td>761 J/g liquefied</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid yield y</td><td>0.074</td></tr><tr><td>Work per g compressed</td><td>463 J/g</td></tr><tr><td>Work per g liquefied (463 / 0.074)</td><td>about 6265 J/g</td></tr><tr><td>Figure of merit</td><td>about 0.12</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>So even a perfect-exchanger Linde–Hampson liquefier uses roughly eight times the ideal work. Later lessons show how precooling, a second pressure level and expansion engines close that gap.</p>`,
q:"Why must heat exchanger effectiveness exceed about 0.84 in a Linde–Hampson liquefier?",o:["Below it the compressor cannot reach the required pressure","Below it the cold lost at the warm end outweighs the cooling from the valve, so yield falls to zero","Below it the gas freezes inside the valve"],a:1,e:"The leak term (1 − ε)(h1 − hg) is subtracted from the valve's small cooling effect. Once it equals that effect, nothing is left to liquefy."},
{t:"Advanced: Precooled and dual-pressure systems",lead:"Two ways to beat the plain Linde–Hampson cycle: pre-chill the gas, or compress less of it.",h:`<h3>Precooled Linde–Hampson</h3><p>Yield rises as the gas is compressed at a lower temperature. <b>Precooling</b> does this with a separate closed-cycle refrigerator (commonly R134a, ammonia or CO<sub>2</sub>) thermally coupled to the liquefier through a three-fluid heat exchanger. The gas being liquefied is the <i>primary fluid</i>; the refrigerant is the <i>secondary fluid</i>. The refrigerant's boiling point at its suction pressure sets the limit: 216.6 K for CO<sub>2</sub>, 240 K for NH<sub>3</sub> and 247 K for R134a at 1 bar.</p><p>With r = ṁ<sub>r</sub>/ṁ the ratio of refrigerant to gas flow, the energy balance gives</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>y = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>) + r (h<sub>a,r</sub> − h<sub>d,r</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>)</b></p><p>The first term is the plain Linde–Hampson yield. The second is the extra yield bought by precooling. The work also gains a term for the refrigerant compressor:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>W/m = [T<sub>1</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>2</sub>) − (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)] + r (h<sub>b,r</sub> − h<sub>a,r</sub>)</b></p><p>When the three-fluid exchanger is perfect, the gas reaches the refrigerant's boiling temperature, and the yield is at its maximum, y<sub>max</sub> = (h<sub>6</sub> − h<sub>3</sub>)/(h<sub>6</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>), with both enthalpies at that temperature.</p><h3>Try the mass ratio r</h3><p>Nitrogen precooled by R134a, 1.013 to 101.3 bar, 300 K, using the lecture's enthalpies. Raise r and watch the yield climb, then stop.</p><div class=calc><label for=pcs><b>Refrigerant ratio r = <span id=pcv>0.05</span></b></label><input type=range id=pcs min=0 max=0.2 step=0.005 value=0.05><div class=res id=pco></div><div class=cap id=pcn></div><div class=two><div><b>Liquid yield y against r</b><svg class=sv id=pcy viewBox='0 0 300 170'></svg></div><div><b>Figure of merit against r</b><svg class=sv id=pcf viewBox='0 0 300 170'></svg></div></div><div class=cap>Dashed line: the limiting ratio, about 0.11. Above it the lecture notes that excess refrigerant makes the state entering the refrigerant compressor a two-phase mixture, which is undesirable. Here the yield is held at y<sub>max</sub> while the work keeps rising, which is an approximation. The calculator uses the refrigerant enthalpies of lecture 6 (h<sub>a</sub> = 380, h<sub>d</sub> = 240, h<sub>b</sub> = 420 J/g), so at r = 0.08 it gives 0.065, slightly different from the 0.063 in the table below, which uses lecture 5's refrigerant data.</div></div><p>The lecture's tabulated results for this case are:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>y</th><th>W/m<sub>f</sub> (J/g)</th><th>FOM</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Simple Linde–Hampson</td><td>0.04</td><td>9475</td><td>0.081</td></tr><tr><td>Precooled, r = 0.08</td><td>0.063</td><td>6132</td><td>0.125</td></tr><tr><td>Precooled, r at limit (y<sub>max</sub>)</td><td>0.074</td><td>about 5200</td><td>about 0.148</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Yield and figure of merit rise with r and with compression pressure, up to the limiting ratio. The limiting r itself grows as the pressure grows. Precooling costs extra work, but the liquid output rises faster than the work, so work per kilogram of liquid falls.</p><h3>Linde dual-pressure system</h3><p>Compression work for an ideal isothermal compressor is W = ṁRT ln(P<sub>2</sub>/P<sub>1</sub>). It falls if the mass flow falls or the pressure ratio falls. The dual-pressure system exploits the first: only part of the gas (ṁ − ṁ<sub>i</sub>) is compressed to an intermediate pressure. The stream ṁ<sub>i</sub> is then added, and the combined flow goes up to the full high pressure. The system has two compressors, a three-fluid exchanger, two J–T valves and two liquid receivers. With i = ṁ<sub>i</sub>/ṁ the intermediate mass ratio, the lecture gives</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>y = [(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>3</sub>) − i (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)] / (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>)</b></p><p>where state 3 is at the high pressure and state 2 at the intermediate pressure. Yield is slightly lower than in the plain cycle, but the work drops by more. In the lecture's comparison for nitrogen between 1 and 200 atm, an intermediate pressure of 50 atm gives y = 0.051 and W/m<sub>f</sub> = 3866 J/g, against 6840 J/g for the simple cycle. That lifts the figure of merit from 0.112 to 0.198.</p>`,
q:"In the precooled Linde–Hampson system, what happens if the refrigerant ratio r is raised well above its limiting value?",o:["Yield keeps rising in a straight line","Yield stops rising because the refrigerant state becomes two-phase, while work keeps increasing","The J–T valve stops working"],a:1,e:"Beyond the limit there is more cold than the primary gas can absorb, so the state at the refrigerant compressor inlet turns into a mixture. The yield sits at ymax and extra work is wasted."},
{t:"Advanced: Claude system",lead:"Adding an expansion engine to recover work and cold.",h:"<h3>Why add an expansion engine</h3><p>A J–T valve expands gas at constant enthalpy. That is irreversible and wastes the pressure energy of the gas. An expansion engine instead lets the gas do work while it expands, which is close to a reversible adiabatic (isentropic) process. It cools any gas whatever its inversion temperature, and the work can help drive the compressor. The system is named after its inventor, who built an air liquefier on this idea and established l'Air Liquide in 1902.</p><h3>The system</h3><div class=fig><svg class=sv viewBox='0 0 640 240' role=img aria-label='Claude liquefier schematic'><circle class=o cx=55 cy=125 r=28 /><text x=55 y=129 text-anchor='middle'>Comp.</text><text x=55 y=40 text-anchor='middle'>makeup gas</text><path class='c fl' d='M55,46 V80' /><rect class=o x=120 y=64 width=70 height=122 rx=6 /><rect class=o x=290 y=64 width=70 height=122 rx=6 /><rect class=o x=420 y=64 width=70 height=122 rx=6 /><text x=155 y=204 text-anchor='middle'>HX1</text><text x=325 y=204 text-anchor='middle'>HX2</text><text x=455 y=204 text-anchor='middle'>HX3</text><path class='c fl' d='M560,80 H55 V97' /><path class='h fl' d='M55,153 V170 H508' /><path class=o d='M508,158 L532,182 L532,158 L508,182 Z' /><text x=520 y=150 text-anchor='middle'>J–T valve</text><path class='h fl' d='M532,170 H560' /><rect class=o x=560 y=60 width=70 height=130 rx=6 /><rect class=liq x=562 y=150 width=66 height=38 /><text x=595 y=105 text-anchor='middle'>Receiver</text><circle class=o cx=240 cy=125 r=24 /><text x=240 y=129 text-anchor='middle'>Exp.</text><path class='h fl' d='M240,170 V149' /><path class='c fl' d='M240,101 V80' /><text x=320 y=232 text-anchor='middle'>Red: warm high-pressure stream. Blue: cold return stream.</text></svg><div class=cap>Animation: a fraction x of the compressed gas is diverted at state 3, expanded in the engine to state e, and rejoins the cold return stream ahead of the first exchanger.</div></div><p>The cycle has a compressor, three counterflow exchangers, an expansion engine working across the second exchanger, a J–T valve and a makeup connection. The rest of the gas, a fraction 1 − x, carries on to the valve.</p><h3>Liquid yield</h3><p>An energy balance on the exchangers, valve and receiver gives:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>y = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>) + x (h<sub>3</sub> − h<sub>e</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>)</b></p><p>The first term is the yield of a simple Linde–Hampson cycle. The second is the extra yield from the expander, where x = m<sub>e</sub>/m. For a fixed expander inlet state 3, y rises in a straight line with x.</p><p>The balance only holds while some gas is left in the return stream, m − m<sub>f</sub> − m<sub>e</sub> &gt; 0, which means <b>x + y &lt; 1</b>. Past that point the lecture uses a limiting yield <b>y = 0.99 − x</b>, and notes that the real yield may be lower.</p><h3>Work</h3><p style='text-align:center'><b>−W<sub>net</sub>/m = T<sub>1</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>2</sub>) − (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>) − x (h<sub>3</sub> − h<sub>e</sub>)</b></p><p>The first part is the compressor work of the simple Linde–Hampson cycle. The last term is the work recovered by the expander, assuming it helps drive the compressor.</p><h3>Explore: nitrogen, 1.013 to 40.52 bar, expander inlet 225 K</h3><div class=calc><label for=clx><b>Expander flow ratio x = <span id=clv>0.20</span></b></label><input type=range id=clx min=0.05 max=0.95 step=0.01 value=0.2><div class=res id=clo></div><div class=cap id=cln></div><div class=two><div><b>Liquid yield y against x</b><svg class=sv id=cly viewBox='0 0 300 170'></svg></div><div><b>Work per g liquefied (J/g) against x</b><svg class=sv id=clw viewBox='0 0 300 170'></svg></div></div><div class=cap>Dashed line: the x where x + y reaches 1, about 0.73. Beyond it the limiting yield applies. The work curve is cut off at 6000 J/g.</div></div><h3>Effect of expander inlet temperature</h3><p>The lecture repeats the calculation for four expander inlet temperatures. Its tabulated results at the best point of each case are:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Expander inlet T<sub>3</sub></th><th>x at best point</th><th>Max yield y</th><th>Min work (J/g liquefied)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>300 K</td><td>0.67</td><td>0.32</td><td>608.8</td></tr><tr><td>275 K</td><td>0.69</td><td>0.30</td><td>659.3</td></tr><tr><td>250 K</td><td>0.72</td><td>0.27</td><td>773.4</td></tr><tr><td>225 K</td><td>0.73</td><td>0.26</td><td>866.8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In every case yield peaks, and work per unit of liquid is lowest, where x + y reaches the limit. The peak moves to higher x as T<sub>3</sub> falls. The best work here, roughly 600 to 870 J/g, is far below the 5214 J/g of the simple Linde–Hampson air case in the previous lesson, although those two cases use different gases and pressures.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>The expander removes energy reversibly and returns work</li><li>y = simple L–H yield + x (h<sub>3</sub> − h<sub>e</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>), valid for x + y &lt; 1</li><li>Yield has a maximum and work per unit liquid a minimum as x increases</li></ul></div>",q:"Why is the Claude-system yield equation only valid while x + y < 1?",o:["Otherwise the expander would overheat","Otherwise no gas would be left in the return stream to cool the incoming gas","Otherwise the compressor work would become negative"],a:1,e:"The return stream carries m − mf − me. It must stay positive, which means x + y < 1. Beyond that the lecture uses the limiting yield y = 0.99 − x."},
{t:"Advanced: Claude family and system comparison",lead:"Real expanders, three famous variants, the hardware inside a plant, and all eight cycles side by side.",h:`<h3>When compressor and expander are not perfect</h3><p>Real machines lose some of the ideal performance. The lecture splits each loss in two. For the compressor, the overall efficiency is η<sub>oval,c</sub> = η<sub>mech,c</sub> × η<sub>iso,c</sub>, which covers friction and the departure from isothermal compression. For the expander, η<sub>oval,e</sub> = η<sub>mech,e</sub> × η<sub>ad,e</sub>, which covers friction and the departure from isentropic expansion. The Claude equations become</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>y = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>) + x η<sub>ad,e</sub> (h<sub>3</sub> − h<sub>e</sub>)/(h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>)</b></p><p style='text-align:center'><b>−W<sub>net</sub>/m = [T<sub>1</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>2</sub>) − (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>)] / η<sub>oval,c</sub> − x η<sub>oval,e</sub> (h<sub>3</sub> − h<sub>e</sub>)</b></p><p>The lecture tests nitrogen between 1 and 50 atm with η<sub>oval,c</sub> = 0.75 and η<sub>mech,e</sub> = η<sub>ad,e</sub> = 0.86. The maximum yield falls by about 10% for T<sub>3</sub> = 300 K and about 9% for 275 K, and the work per unit of liquid rises, so the best operating point shifts.</p><h3>Three variants</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Idea</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Kapitza</b> (1939, Pyotr Kapitza)</td><td>A low-pressure air liquefier. The first exchanger is replaced by a set of valved regenerators that cool the incoming air and purify it by freezing out impurities, then warm and flush them out in the next half-cycle. It was the first to use a rotary turbo-expander in place of a reciprocating one, which let the third exchanger of the Claude system be dropped.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Heylandt</b> (1949)</td><td>A high-pressure air liquefier, typically around 200 atm. Heylandt noticed that with x = 0.6 the best expander inlet temperature was close to ambient, so he removed the first exchanger. The expander then runs at ambient inlet, which greatly simplifies lubrication and operation on the high-pressure side.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Collins</b> (1946, Samuel C. Collins)</td><td>An extension of the Claude system to reach liquid-helium temperatures, using two to six expanders depending on the inlet pressure. Helium's inversion temperature is only about 45 K, so the gas must be at less than about 7.5 K before the J–T valve to give any liquid.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Compare all eight cycles</h3><p>The lecture holds the working fluid and conditions fixed (nitrogen from 1 atm and 300 K, perfect equipment) so the cycles can be compared. Collins is for helium. Pick a measure.</p><div class=calc><label for=scm><b>Measure</b></label><select id=scm><option value=0>Liquid yield y</option><option value=2>Figure of merit</option><option value=1>Work per g liquefied (J/g, log scale)</option></select><svg class=sv id=scc viewBox='0 0 640 300' role=img aria-label='Bar chart comparing liquefaction cycles'></svg><div class=cap id=scn></div></div><p>Cycles with an expansion engine (Claude, Kapitza, Heylandt) reach a figure of merit above 0.85 and need only about 800 to 900 J/g, close to the ideal 767 J/g. The throttling-only cycles need 4000 to 7000 J/g. Helium by the Collins cycle needs about 25,000 J/g even though its figure of merit is 0.27, because helium is so hard to liquefy.</p><h3>Hardware that decides real performance</h3><ul><li><b>Heat exchangers</b> must give high effectiveness with little pressure drop, be compact and light, and be reliable. Common forms are Linde tube-in-tube (with a wire spacer on the low-pressure side to enhance heat transfer), the Collins coiled-fin tube exchanger with edge-wound copper helix, and the aluminium brazed plate-fin exchanger, the most compact, widely used in air separation and helium plants.</li><li><b>Compressors</b> are the biggest heat source in the plant. Reciprocating machines suit high pressure and low flow, rotary ones suit high flow at moderate pressure. Screw and scroll compressors offer higher isothermal efficiency, low cost and low vibration but are oil-lubricated, so oil filters and a charcoal bed purify the gas. Centrifugal machines are used in large separation plants.</li><li><b>Expanders</b> make the cold. Reciprocating types serve low flow and high pressure ratio, turbo-expanders serve high flow and low pressure ratio, with a compressor on the same shaft absorbing the output and almost no maintenance. They must be well insulated against heat leak.</li></ul>`,
q:"What did the Kapitza system change compared with the Claude system?",o:["It used a piston expander at very high pressure","It used a rotary turbo-expander, which allowed the third heat exchanger to be dropped","It added a second J–T valve and receiver"],a:1,e:"Kapitza's low-pressure system used valved regenerators and the first turbo-expander, so the third exchanger of the Claude system was no longer needed."},
{t:"Advanced: Liquefier cycles in motion",lead:"Follow the gas around the loop in the Linde–Hampson, precooled and Claude cycles, and see where the liquid comes from.",h:`<h3>Read the loop</h3><p>Every liquefier does the same six things in the same order. Compress the gas, cool it back to room temperature, cool it further against the cold stream returning from the receiver, expand it through a valve so that a fraction condenses, separate the liquid from the vapour, and send the cold vapour back to chill the next batch of gas. Make-up gas replaces whatever leaves as liquid. The animation below shows these six steps in a plain Linde–Hampson loop. The tabs add the two big improvements: a pre-cooling bath and an expansion engine. Pipe colour runs from red (warm) to blue (cold), and the moving dots show the direction of flow.</p><div class=fig><div><button class=btn id=lcb0>Linde–Hampson</button> <button class=btn id=lcb1>Precooled</button> <button class=btn id=lcb2>Claude (expander)</button></div><svg class=sv id=lcf viewBox='0 0 640 374' role=img aria-label='Animated flow diagram of a gas liquefier loop with compressor, heat exchanger, J–T valve and liquid receiver'></svg><div><div id=lcc0><label for=lcp><b>Compression pressure</b></label><select id=lcp><option value=0>40.52 bar</option><option value=1>101.3 bar</option></select></div><div id=lcc1><label for=lcr><b>Refrigerant ratio r = <span id=lcrv>0.05</span></b></label><input type=range id=lcr min=0 max=0.2 step=0.005 value=0.05></div><div id=lcc2><label for=lcx><b>Fraction through the expander x = <span id=lcxv>0.30</span></b></label><input type=range id=lcx min=0 max=0.7 step=0.01 value=0.3></div><div class=res id=lco></div><div class=cap id=lcn></div></div></div><h3>What each tab adds</h3><ul><li><b>Linde–Hampson.</b> The only cooling is the J–T valve, helped by the counterflow exchanger. Only about 2 to 4% of the gas becomes liquid per pass for nitrogen, so the compressor does a great deal of work for little liquid. The yield is y = (h₁ − h₂)/(h₁ − h<sub>f</sub>).</li><li><b>Precooled.</b> A separate refrigerator, such as an R134a loop, chills the high-pressure gas before it enters the exchanger. A lower starting temperature means a larger enthalpy difference across the valve and a higher yield. The yield rises linearly with r until the refrigerant stream reaches its limit.</li><li><b>Claude.</b> A fraction x of the compressed gas is diverted to an expansion engine, where it does work and comes out much colder. It then joins the cold return stream. The engine adds a second source of cooling, and the yield rises linearly with x until x + y approaches 1.</li></ul><p>The numbers under the figure use the lecture's enthalpies for nitrogen at 300 K: the Linde–Hampson and Claude cases use 1.013 to 40.52 bar or 101.3 bar, the precooled case uses 1.013 to 101.3 bar with R134a. The figure of merit is the ideal work of liquefying nitrogen (767 J/g) divided by the real work per gram liquefied. Values are rounded from the lecture tables, so results are approximate, and the ideal expander and exchanger are assumed.</p><h3>Why the exchanger is the heart of the machine</h3><p>The J–T valve only cools the gas by a few kelvin per pass. The counterflow heat exchanger is what lets the cold build up: each trip around the loop starts a little colder than the one before, until the gas reaching the valve is cold enough to condense. Look at the colour gradient in the high-pressure line as you step through the tabs: the pipe is red at the top and deep blue at the bottom because the returning vapour has cooled it. An exchanger that is not effective enough lets the warm end leak cold away and the loop never reaches liquid.</p>`,
q:"In the Claude cycle, what does the expansion engine add that the J–T valve alone cannot?",o:["It cools part of the gas by making it do work, which removes more energy than throttling and also recovers some compressor work","It makes the gas liquid directly inside the engine","It removes the need for a heat exchanger"],a:0,e:"A throttle conserves enthalpy and only cools a real gas a little. An expansion engine takes energy out as work, giving much greater cooling for any gas, and the recovered work lowers the net compressor power."},
{t:"Advanced: Liquefaction cycles on the T–s diagram",lead:"Follow one parcel of nitrogen round the Carnot, ideal, Linde–Hampson, precooled and Claude cycles on a real temperature–entropy diagram, and see where the work goes and where the cold comes from.",h:`<p>The earlier lessons give the energy balances of each liquefier. To <em>see</em> what the gas does, engineers draw the cycle on a <b>temperature–entropy (T–s) diagram</b>. This lesson draws it for nitrogen, using the accurate reference equation of state for nitrogen, so the lines are real, not sketches. The entropy is measured from saturated liquid at 1 atm. This lesson is not taken from the lectures, and the figures it reports can differ a little from the lecture's table values because they come from a different property source.</p><h3>How to read a T–s diagram</h3><ul><li>The <b>dome</b> is the two-phase region. Inside it, liquid and vapour coexist. Left of the dome is liquid, right of it is gas, and above it, supercritical fluid. The top of the dome is the critical point, 126 K for nitrogen.</li><li>A horizontal line is <b>isothermal</b>; a vertical line is <b>isentropic</b> (reversible and adiabatic, as in an ideal compressor or expander).</li><li>The area under a path, ∫T ds, is the <b>heat</b> exchanged in that step. A heat leak into a reversible step moves the path to the right; a heat rejection moves it to the left.</li><li>Thin curves are <b>isobars</b> (constant pressure). The ones drawn are 1 bar (the low-pressure side) and the cycle's high pressure.</li><li>Curves of constant enthalpy, <b>isenthalps</b>, can be switched on. A Joule–Thomson expansion follows one of them, always towards higher entropy because it is irreversible.</li></ul><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=ycf viewBox='0 0 640 380' role=img aria-label='Temperature-entropy diagram of nitrogen with the dome, isobars and an animated liquefaction cycle'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=ycs><b>Cycle</b></label><select id=ycs><option value=carnot>Carnot refrigerator (77 K to 300 K)</option><option value=ideal>Ideal (reversible) liquefier</option><option value=lh selected>Linde–Hampson liquefier</option><option value=pc>Precooled Linde–Hampson</option><option value=cl>Claude liquefier (schematic)</option></select><label for=ycp><b>High pressure</b></label><select id=ycp></select><label><input type=checkbox id=yci> Show isenthalps (lines of constant enthalpy)</label><div><button class=btn id=ycg>Pause</button></div></div><div class=res id=yco></div></div><div class=cap id=ycn></div></div><h3>What to notice</h3><ul><li><b>Carnot refrigerator.</b> A rectangle running counter-clockwise. The cooling effect is the area under the cold isothermal at 77 K; the work is the enclosed area. The ratio is the Carnot coefficient of performance, T<sub>c</sub>/(T<sub>h</sub> − T<sub>c</sub>) = 0.345. It is the limit nothing can beat, but it needs a perfect compressor and expander.</li><li><b>Ideal liquefier.</b> Compress isothermally until the entropy has fallen to that of the saturated liquid, then expand isentropically to the liquid state at 77 K. The work is T<sub>1</sub>(s<sub>1</sub> − s<sub>f</sub>) − (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>), about 769 kJ per kg of nitrogen liquefied.</li><li><b>Linde–Hampson.</b> The isothermal compression removes entropy at 300 K; the counterflow exchanger cools the high-pressure gas along the isobar; the valve then throttles it across a slanting isenthalp into the dome, so only a fraction y comes out as liquid and the rest returns cold. The cold vapour warms along the 1 bar isobar back to the compressor. Raise the pressure and the throttling step becomes longer: more liquid.</li><li><b>Precooled.</b> An external refrigerator takes the high-pressure gas from the warm exchanger temperature down to 230 K, so the gas arrives at the valve colder and more of it condenses. Compare y.</li><li><b>Claude (schematic).</b> A fraction of the flow does work in an expander at 180 K, which is a nearly vertical, nearly isentropic path to a much lower temperature: far more cooling than throttling. The remainder follows the Joule–Thomson path. The numbers here are illustrative; the yield of a real Claude plant is worked out in its own lesson.</li></ul><p>For the Linde–Hampson and precooled cycles the numbers come from the standard energy balance with an ideal exchanger (no temperature difference at the warm end, no pressure drop) and an isothermal compressor: y = (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>2</sub>) / (h<sub>1</sub> − h<sub>f</sub>), with the extra precooler heat added for the precooled case. The <b>figure of merit</b> is the ideal work divided by the actual work per kilogram of liquid. At 200 bar the Linde–Hampson cycle reaches about 12 per cent of the ideal: the rest is lost in throttling, an irreversible step.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>On a T–s diagram, area under a path is heat; the enclosed area of a cycle is work</li><li>A throttle (Joule–Thomson valve) follows an isenthalp and always creates entropy; an expander follows a vertical isentrope</li><li>Higher pressure and precooling both lengthen the cooling step and raise the liquid yield</li><li>The Linde–Hampson cycle is simple but far from ideal; the figure of merit shows how far</li></ul></div>`,
q:"On a T–s diagram, what does the Joule–Thomson valve in a Linde–Hampson liquefier look like?",o:["A slanting line at constant enthalpy, moving to higher entropy as the temperature drops into the dome","A vertical line at constant entropy","A horizontal line at constant temperature"],a:0,e:"Throttling is irreversible, so entropy increases. The gas follows an isenthalp, which slants down and to the right into the two-phase region. An ideal expander would give a vertical line."},
{t:"Advanced: Gas separation and ideal work",lead:"Why unmixing gases costs work, and how much, for air and for any binary mixture.",h:`<h3>Why separate gases at all</h3><p>Air, roughly 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen and 1% argon with traces of helium, neon, krypton and xenon, is the raw material for most industrial gases. Liquid nitrogen precools most cryogenic systems, argon is used in welding and chemistry, and liquid oxygen and hydrogen fuel rockets. Ammonia plants must also separate nitrogen, argon and other inert purge gases at low temperature. <b>Gas separation</b> is the process of splitting a mixture into its components and purifying them.</p><h3>Four common methods</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Principle</th><th>Example from the lecture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Synthetic membrane</td><td>A porous medium passes only certain molecules</td><td>A thin palladium sheet passes hydrogen</td></tr><tr><td>Adsorption</td><td>Certain molecules stick to a surface</td><td>Finely divided nickel adsorbs hydrogen</td></tr><tr><td>Absorption</td><td>A substance is taken into another phase, often chemically</td><td>Sodium hydroxide solution absorbs CO<sub>2</sub> from a stream</td></tr><tr><td>Distillation</td><td>Components differ in volatility (boiling point)</td><td>Cryogenic distillation of liquid air gives O<sub>2</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>, Ar, Ne, Kr and Xe</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For air, one can liquefy first and separate the liquid (cryogenic separation) or separate the gas at room temperature. The lecture lists the advantages of the cryogenic route: the separation work is lower at lower temperature (see below), the boiling points of the components are further apart in the relevant sense, large quantities can be handled, and very high purity is possible.</p><h3>Mixing is irreversible</h3><p>Remove the wall between two gases and they mix on their own. Putting the wall back does not unmix them. Unmixing needs work, so the ideal separator is a thought experiment: a chamber with two frictionless opposing pistons made of semi-permeable membranes, one passing only gas A and the other only gas B. Pushing them reversibly and isothermally at the mixture temperature T<sub>m</sub> separates the gases. The work for a mixture of N constituents with mole fractions y<sub>j</sub> is</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>W<sub>i</sub>/n<sub>m</sub> = ℜ T<sub>m</sub> Σ y<sub>j</sub> ln(1/y<sub>j</sub>)</b></p><p>per mole of mixture, with ℜ = 8.314 J/mol·K. Dividing by a constituent's mole fraction gives the work per mole of that constituent, and dividing by its molar mass gives work per unit mass.</p><h3>Composition first</h3><p>The formulas use mole fractions, so percentages must be converted. For a 60% A, 40% B mixture by weight with molar masses 28 and 32 g/mol, the lecture finds y<sub>A</sub> = 0.631 and y<sub>B</sub> = 0.369. By volume, an ideal gas mixture has volume fraction equal to mole fraction, so y<sub>A</sub> = 0.6 and y<sub>B</sub> = 0.4.</p><h3>Try it</h3><div class=calc><label for=gsp><b>Gas pair</b></label><select id=gsp></select><label for=gsa><b>Mole fraction of A = <span id=gsav>0.80</span></b></label><input type=range id=gsa min=0.01 max=0.99 step=0.01 value=0.8><label for=gst><b>Separation temperature = <span id=gstv>300 K</span></b></label><input type=range id=gst min=80 max=300 step=5 value=300><div class=res id=gso></div><div class=cap id=gsn></div><b>Ideal work per mole of mixture against y<sub>A</sub></b><svg class=sv id=gsc viewBox='0 0 320 170'></svg></div><p>The lecture's nitrogen–oxygen case (80% N<sub>2</sub>, 20% O<sub>2</sub>, 300 K) gives 1248.1 J per mole of mixture, 1560.1 J per mole of N<sub>2</sub> (55.71 J/g) and 6240.5 J per mole of O<sub>2</sub> (195.01 J/g). The work per mole of mixture is always smaller than the work per mole of any one constituent, because the mixture contains all of them.</p><p>Three features follow from the formula. The work is proportional to T<sub>m</sub>, so a cooler separation costs less. For two gases, the work per mole of mixture is greatest for an equal mixture, where it equals ℜT ln 2. And for three gases the maximum comes at y = 1/3 each.</p><h3>Air as a three-gas mixture</h3><p>With 78% N<sub>2</sub>, 21% O<sub>2</sub> and 1% Ar at 300 K, the lecture gets 1415.6 J per mole of air. Spread over each constituent that becomes:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Constituent</th><th>Mole fraction</th><th>Work per mole of that gas (J/mol)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>0.21</td><td>6740.9</td></tr><tr><td>Argon</td><td>0.01</td><td>141,560</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Argon costs about twenty times more per mole than oxygen simply because it is so scarce: the whole separation effort is divided among very few argon molecules. The lecture also separates the cases where all three gases are isolated and where only one gas is pulled out, leaving the other two mixed.</p>`,
q:"Why is the ideal work per mole of argon in air so much larger than per mole of oxygen?",o:["Argon molecules are heavier","Argon is only about 1% of the mixture, so the work of the whole separation is shared among very few moles of argon","Argon has a higher boiling point than oxygen"],a:1,e:"Work per mole of a constituent is the work per mole of mixture divided by that constituent's mole fraction. A mole fraction of 0.01 gives a factor of 100."},
{t:"Advanced: Mixtures, phase diagrams and K-values",lead:"The Gibbs phase rule, dew and bubble lines, Raoult's law and how to read a vapour–liquid equilibrium.",h:`<h3>How many properties fix the state</h3><p>The <b>Gibbs phase rule</b>, formulated by J. Willard Gibbs in 1878, gives the number of independent intensive properties F needed to fix a mixture in equilibrium, from the number of components C and phases P:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>F = C − P + 2</b></p><p>Single-phase nitrogen has C = 1, P = 1 and so F = 2: pressure and temperature fix everything else. Two-phase nitrogen has F = 1, so the saturation pressure is set by the temperature alone. A single-phase two-gas mixture has F = 3 (pressure, temperature and composition). A boiling mixture of liquid air is C = 2, P = 2 and so F = 2: at a given pressure, temperature and composition are tied together.</p><h3>The temperature–composition diagram</h3><p>At constant pressure, plot temperature against the mole fraction of one component. Two curves appear. The upper one is the <b>dew line</b>, where vapour starts to condense. The lower is the <b>bubble line</b>, where liquid starts to boil. Between them lies a two-phase region. For nitrogen and oxygen at 1 atm, above about 90 K the mixture is all vapour, and below about 77 K it is all liquid.</p><p>Cool a vapour of composition 1 down to the dew line and the first drop forms. Its composition is read along the horizontal tie line to the bubble line, and it is richer in oxygen, the higher-boiling component. Cool further and more liquid forms, while the remaining vapour grows richer in nitrogen. <b>Condensation or boiling of a mixture is therefore not isothermal.</b> Repeating this enrichment is the idea behind rectification. The further apart the boiling points, the more complete the split in one step. Mixtures that behave like a single substance at some composition, called <b>azeotropes</b>, cannot be separated this way, though cryogens rarely show them.</p><h3>Three laws</h3><ul><li><b>Dalton's law</b> (1801): in a mixture of non-reacting ideal gases, the partial pressure is p<sub>j</sub> = p<sub>tot</sub> y<sub>j</sub>, and the partial pressures sum to the total.</li><li><b>Raoult's law</b> (1882): over an ideal liquid mixture, the partial pressure of component j is p<sub>j</sub> = π<sub>j</sub> x<sub>j</sub>, where π<sub>j</sub> is its pure-component vapour pressure and x<sub>j</sub> its liquid mole fraction.</li><li><b>Gibbs–Dalton law</b>: apply Dalton's law to the vapour above the liquid, so p<sub>j</sub> = y<sub>j</sub> p<sub>tot</sub>.</li></ul><p>Combine the last two and the <b>distribution coefficient</b> appears:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>K<sub>j</sub> = y<sub>j</sub> / x<sub>j</sub> = π<sub>j</sub> / p<sub>tot</sub></b></p><p>It is defined only where two phases coexist. K is above 1 for the more volatile component (nitrogen here) and below 1 for the less volatile one, and it approaches 1 at that component's own boiling point. For a two-component mixture, requiring both y's and both x's to sum to 1 gives the liquid composition directly:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>x<sub>A</sub> = (1 − K<sub>B</sub>) / (K<sub>A</sub> − K<sub>B</sub>),  y<sub>A</sub> = K<sub>A</sub> x<sub>A</sub></b></p><p>For real mixtures K is tabulated from experiment as ln(K p<sub>mix</sub>/p<sub>0</sub>) with p<sub>0</sub> = 1 atm. Worked case from the lecture: nitrogen–oxygen at 5 atm and 100 K. The table gives 2.004 for nitrogen and 1.042 for oxygen, so K<sub>N2</sub> = e<sup>2.004</sup>/5 = 1.483 and K<sub>O2</sub> = e<sup>1.042</sup>/5 = 0.567. Then x<sub>N2</sub> = (1 − 0.567)/(1.483 − 0.567) = 0.472, and the vapour holds y<sub>N2</sub> = 1.483 × 0.472 = 0.70.</p><h3>Explore the nitrogen–oxygen diagram at 1 atm</h3><p>The calculator interpolates the lecture's 1 atm K-value table for 78 to 90 K, using straight lines between table points, and extends the lines slightly at the ends. Set the temperature and the overall composition and read off the liquid and vapour compositions and how much of each there is (the lever rule).</p><div class=calc><label for=pdt><b>Temperature = <span id=pdv>84.0 K</span></b></label><input type=range id=pdt min=77.5 max=90.5 step=0.1 value=84><label for=pdz><b>Overall N₂ mole fraction = <span id=pzv>0.50</span></b></label><input type=range id=pdz min=0.05 max=0.95 step=0.01 value=0.5><div class=res id=pdo></div><div class=cap id=pdn></div><svg class=sv id=pdc viewBox='0 0 320 200' role=img aria-label='Temperature composition diagram for nitrogen and oxygen at 1 atm'></svg></div><p>Blue curve: bubble line (liquid). Green curve: dew line (vapour). The horizontal tie line joins the two phase compositions at the chosen temperature, and the dot is the overall mixture. The fraction of vapour is (z − x)/(y − x).</p>`,
q:"For a two-phase nitrogen–oxygen mixture, what does a distribution coefficient K below 1 mean for oxygen?",o:["Oxygen is richer in the vapour than in the liquid","Oxygen is richer in the liquid than in the vapour, because it is the less volatile component","Oxygen cannot be present in the mixture"],a:1,e:"K = y/x. A value below 1 means the vapour holds less of that component than the liquid does, which is the case for the higher-boiling oxygen."},
{t:"Advanced: Rectification columns and plate calculations",lead:"How a distillation column makes pure nitrogen and oxygen, and how to count the plates it needs.",h:`<h3>Inside a rectification column</h3><p>A <b>rectification column</b> is a vertical, well-insulated vessel, often spherical for the 1 to 5 atm used in cryogenic plants. A condenser sits in the top dome and a boiler (reboiler) at the bottom. Inside are horizontal <b>plates</b> with holes, through which vapour rises in small bubbles, and <b>downcomers</b>, through which liquid falls from plate to plate. The two-phase feed enters part way up.</p><p>On every plate the rising vapour and the falling liquid meet and exchange heat and matter. The vapour cools slightly and loses some oxygen, the liquid warms slightly and loses some nitrogen. Repeating this up the column enriches the vapour in the low-boiling component (nitrogen) and the liquid in the high-boiling one (oxygen). Heat is removed at the top (the condenser load Q<sub>D</sub>) to make liquid reflux, and added at the bottom (Q<sub>B</sub>) to evaporate it. Infinite plates would give 100% pure products, so size and cost set the achievable purity.</p><h3>Ideal plates and Murphree efficiency</h3><p>On an ideal plate the vapour and liquid leaving are in equilibrium, which can be traced on the temperature–composition diagram with isotherms and constant-composition lines. A real plate falls short. The <b>Murphree efficiency</b> compares the real change in vapour composition with the ideal one:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>η<sub>M</sub> = (y<sub>j</sub> − y<sub>j−1</sub>) / (y<sub>0,j</sub> − y<sub>j−1</sub>)</b></p><p>Here y<sub>j−1</sub> is the vapour entering plate j from below, y<sub>j</sub> the vapour actually leaving, and y<sub>0,j</sub> the vapour that would leave at equilibrium. The lecture's examples at 1 atm give η<sub>M</sub> = (0.84 − 0.5)/(0.85 − 0.5) = 0.97, and at 2 atm 0.843. High efficiency needs small bubbles, long contact time and high heat and mass transfer coefficients.</p><h3>The McCabe–Thiele method</h3><p>Plate counts can be found by the Ponchon–Savarit method (general, uses enthalpy–composition diagrams), by numerical methods, or by the graphical <b>McCabe–Thiele method</b> of 1925. It is less general but easy to follow. It assumes the molar flows of liquid and vapour stay constant along each section, which corresponds to flat enthalpy lines, so the liquid and vapour flow rates L and V are constant in each section.</p><p>With D the distillate, B the bottoms, and x<sub>D</sub> and x<sub>B</sub> their mole fractions of the light component, mole and energy balances on the enriching (upper) and stripping (lower) sections give two straight operating lines:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>Enriching: y<sub>n</sub> = (L<sub>n+1</sub>/V<sub>n</sub>) x<sub>n+1</sub> + (D/V<sub>n</sub>) x<sub>D</sub></b><br><b>Stripping: y<sub>m</sub> = (L<sub>m+1</sub>/V<sub>m</sub>) x<sub>m+1</sub> − (B/V<sub>m</sub>) x<sub>B</sub></b></p><p>Each passes through the diagonal y = x at its product composition. L<sub>n+1</sub>/V<sub>n</sub> = 1 − D/V<sub>n</sub> is called the internal reflux ratio. The feed condition sets the <b>q line</b>, y = qx/(q − 1) − x<sub>F</sub>/(q − 1), where q is the liquid fraction of the feed. Saturated vapour feed has q = 0 (horizontal line), saturated liquid q = 1 (vertical), a two-phase feed 0 &lt; q &lt; 1, subcooled liquid q &gt; 1 and superheated vapour q &lt; 0. The operating lines meet on the q line.</p><p>To find the plates, start at the top product (x<sub>D</sub>, x<sub>D</sub>) on the diagonal and step: horizontally to the equilibrium curve (the liquid leaving a plate is in equilibrium with the vapour leaving it), then vertically to the operating line (that gives the vapour rising from the next plate). Switch to the stripping line once past the feed. Continue until x falls to x<sub>B</sub>. Each horizontal run is one equilibrium stage.</p><h3>The lecture's worked column</h3><p>Nitrogen–oxygen at 1 atm. Feed 50% N<sub>2</sub>, q = 0.7. Top product 97% N<sub>2</sub>, bottom product 5% N<sub>2</sub> (95% O<sub>2</sub>) at B = 20 mol/s, condenser load 500 kW.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Feed and distillate flows</td><td>F = 39.14 mol/s, D = 19.14 mol/s</td></tr><tr><td>Feed enthalpy h<sub>F</sub> = q h + (1 − q) H</td><td>2856.4 J/mol</td></tr><tr><td>Boiler duty Q<sub>B</sub></td><td>430.6 kW</td></tr><tr><td>Enriching line</td><td>y = 0.773 x + 0.22</td></tr><tr><td>Stripping line</td><td>y = 1.274 x − 0.013</td></tr><tr><td>q line</td><td>y = −2.34 x + 1.67</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The lecture steps this out in a spreadsheet and counts 9 vertical lines, split as 3 in the enriching and 6 in the stripping section, plus the condenser and boiler. Try the same column below.</p><div class=calc><div class=two><div><label for=mtd><b>Top purity x<sub>D</sub> = <span id=mdv>0.970</span></b></label><input type=range id=mtd min=0.8 max=0.999 step=0.001 value=0.97></div><div><label for=mtb><b>Bottom x<sub>B</sub> = <span id=mbv>0.050</span></b></label><input type=range id=mtb min=0.005 max=0.3 step=0.005 value=0.05></div></div><div class=two><div><label for=mtf><b>Feed x<sub>F</sub> = <span id=mfv>0.50</span></b></label><input type=range id=mtf min=0.2 max=0.8 step=0.01 value=0.5></div><div><label for=mtq><b>Liquid fraction q = <span id=mqv>0.70</span></b></label><input type=range id=mtq min=0 max=1.2 step=0.05 value=0.7></div></div><label for=mtr><b>Internal reflux ratio L/V = <span id=mrv>0.773</span></b></label><input type=range id=mtr min=0.5 max=0.99 step=0.001 value=0.773><div class=res id=mto></div><div class=cap id=mtn></div><div class=two><svg class=sv id=mtcol viewBox='0 0 300 350' role=img aria-label='Column drawn with the number of plates the calculator finds'></svg><svg class=sv id=mtc viewBox='0 0 320 320' role=img aria-label='McCabe-Thiele diagram for nitrogen and oxygen'></svg></div><div class=cap>Blue: equilibrium curve. Dashed: diagonal. Green: enriching and stripping operating lines. Red: q line. Black staircase: the plates. The column on the left is drawn with the number of stages the staircase finds: bar length shows the nitrogen fraction of the liquid on each plate, blue bubbles rise, amber liquid falls.</div></div><p>This page builds the equilibrium curve from the lecture's 1 atm K-value table and gets 6 stages for the lecture's settings. The lecture's spreadsheet, which uses an equilibrium curve read from its temperature–composition diagram, gets 9. A stage count is very sensitive to the curve, so treat both as illustrative of the method.</p><h3>Reflux: the central trade-off</h3><ul><li><b>Total reflux.</b> All vapour from the top is condensed and returned (L/V = 1). The operating lines lie on the diagonal and the column needs the fewest plates, but no product comes out.</li><li><b>Minimum reflux.</b> As reflux falls the operating lines move toward the equilibrium curve, the plate count rises, and it grows without limit when the operating lines meet the curve. The lecture also notes the lines cannot cross it, since that would violate mass conservation. Condenser load and refrigeration cost are lowest here, and a finite product flows.</li><li><b>In practice</b> a compromise is used: 1.15 to 1.35 times the minimum reflux in ordinary distillation, and 1.05 to 1.15 times in cryogenic columns, where refrigeration is expensive.</li></ul><p>Asking for higher top purity or lower bottom impurity adds plates, and the feed condition q fixes the slope of the q line and so the approximate feed position. Because a change in D, Q<sub>D</sub>, B or Q<sub>B</sub> alters the others through the mass and energy balances, the design variables cannot be adjusted independently.</p>`,
q:"What happens to the number of plates and the product flow as a column approaches total reflux?",o:["Plates are at a minimum and product flow falls to zero","Plates are at a maximum and product flow is greatest","Plates are at a minimum and product flow is greatest"],a:0,e:"At total reflux L/V = 1, the operating lines coincide with the diagonal and need the fewest plates, but everything is returned to the column, so no product leaves."},
{t:"Advanced: Air separation and distillation in motion",lead:"See how a double column turns compressed air into nitrogen, oxygen and argon, and step off the plates of a binary column to see how many are needed.",h:`<p>Almost all the nitrogen, oxygen and argon in the world is made by cryogenic distillation of liquid air. This lesson animates the two ideas behind it: the <b>double column</b> that links two columns at different pressures, and the <b>plate-by-plate stepping</b> that tells you how tall a column must be. This lesson is general process engineering, not taken from the lectures; the pressures and the layout are the common textbook arrangement, simplified.</p><h3>The double-column air separation unit</h3><p>Air is compressed to about 6 bar, cleaned of water vapour and carbon dioxide (which would freeze), and cooled in a large counterflow heat exchanger by the cold products leaving the plant. It enters the bottom of the <b>high-pressure (HP) column</b>. Rising vapour meets falling liquid on the plates: the vapour grows richer in nitrogen, the liquid richer in oxygen. At the top of the HP column, nearly pure nitrogen vapour reaches the <b>condenser–reboiler</b>, where it is condensed against boiling oxygen from the <b>low-pressure (LP) column</b> above, which works at about 1.3 bar. This is the trick: nitrogen at the higher pressure condenses at a <b>higher temperature</b> than oxygen boils at the lower pressure, so the condensing nitrogen provides the heat to boil the oxygen, and the same unit serves as reflux source for one column and as reboiler for the other. Part of the condensed nitrogen returns to the HP column as reflux, part goes to the top of the LP column. The oxygen-rich liquid from the bottom of the HP column is fed to the middle of the LP column. Gaseous nitrogen leaves the top of the LP column and oxygen the bottom; argon, whose boiling point (87.3 K) lies between those of nitrogen (77.4 K) and oxygen (90.2 K) at 1 atm, builds up in the lower middle of the LP column, and a side draw feeds a smaller column that produces argon.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=dcf viewBox='0 0 640 380' role=img aria-label='Animated double-column air separation unit with flows of air, nitrogen, oxygen, argon and liquids'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=dcs><b>Follow</b></label><select id=dcs><option value=all>All streams</option><option value=air>Air</option><option value=n2>Nitrogen</option><option value=o2>Oxygen</option><option value=ar>Argon</option></select></div><div class=res id=dco></div></div><div class=cap id=dcn></div></div><h3>How tall must a column be? The McCabe–Thiele method</h3><p>On every ideal plate the vapour leaving is in equilibrium with the liquid leaving. For a binary mixture of a light component (nitrogen) and a heavy one (oxygen), the equilibrium curve for a constant <b>relative volatility</b> α is y* = α x / (1 + (α − 1) x), where x and y are the mole fractions of the light component in the liquid and the vapour. Between plates, the compositions of passing streams lie on <b>operating lines</b>: above the feed, y = R/(R+1) x + x<sub>D</sub>/(R+1), where R is the <b>reflux ratio</b> and x<sub>D</sub> the top product; below the feed the line runs from the point (x<sub>B</sub>, x<sub>B</sub>) at the bottom to its meeting with the first line at x = x<sub>F</sub>. Stepping between the operating lines and the equilibrium curve counts the plates. The animation does it for you, one plate at a time, and the column on the right colours each plate by the liquid composition on it. The feed is taken as saturated liquid, and the model assumes constant molar flow (a standard simplification). It treats air as a binary mixture of 79 per cent nitrogen and 21 per cent oxygen.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=spf viewBox='0 0 640 340' role=img aria-label='McCabe-Thiele diagram being stepped plate by plate, with a column drawing whose plates are coloured by composition'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=spa><b>Relative volatility α = <span id=spav>3.3</span></b></label><input type=range id=spa min=1.5 max=6 step=0.05 value=3.3><label for=spd><b>Top product, nitrogen purity = <span id=spdv>0.99</span></b></label><input type=range id=spd min=0.9 max=0.9999 step=0.0005 value=0.99><label for=spb><b>Bottom product, nitrogen left = <span id=spbv>0.02</span></b></label><input type=range id=spb min=0.001 max=0.2 step=0.001 value=0.02><label for=spr><b>Reflux = <span id=sprv>1.5</span> × the minimum</b></label><input type=range id=spr min=1.05 max=4 step=0.05 value=1.5><div><button class=btn id=spg>Replay</button></div></div><div class=res id=spo></div></div><div class=cap id=spn></div></div><p>Try it. Raise the purity of the top product and the plate count grows quickly. Lower the reflux towards the minimum, the least that could ever work, and the staircase squeezes against the pinch point where the operating line meets the equilibrium curve: the number of plates rises steeply. Lower α (components that are harder to separate) and the staircase gets shorter steps, so more plates are needed. This is the trade between the capital cost of a taller column and the running cost of more reflux, and it is the central design choice. The minimum number of plates, reached at total reflux, is given by the Fenske equation, N<sub>min</sub> = ln[ (x<sub>D</sub>/(1 − x<sub>D</sub>)) ((1 − x<sub>B</sub>)/x<sub>B</sub>) ] / ln α.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>A double column couples a high-pressure and a low-pressure column through one condenser–reboiler</li><li>Nitrogen condensing at 6 bar boils oxygen at 1.3 bar: the same exchanger is condenser and reboiler</li><li>The McCabe–Thiele staircase counts ideal plates between the operating lines and the equilibrium curve</li><li>More reflux means fewer plates but more energy; the minimum reflux needs infinitely many plates</li></ul></div>`,
q:"Why can the nitrogen condensing at the top of the HP column boil the oxygen at the bottom of the LP column?",o:["Nitrogen at the higher pressure condenses at a higher temperature than oxygen boils at the lower pressure","Nitrogen is hotter than oxygen at any pressure","The condenser is warmed from outside"],a:0,e:"The boiling temperature rises with pressure. Nitrogen at about 6 bar condenses at a temperature above that of oxygen boiling at about 1.3 bar, so heat flows from the condensing nitrogen to the boiling oxygen."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryocooler fundamentals and the Stirling cycle",lead:"Closed-cycle machines that replace liquid cryogens, and the ideal and real Stirling refrigerator.",h:`<h3>What a cryocooler is</h3><p>A <b>cryocooler</b> is a mechanical refrigerator that makes low temperature by compressing and expanding a gas in a <b>closed cycle</b>: the mass of working gas stays constant. It has a compressor, a heat exchanger and an expander, and the cold made in the expander is passed to the object to be cooled through a cold-end heat exchanger (an evaporator). Machines that reach 77 K or 4.2 K can replace liquid nitrogen or liquid helium, which is attractive because they need no cryogen supply, run reliably with little maintenance, and cryogen prices are rising while cryocooler prices are falling.</p><p>Practical requirements depend on the use but usually include low weight and volume, quick cool-down, low vibration, long mean time between failures, little sensitivity to orientation and noise, and cost-effectiveness. The numbers that decide the choice are the cooling power at a given temperature, the compressor power, the cooling water and servicing needs, vibration level and cost.</p><h3>Classification</h3><p>Cryogenic refrigeration can be open cycle (stored gas, stored cryogen) or closed cycle. The closed-cycle machines split by how they exchange heat:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Heat exchanger type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Cryocoolers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Recuperative</b></td><td>Two streams flow steadily and at the same time, separated by a solid wall (counter, cross or parallel flow)</td><td>Joule–Thomson, Brayton, Claude</td></tr><tr><td><b>Regenerative</b></td><td>One matrix stores heat from the warm gas, then gives it to the cold gas in alternate periods, so the flow is oscillating and the heat transfer is indirect</td><td>Stirling, Gifford–McMahon, pulse tube</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In the J–T cryocooler a reciprocating compressor feeds a counterflow exchanger and an isenthalpic valve, so the gas must start below its inversion temperature. The Brayton cooler is similar but expands the gas in a work-producing expander. Regenerative machines need only a periodic pressure oscillation and a regenerator, which makes them compact.</p><h3>The ideal Stirling cycle</h3><p>Robert Stirling conceived the cycle in 1815 as an engine; John Herschel suggested using it as a cooler in 1834, Alexander Kirk built one in 1861, and Jan Koehler made the first commercial machine in 1956 and liquefied nitrogen with it in 1965. The ideal refrigeration cycle has four processes on a p–V diagram:</p><ul><li><b>1 → 2</b> isothermal compression at the warm temperature T<sub>C</sub>; heat is rejected.</li><li><b>2 → 3</b> constant-volume cooling; the regenerator absorbs heat from the gas.</li><li><b>3 → 4</b> isothermal expansion at the cold temperature T<sub>E</sub>; the gas absorbs heat Q<sub>E</sub>, the useful cooling.</li><li><b>4 → 1</b> constant-volume heating; the regenerator gives its heat back.</li></ul><p>With a perfect regenerator the two constant-volume steps cancel, and the COP equals that of a Carnot refrigerator, COP = T<sub>E</sub> / (T<sub>C</sub> − T<sub>E</sub>). Scrub or play the cycle:</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=stv viewBox='0 0 320 220' role=img aria-label='Ideal Stirling cycle on a pressure volume diagram'></svg><button class=g id=csb style='margin-top:8px;padding:6px 14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--accent);background:transparent;color:var(--accent);font:inherit;cursor:pointer'>Pause</button><label for=csph>Position in the cycle</label><input type=range id=csph min=0 max=399 step=1 value=0><div class=cap id=cst></div></div><h3>Real machines</h3><p>An actual Stirling cooler has continuous (harmonic) piston motion instead of the discontinuous ideal one, dead volume, an imperfect regenerator, and non-isothermal compression and expansion. The geometry can be <b>α-type</b> (two pistons, a compression piston and an expansion piston, whose motion is out of phase by an angle α), <b>β-type</b> (a displacer and power piston in one cylinder) or <b>γ-type</b> (a displacer in one cylinder and a piston in another, with a variable compression space). Typical features of Stirling coolers: high efficiency, small size and weight, dry or no lubrication, and high reliability, but vibration from the moving displacer. Uses include infrared sensors, satellites, high-temperature superconductors and the liquefaction of nitrogen, air and hydrogen.</p><h3>Watch a real α-type machine</h3><p>Here is an α-type cooler: a warm compression piston on the left, a regenerator in the middle and a cold expansion piston on the right, with helium-like gas shuttling between them. Gas colour shows where it is warm or cold, and its depth of shade shows pressure. The loops on the right are the machine's p–V cycles from Schmidt's isothermal model (k = 2.85, T<sub>C</sub>/T<sub>E</sub> = 300/77.2, dead volume X = 1). The area of the blue loop is the cooling per cycle. Move the phase angle α and watch the blue loop open and close.</p><div class=fig><div class=two><svg class=sv id=stm viewBox='0 0 420 262' role=img aria-label='Animated α-type Stirling cooler'></svg><svg class=sv id=stl viewBox='0 0 300 262' role=img aria-label='Pressure volume loops of the compression and expansion spaces'></svg></div><button class=btn id=smb>Pause</button><label for=smp>Crank angle</label><input type=range id=smp min=0 max=359 step=1 value=0><label for=sma><b>Phase angle α = <span id=smav>90</span>°</b></label><input type=range id=sma min=0 max=180 step=1 value=90><div class=res id=smo></div><div class=cap id=smn></div></div><h3>Schmidt's analysis and sizing</h3><p>In 1861 Gustav Schmidt gave an analysis of a more realistic cycle that assumes perfect isothermal compression and expansion, harmonic piston motion and perfect regeneration. It is a first guess of dimensions. Its non-dimensional design parameters are the swept volume ratio k = V<sub>C</sub>/V<sub>E</sub>, the temperature ratio τ = T<sub>C</sub>/T<sub>E</sub>, the dead volume ratio X = V<sub>D</sub>/V<sub>E</sub>, and the phase angle α; the operating parameters are speed N and maximum pressure p<sub>max</sub>. Results are non-dimensionalised as Q<sub>max</sub> = Q<sub>E</sub>/(p<sub>max</sub>V<sub>T</sub>), with V<sub>T</sub> the total swept volume. In 1962 Graham Walker combined the effects into optimisation charts. Real losses (regenerator ineffectiveness, pressure drop, solid and shuttle conduction, drive losses) mean Q<sub>net</sub> = Q<sub>E</sub> − Σ losses and W<sub>total</sub> = W<sub>T</sub> + Σ losses. The lecture's rule is to design for three times the required cooling, Q<sub>E,design</sub> = 3 Q<sub>E,req</sub>.</p><p>The lecture sizes an α-type helium Stirling nitrogen liquefier (T<sub>E</sub> = 77.2 K, T<sub>C</sub> = 300 K) and reads k = 2.85, α = 0.575 (quoted as 32.9°) and Q<sub>max</sub> = 0.07 off Walker's chart for X = 1. The watch-it-run figure above suggests the optimum phase angle for this k and X is near 90° to 100°, where the model also gives Q<sub>max</sub> ≈ 0.07, so 0.575 may be in units of π radians (about 103°) rather than radians. Treat the angle with caution; the volumes below do not depend on it. Change the capacity, pressure and speed and see the resulting dimensions. The chart values are held fixed, so they only hold near these conditions.</p><div class=calc><label for=sdl><b>Capacity = <span id=sdlv>10</span> litres of LN₂ per hour</b></label><input type=range id=sdl min=1 max=50 step=1 value=10><label for=sdp><b>Maximum pressure = <span id=sdpv>40</span> bar</b></label><input type=range id=sdp min=10 max=60 step=1 value=40><label for=sdn><b>Speed = <span id=sdnv>1440</span> rpm</b></label><input type=range id=sdn min=600 max=3000 step=60 value=1440><div class=res id=sdo></div><div class=cap id=sdq></div></div><p>With the lecture's inputs the result is about 965 W required, 2895 W design, V<sub>T</sub> ≈ 430 cm³, compression cylinder diameter 81.4 mm and stroke 60.8 mm, expansion cylinder 57.5 mm by 43.1 mm, using a stroke-to-bore ratio of 0.75. For a very large design the lecture suggests using two or more cylinders, or working near the maximum, with phase angles of roughly 60° to 120°.</p>`,
q:"In a Stirling cooler with a perfect regenerator, what is the COP of the ideal cycle?",o:["Zero, because the regenerator wastes heat","Equal to the Carnot COP, T_E / (T_C − T_E)","Always greater than the Carnot COP"],a:1,e:"With perfect regeneration the two constant-volume steps cancel, leaving two isothermal steps, so the ideal Stirling refrigerator reaches Carnot efficiency."},
{t:"Advanced: Gifford–McMahon and pulse tube coolers",lead:"Valves instead of a direct drive, and a cooler with no moving part at the cold end.",h:`<h3>Gifford–McMahon (GM)</h3><p>W. E. Gifford and H. O. McMahon introduced valves into a regenerative cooler in 1950. A compressor feeds high- and low-pressure lines, and valves open and close in sequence to produce the pressure pulse. A displacer containing the regenerator moves between a warm space and a cold space. The valve timing relative to the displacer position is vital. Commercial machines use rotary valves, but designs with poppet or solenoid valves also exist.</p><p>In outline: with the high-pressure valve open the gas fills the system; the displacer then moves so that gas passes through the regenerator and is cooled; the low-pressure valve opens and the gas expands in the cold space, absorbing heat; finally the displacer pushes the cold gas back through the regenerator at constant pressure, cooling it. The regenerator material sets the range: a single stage with stainless steel mesh reaches about 30 K; two stages reach about 10 K, and about 4.2 K with lead in the regenerator.</p><h3>Stirling against GM</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Stirling</th><th>Gifford–McMahon</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Frequency</td><td>20 to 150 Hz</td><td>1 to 5 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Compressor connection</td><td>Direct to the expander</td><td>Through valves</td></tr><tr><td>Compressor</td><td>Dry</td><td>Oil-lubricated</td></tr><tr><td>Efficiency</td><td>High COP</td><td>Lower COP</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure ratio</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Two-stage reach</td><td>about 20 K</td><td>about 4 K</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Compact, can be miniaturised</td><td>Bulky, high-power compressors</td></tr><tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Space applications</td><td>Mostly land-based</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>The pulse tube cooler</h3><p>Moving displacers wear, vibrate and need rubbing seals at low temperature. The <b>pulse tube cooler</b> removes the cold displacer altogether: an oscillating gas flow in a thin-walled tube does the job. Its parts are the compressor, an aftercooler, the regenerator, a cold-end heat exchanger, the pulse tube, a hot-end heat exchanger and a <b>phase-shift mechanism</b>.</p><p>When the compressor pressurises the system, high-pressure gas flows through the regenerator, where it is precooled, into the pulse tube and compresses the gas already at the top, which warms. That heat is rejected at the hot end, held at ambient by water. During depressurisation the gas in the tube expands and cools, and the cold gas returning through the regenerator precools the next incoming gas. The cooling effect is lifted at the cold-end heat exchanger. Because no mechanical drive moves the gas in the tube, vibration is lower than in Stirling and GM machines.</p><p>The advantages: no moving parts at the cold end, no low-temperature seals, long life. The drawbacks the lecture lists: orientation effects, and for orifice types, a small orifice that can clog.</p><h3>Pulse tube classification</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>By</th><th>Types</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Drive</td><td>Stirling type (high frequency), GM type (low frequency, valves)</td><td>Like the parent machines</td></tr><tr><td>Geometry</td><td>Inline, U-type, coaxial, annular</td><td>Inline has no change of flow direction and the least pressure loss, so the best performance, but it is the least compact. Coaxial puts the cold end at the centre for compactness</td></tr><tr><td>Phase shift</td><td>Basic, orifice, inertance tube, double inlet valve</td><td>Sets the timing between pressure and mass flow</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Why the phase matters: phasor analysis</h3><p>The lecture analyses the orifice pulse tube for a monatomic gas, assuming sinusoidal pressure and temperature in the tube and adiabatic gas behaviour. The mass flow at the cold end is the sum of two vectors, and it lies at a phase angle to the pressure. With perfect regeneration, the heat lifted at the cold end is the time-averaged enthalpy flow through the pulse tube, which depends on that phase angle and on the pressure amplitude. A pulse tube cooler therefore needs a phase-shift mechanism to put the mass flow and pressure in step: an orifice, a longer inertance tube, or a double-inlet valve. The lecture later maps the system onto an electrical analogy.</p>`,
q:"Which cooler has no moving part at its cold end, and what keeps its gas flow properly timed?",o:["Gifford–McMahon; a rotary valve","Pulse tube; a phase-shift mechanism such as an orifice or inertance tube","Stirling; a flexure bearing"],a:1,e:"The pulse tube cooler replaces the displacer with an oscillating gas column, and a phase-shift mechanism sets the timing between the pressure wave and the mass flow."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryocoolers in motion",lead:"Watch the displacer, the valves and the gas parcels of the Gifford–McMahon, pulse tube and recuperative coolers, and see why each one makes cold.",h:`<h3>One pressure wave, four strokes</h3><p>A regenerative cooler needs only two things: a gas whose pressure rises and falls, and a regenerator that stores heat between strokes. In the <b>Gifford–McMahon (GM)</b> machine the pressure wave is made by two valves connecting the cold head to a compressor's high- and low-pressure lines, and a <b>displacer</b> that holds the regenerator moves the gas between a warm space at the top and a cold space at the bottom. One turn of the cycle has four steps:</p><ol><li><b>Pressurise.</b> The displacer sits at the cold end. The high-pressure valve opens and gas flows into the warm space. It heats as it is compressed and rejects that heat at the warm flange.</li><li><b>Cool at high pressure.</b> The displacer rises. The gas flows down through the regenerator into the cold space, giving its heat to the regenerator material on the way.</li><li><b>Expand.</b> The high-pressure valve closes and the low-pressure valve opens. The gas in the cold space expands and drops in temperature, and it absorbs heat from the load at the cold tip. This is the useful cooling.</li><li><b>Return at low pressure.</b> The displacer moves down and pushes the cold gas back up through the regenerator, which it cools for the next cycle, before the gas returns to the compressor.</li></ol><p>Use the tabs below to move between the three machines. Press Pause and drag the cycle slider to freeze any moment, and watch the valves, the displacer, the gas colour and the pressure marker together. The compressor's work heats and cools the gas through the pressure ratio; the regenerator carries the cold forward from stroke to stroke.</p><div class=fig><div><button class=btn id=ccb0>Gifford–McMahon</button> <button class=btn id=ccb1>Pulse tube</button> <button class=btn id=ccb2>Recuperative (J–T, Brayton)</button> <button class=btn id=ccg>Pause</button></div><svg class=sv id=ccf viewBox='0 0 560 320' role=img aria-label='Animated diagram of a cryocooler cold head with moving gas and a displacer or gas parcels'></svg><div class=two><svg class=sv id=ccc viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='Pressure against cycle phase for the selected cooler'></svg><div><label for=ccp><b>Cycle position</b></label><input type=range id=ccp min=0 max=999 step=1 value=0><label for=ccr><b>Pressure ratio = <span id=ccrv>2.5</span></b></label><input type=range id=ccr min=1.3 max=4 step=0.1 value=2.5><label for=ccs><b>Animation speed = <span id=ccsv>1.0</span>×</b></label><input type=range id=ccs min=0.25 max=2 step=0.05 value=1><div id=ccmw><label for=ccm><b>Expansion device</b></label><select id=ccm><option value=0>J–T valve</option><option value=1>Expansion turbine (Brayton)</option></select></div><div class=res id=cco></div></div></div><div class=cap id=ccn></div></div><h3>Why the regenerator is the heart of it</h3><p>In the GM cooler the regenerator is a porous matrix of stainless-steel mesh, or lead at the lowest temperatures, that absorbs heat from the downward-moving gas and gives it back to the upward-moving gas. It must have a large surface and a heat capacity far above that of the gas. The material sets the range: a single stage with stainless-steel mesh reaches about 30 K, two stages reach about 10 K, and a lead regenerator gets down to about 4.2 K. Regenerator performance is a major limit on how cold and how powerful the cooler can be.</p><h3>A cooler with no cold moving part</h3><p>The <b>pulse tube</b> cooler removes the displacer entirely. The gas itself acts as the piston. The pulse tube is a thin empty tube between a cold-end heat exchanger and a hot-end one; gas parcels in the middle of it never leave it. When the compressor raises the pressure, high-pressure gas flows through the regenerator, is pre-cooled, and pushes into the tube, compressing the gas already there. That gas warms, and the heat is rejected at the hot end, which is held at ambient by water. When the pressure falls the gas in the tube expands and cools, and it flows back toward the cold end, where it absorbs heat. The pulse-tube animation draws each gas parcel in a colour that follows its temperature: parcels near the hot end are red at high pressure, and parcels near the cold end are blue at low pressure.</p><p>What makes this work is the <b>phase shift</b> between pressure and flow in the tube. In the simplest design an orifice and a reservoir at the hot end make the gas in the tube lag behind the pressure, so the gas arrives at the cold end at the right moment to absorb heat. Without it, no net cooling is produced. The advantages are no moving parts at the cold end, no low-temperature seals and lower vibration.</p><h3>Steady flow: J–T and Brayton coolers</h3><p>The third tab shows a different family: <b>recuperative</b> coolers, in which two streams flow steadily in opposite directions through a counterflow heat exchanger separated by a solid wall. There is no pressure wave. A compressor feeds the high-pressure stream through the exchanger, and an expansion device drops its pressure and temperature. The returning cold low-pressure stream cools the incoming gas, so the cold builds up from the compressor end to the cold end. If the expansion device is a throttle valve, the machine is a <b>Joule–Thomson cooler</b>, and the gas must start below its inversion temperature. If it is a work-producing turbine, the machine is a <b>Brayton cooler</b>, which cools any gas and recovers some work. Unlike the closed liquefier loops earlier in the course, a cryocooler of this kind has no liquid product: the cold stage absorbs a heat load and the gas goes back to the compressor.</p><h3>Pressure ratio and the temperature swing</h3><p>The pressure ratio sets how much the gas warms and cools. For an ideal adiabatic change in helium, T varies as p<sup>0.4</sup>, so at a ratio of 2.5 the gas swings from about 208 K to 432 K starting from 300 K. In a real cooler the regenerator pre-cools the gas before it expands, so the cold end reaches far below what a single expansion from room temperature could. Frequency matters as well. From the lecture table, Stirling coolers run at 20 to 150 Hz, compact and with a dry compressor, while GM coolers run at 1 to 5 Hz with an oil-lubricated compressor and valves. The animation is slowed so you can see the strokes.</p>`,
q:"In a pulse tube cooler, what replaces the moving displacer of a GM cooler?",o:["An oscillating gas flow in a thin tube, with a phase-shift mechanism such as an orifice and a reservoir","A second compressor at the cold end","A bath of liquid nitrogen around the regenerator"],a:0,e:"The gas in the pulse tube acts as a gas piston. The orifice and reservoir shift the phase between pressure and flow so the gas expands and cools just as it reaches the cold end, with no moving part at low temperature."},
{t:"Advanced: The Stirling cryocooler cycle on p–V and T–s diagrams",lead:"Watch the four steps of the ideal Stirling refrigerator in the machine and on the two diagrams, and see how an imperfect regenerator eats the cooling.",h:`<p>A Stirling cryocooler uses a fixed amount of helium that is compressed, moved between a warm end and a cold end, and expanded. The <b>regenerator</b> stores heat from the gas on its way to the cold end and gives it back on the way back. This lesson is general thermodynamics, not taken from the lectures; it treats helium as an ideal gas and the machine as ideal. Gifford–McMahon and pulse tube coolers use valves instead of a moving cold piston but rely on the same regenerative idea; their real cycles are more complex than the four-step ideal cycle drawn here.</p><h3>The four steps</h3><ol><li><b>1→2, isothermal compression at the warm temperature T<sub>h</sub>.</b> The warm piston moves in. The gas is squeezed and heat flows out to the surroundings at T<sub>h</sub>.</li><li><b>2→3, constant-volume cooling.</b> The gas is pushed through the regenerator to the cold end. The regenerator takes heat from it, cooling it from T<sub>h</sub> to T<sub>c</sub>.</li><li><b>3→4, isothermal expansion at the cold temperature T<sub>c</sub>.</b> The cold piston moves out. The gas expands and absorbs heat from the load: <b>this is the cooling effect</b>.</li><li><b>4→1, constant-volume heating.</b> The gas goes back through the regenerator, which returns the stored heat and warms it to T<sub>h</sub>.</li></ol><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=sdf viewBox='0 0 640 340' role=img aria-label='Animated ideal Stirling cooler with pistons and regenerator, and its p-V and T-s diagrams with a moving state point'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=sdt><b>Cold temperature = <span id=sdtv>77</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=sdt min=10 max=200 step=1 value=77><label for=sdr><b>Volume ratio V<sub>max</sub>/V<sub>min</sub> = <span id=sdrv>2</span></b></label><input type=range id=sdr min=1.2 max=5 step=0.05 value=2><label for=sde><b>Regenerator effectiveness = <span id=sdev>0.99</span></b></label><input type=range id=sde min=0.9 max=1 step=0.001 value=0.99><label for=sdn><b>Helium in the machine = <span id=sdnv>5</span> mmol</b></label><input type=range id=sdn min=0.5 max=50 step=0.5 value=5><label for=sdq><b>Frequency = <span id=sdqv>50</span> Hz</b></label><input type=range id=sdq min=5 max=60 step=1 value=50><div><button class=btn id=sdg>Pause</button></div></div><div class=res id=sdo></div></div><div class=cap id=sdx></div></div><h3>What the diagrams show</h3><p>On the <b>p–V diagram</b> the enclosed area is the net work put in per cycle. On the <b>T–s diagram</b>, two horizontal lines (the isothermal steps) and two curved lines (the constant-volume steps, which are the same shape shifted in entropy) enclose the work again; the area under the cold line is the cooling effect and the area under the warm line the heat rejected. With a perfect regenerator the two curved sides are exactly the same curve displaced horizontally, so the heat the regenerator takes in step 2→3 equals what it gives back in step 4→1 and the cycle needs no net heat exchange from them. Then, per mole of gas,</p><p>Q<sub>c</sub> = R T<sub>c</sub> ln r, Q<sub>h</sub> = R T<sub>h</sub> ln r, W = R (T<sub>h</sub> − T<sub>c</sub>) ln r,</p><p>where r = V<sub>max</sub>/V<sub>min</sub> and R = 8.314 J/mol·K. The ratio Q<sub>c</sub>/W is the Carnot value T<sub>c</sub>/(T<sub>h</sub> − T<sub>c</sub>): an ideal Stirling cycle with a perfect regenerator is as efficient as it is possible to be.</p><h3>The imperfect regenerator</h3><p>A real regenerator does not return all the heat. If its effectiveness is ε<sub>r</sub>, a fraction (1 − ε<sub>r</sub>) of the heat that should be recovered, c<sub>v</sub> (T<sub>h</sub> − T<sub>c</sub>) per mole with c<sub>v</sub> = 3R/2 for helium, is not recovered and shows up as an extra heat load on the cold end. The net cooling is then Q<sub>c,net</sub> = R T<sub>c</sub> ln r − (1 − ε<sub>r</sub>) c<sub>v</sub> (T<sub>h</sub> − T<sub>c</sub>). Try it. At 77 K a regenerator effectiveness of 0.99 costs a modest part of the cooling. Lower the cold temperature to 20 K and the same regenerator becomes costly, because the net cooling falls and, below some temperature, goes to zero: <b>the machine can no longer cool</b>. This is the reason why reaching 4 K needs special regenerator materials with high heat capacity at low temperature, and why the regenerator is the heart of every cryocooler. (The simple formula above is an approximation, and the heat capacity of the regenerator material also limits real machines at low temperature.)</p><p>The cooling power in watts of the ideal machine is Q<sub>c</sub> × n × f, with n the moles of gas swept and f the frequency. This is an <b>upper limit</b>: real machines deliver a fraction of it because of the dead volumes, friction, pressure drop and finite heat transfer.</p><div class=key><b>Key points</b><ul><li>Ideal Stirling cycle: isothermal compression, cooling at constant volume, isothermal expansion, heating at constant volume</li><li>The regenerator stores and returns the heat of the constant-volume steps; with ε<sub>r</sub> = 1 the cycle reaches the Carnot coefficient of performance</li><li>Each missing per cent of regenerator effectiveness loads the cold end with (1 − ε<sub>r</sub>) c<sub>v</sub> ΔT per mole per cycle</li><li>At low temperature the regenerator losses dominate, which is why 4 K coolers are difficult</li></ul></div>`,
q:"What does the regenerator do in an ideal Stirling cooler?",o:["It stores the heat of the gas on its way to the cold end and gives it back on the return, so the cold end sees only the expansion","It compresses the gas","It rejects heat to the surroundings"],a:0,e:"The regenerator is a heat store. If it is perfect, the constant-volume steps need no net heat from outside; any shortfall appears as an extra load on the cold end."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryogenic insulation",lead:"Foams, powders, vacuum and multilayer insulation, with the heat leak of a real LN2 vessel.",h:`<h3>Why insulation</h3><p>A cryogen in a vessel boils off continuously because heat leaks in. The vessel cannot be sealed, since the vapour-to-liquid volume ratio of a typical cryogen is about 175 (and the pressure would rise). Heat reaches the cryogen by <b>conduction</b> through supports and any gas, by <b>convection</b> of gas in the space, and by <b>radiation</b> from the warm wall at 300 K to the cold one at 77 K. Insulation is chosen for its thermal conductivity, working temperature, effectiveness, cost, ease of application, weight and reliability, and often combined.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Insulation</th><th>Made of</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expanded foam</td><td>Mass</td></tr><tr><td>Gas-filled powders and fibres</td><td>Mass</td></tr><tr><td>Vacuum alone</td><td>Vacuum</td></tr><tr><td>Evacuated powders</td><td>Mass + vacuum</td></tr><tr><td>Opacified powders</td><td>Mass + vacuum + reflective material</td></tr><tr><td>Multilayer insulation</td><td>Vacuum + reflective layers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Heat transfer through a layer is described by the <b>apparent thermal conductivity</b> k<sub>A</sub>, defined by Q = k<sub>A</sub> A (T<sub>1</sub> − T<sub>2</sub>)/L so that all modes are lumped together.</p><h3>Foams and powders</h3><p><b>Expanded foam</b> is a low-density cellular solid with gas (such as CO<sub>2</sub>) in the voids, so heat moves by conduction in solid and gas. For 77 to 300 K the lecture gives:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Density (kg/m³)</th><th>k<sub>A</sub> (mW/m·K)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Polyurethane foam</td><td>11</td><td>33</td></tr><tr><td>Polystyrene foam</td><td>39</td><td>33</td></tr><tr><td>Rubber foam</td><td>80</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>Silica foam</td><td>160</td><td>55</td></tr><tr><td>Glass foam</td><td>140</td><td>35</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Foam is cheap and can be blown directly onto the surface. At LN2 temperature, however, the CO<sub>2</sub> in the cells condenses, and over time air, hydrogen or helium diffuse in, so k<sub>A</sub> rises. Foams also contract a lot: the linear expansion coefficient is 7.2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> per °C for polystyrene foam against 1.15 × 10<sup>−5</sup> for carbon steel, so a foam fitted snugly can crack or pull away.</p><p><b>Gas-filled powders and fibres</b> (77 to 300 K) break up the gas into tiny gaps so gas conduction and convection fall. Perlite (50 kg/m³, 26 mW/m·K), silica aerogel (80, 19), glass fibre (10, 25) and rock wool (160, 35). The powders settle and pack, which raises solid conduction, and moisture and air must be kept out with a vapour barrier or nitrogen purge. As the solid fraction tends to zero, k<sub>A</sub> tends to the conductivity of the gas, the lowest possible for this type.</p><h3>Radiation and shields</h3><p>Radiation needs no matter. Between surfaces at T<sub>1</sub> and T<sub>2</sub>, Q = F<sub>e</sub> F<sub>1→2</sub> σ A (T<sub>1</sub><sup>4</sup> − T<sub>2</sub><sup>4</sup>), where F<sub>e</sub> is the effective emissivity factor. Placing N thin, low-emissivity <b>radiation shields</b> in the gap cuts F<sub>e</sub>:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>1/F<sub>N</sub> = (1/e<sub>1</sub> + 1/e<sub>s</sub> − 1) + (N − 1)(2/e<sub>s</sub> − 1) + (1/e<sub>s</sub> + 1/e<sub>2</sub> − 1)</b></p><p>For N = 0 it reduces to 1/F = 1/e<sub>1</sub> + 1/e<sub>2</sub> − 1. With e<sub>1</sub> = e<sub>2</sub> = 0.8 and shield emissivity e<sub>s</sub> = 0.05, the lecture finds F = 0.667 for no shields and F<sub>10</sub> = 0.00255 with ten. See the effect on a 300 K to 77 K wall:</p><div class=calc><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=dwr viewBox='0 0 640 290' role=img aria-label='Cross-section of an insulated LN2 vessel: radiation arrows from the warm outer wall thin out as radiation shields are added'></svg><button class=btn id=dwb>Spoil the vacuum</button></div><label for=ins><b>Number of shields N = <span id=insv>10</span></b></label><input type=range id=ins min=0 max=60 step=1 value=10><label for=ise><b>Shield emissivity = <span id=isev>0.05</span></b></label><input type=range id=ise min=0.02 max=0.3 step=0.01 value=0.05><label for=isa><b>Wall area = <span id=isav>10</span> m²</b></label><input type=range id=isa min=1 max=50 step=1 value=10><div class=res id=iso></div><div class=cap id=isn></div><b>Radiation heat flux against number of shields (log scale)</b><svg class=sv id=isc viewBox='0 0 320 170'></svg></div><h3>Vacuum and residual gas</h3><p>Even in vacuum a few gas molecules remain. When their mean free path λ exceeds the gap, they carry heat directly between the surfaces in <b>free molecular conduction</b>. A molecule that hits a surface leaves at a temperature between its own and the surface's; the <b>accommodation coefficient</b> a measures how fully it comes to the surface temperature. For two surfaces, F<sub>a</sub> = 1/(1/a<sub>1</sub> + 1/a<sub>2</sub> − 1), and kinetic theory gives Q = G p A (T<sub>2</sub> − T<sub>1</sub>), so the heat flow is proportional to pressure and the temperature difference, independent of the gap. Approximate accommodation coefficients, from the lecture:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Temperature</th><th>He</th><th>H<sub>2</sub></th><th>Ne</th><th>Air</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>300 K</td><td>0.29</td><td>0.29</td><td>0.66</td><td>0.8 to 0.9</td></tr><tr><td>78 K</td><td>0.42</td><td>0.53</td><td>0.83</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>20 K</td><td>0.59</td><td>0.97</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Insulating vacuum is normally held between about 10<sup>−3</sup> and 10<sup>−5</sup> torr. In an evacuated powder, k<sub>A</sub> is directly proportional to pressure between about 15 torr and 10<sup>−3</sup> torr; multilayer insulation reaches its lowest k<sub>A</sub> only once the pressure is down to about 10<sup>−5</sup> torr.</p><h3>Multilayer insulation (MLI)</h3><p>MLI was first developed in 1951 and alternates highly reflecting foils (typically 6 µm aluminium) with low-conductivity spacers (glass fibre, nylon net) in a good vacuum. For an optimum job the vacuum should be about 7.5 × 10<sup>−5</sup> torr, and performance depends on compressive load, number of shields, gas type and pressure, perforations and operating temperature. Layer bulk density follows from the foil and spacer masses per layer, and apparent conductivities of tens of µW/m·K are reported, far below any powder. Between layers heat crosses by radiation and by solid conduction through the spacers.</p><h3>Comparison on a real vessel</h3><p>The lecture compares insulations on a spherical LN2 vessel (inner 1.2 m, outer 1.6 m, 77 K to 300 K, emissivity 0.8):</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Insulation</th><th>Heat leak</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Perlite at atmospheric pressure</td><td>349.7 W</td></tr><tr><td>Vacuum alone (1.5 mPa)</td><td>2648 W radiation + 0.356 W gas</td></tr><tr><td>Vacuum + 10 shields</td><td>11.02 W</td></tr><tr><td>Evacuated fine perlite</td><td>12.7 W</td></tr><tr><td>50/50 copper–Santocel (opacified)</td><td>4.41 W</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Opacified powder is best. With latent heat 200 kJ/kg and liquid density 807 kg/m³, 4.41 W boils off 0.098 L/h of nitrogen, 2.36 L per day. The same 4.41 W would vaporise about 151 L of helium per day (20.2 kJ/kg, 124.8 kg/m³; 1 L/h of helium is about 0.7 W). That is why the lecture says insulations for 77 K down to 4 K need to be better.</p>`,
q:"Why does adding radiation shields cut the heat flow so strongly?",o:["Each low-emissivity shield adds a large series resistance to radiation, so 1/F grows roughly in proportion to the number of shields","Shields lower the temperature of the warm wall","Shields remove the residual gas"],a:0,e:"With a low-emissivity shield, each added layer adds about (2/e_s − 1) to 1/F, so 10 shields change F from 0.667 to about 0.0026."},
{t:"Advanced: Vacuum technology",lead:"Mean free path, conductance, pump-down time and the pumps that make a cryogenic vacuum.",h:`<h3>Why vacuum</h3><p>The heat leak into a cryogenic vessel is Q<sub>net</sub> = Q<sub>gas conduction</sub> + Q<sub>convection</sub> + Q<sub>solid conduction</sub> + Q<sub>radiation</sub>. Good vacuum removes gas conduction and convection, and it is what makes evacuated powders and multilayer insulation work. A <b>perfect vacuum</b> would be a space with no matter, which is an idealisation; real vacua are simply pressures well below atmosphere.</p><h3>Mean free path and flow regimes</h3><p>The <b>mean free path</b> λ is the average distance a molecule travels between collisions: λ = (μ/p) √(πℜT/2), with ℜ here the specific gas constant. It rises as pressure falls and as temperature rises. Its size relative to the pipe or vessel decides how the gas behaves, through the <b>Knudsen number</b> N<sub>Kn</sub> = λ/D, analogous to the Reynolds number:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>N<sub>Kn</sub></th><th>Regime</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 0.01</td><td>Continuum (viscous)</td><td>Molecules collide with each other; ordinary fluid flow</td></tr><tr><td>0.01 to 0.3</td><td>Mixed (slip)</td><td>Molecules near the wall slip</td></tr><tr><td>above 0.3</td><td>Free molecular</td><td>Molecules hit walls far more than each other</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Pressure units: 1 torr = 1 mm Hg = 133.3 Pa, 1 bar = 10<sup>5</sup> Pa, 1 atm = 101,325 Pa = 760 torr. Vacuum is graded by pressure: rough 25 to 760 torr, medium 10<sup>−3</sup> to 25 torr, high 10<sup>−6</sup> to 10<sup>−3</sup> torr, very high 10<sup>−9</sup> to 10<sup>−6</sup> torr, ultra-high below 10<sup>−9</sup> torr.</p><div class=calc><label for=vkd><b>Pipe diameter = <span id=vkdv>40</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=vkd min=5 max=200 step=5 value=40><label for=vkp><b>Pressure = <span id=vkpv>0.15</span> Pa</b></label><input type=range id=vkp min=-5 max=5 step=0.05 value=-0.82><div class=res id=vko></div><div class=cap>Air at 300 K, μ = 18.47 µPa·s. The default reproduces the lecture's 40 mm pipe at about 0.15 Pa: N<sub>Kn</sub> is about 1.1.</div></div><h3>Conductance and series pipes</h3><p>Gas flow through a pipe is driven by a pressure difference. In continuum flow, Poiseuille's law gives a throughput proportional to the fourth power of the diameter. The <b>conductance</b> C of a pipe is its throughput per unit pressure difference, in m³/s. Long tubes (L/D &gt; 30) have C = πD⁴p̄/(128μL) in continuum flow and C = (D³/L)√(πℜT/18M) in free molecular flow. Short tubes, elbows and apertures have their own formulas in the lecture's table. Conductances combine like electrical conductances: in series 1/C = Σ 1/C<sub>i</sub>, in parallel C = Σ C<sub>i</sub>.</p><p>Lecture tutorial: a 40 mm × 400 mm pipe in series with a 30 mm × 400 mm pipe, air at 300 K. Both are free molecular (N<sub>Kn</sub> 1.132 and 22.65) and short, with conductances 0.0173 and 0.0079 m³/s, so the overall conductance is 0.00542 m³/s.</p><h3>Pumping speed and pump-down</h3><p>A pump has speed S<sub>p</sub> at its inlet, but the vessel sees a lower system speed S<sub>s</sub> because of the connecting pipe:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>1/S<sub>s</sub> = 1/S<sub>p</sub> + 1/C<sub>o</sub></b></p><p>S<sub>s</sub> can never exceed S<sub>p</sub> or C<sub>o</sub>. If C<sub>o</sub> = S<sub>p</sub> the system speed is half the pump speed. Gas enters the vessel through real leaks, trapped pockets and especially outgassing of walls and seals, with a throughput Q<sub>i</sub>. A mass balance gives V dp/dt = Q<sub>i</sub> − S<sub>s</sub> p. At steady state the <b>ultimate pressure</b> is p<sub>u</sub> = Q<sub>i</sub>/S<sub>s</sub>. For constant S<sub>s</sub>, integrating from p<sub>1</sub> to p<sub>2</sub> gives the <b>pump-down time</b>:</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>t = (V/S<sub>s</sub>) ln[(p<sub>1</sub> − p<sub>u</sub>)/(p<sub>2</sub> − p<sub>u</sub>)]</b></p><div class=calc><label for=vpv><b>Volume = <span id=vpvv>1</span> m³</b></label><input type=range id=vpv min=0.1 max=10 step=0.1 value=1><label for=vps><b>Pump speed S<sub>p</sub> = <span id=vpsv>1.54</span> L/s</b></label><input type=range id=vps min=0.1 max=50 step=0.01 value=1.54><label for=vpc><b>Pipe conductance C<sub>o</sub> = <span id=vpcv>5.42</span> L/s</b></label><input type=range id=vpc min=0.1 max=100 step=0.01 value=5.42><label for=vpe><b>Ultimate pressure = <span id=vpev>0.0001</span> Pa</b></label><input type=range id=vpe min=-6 max=-1 step=0.1 value=-4><label for=vpt><b>Target pressure = <span id=vptv>1000</span> Pa</b></label><input type=range id=vpt min=-2 max=3 step=0.05 value=3><div class=res id=vpo></div><div class=cap>Starts from 1 atm (101,325 Pa) and assumes a constant system speed, the lecture's model. Defaults are the lecture's tutorial: a 1 m³ vessel, 1 kPa in about an hour.</div></div><p>In the lecture's second tutorial the required S<sub>s</sub> is 0.0012 m³/s and the pump speed needed is 0.00154 m³/s (92.4 L/min). Working from the exact S<sub>s</sub> = 0.001283 m³/s gives a pump speed near 0.0017 m³/s, so the lecture's figure is slightly rounded.</p><h3>Vacuum pumps</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Pump</th><th>How it works and where it is used</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rotary vane</td><td>Spring-loaded vanes in an eccentric rotor trap and push gas out; an oil-sealed primary (roughing) pump. A <b>gas ballast</b> lets a little air in so water vapour does not condense in the pump</td></tr><tr><td>Roots</td><td>Two synchronised lobed rotors with a fine clearance (about 0.3 mm) move gas without touching; need a backing pump</td></tr><tr><td>Diffusion</td><td>A heater vaporises oil whose downward vapour jet carries gas molecules to a backing pump; needs chilled baffles or a cold trap to stop oil backstreaming</td></tr><tr><td>Turbomolecular</td><td>6 to 7 stages of fast-spinning blades and stators knock molecules toward the outlet; backed by a rough pump</td></tr><tr><td>Cryopump</td><td>A two-stage cold head condenses gases on cold surfaces; helium, hydrogen and neon are adsorbed on a cooled adsorbent; clean and self-contained</td></tr><tr><td>Others</td><td>Getter, sputter ion, sublimation and adsorption pumps</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Choosing a pump depends on the process, the ultimate pressure needed, chamber volume and surface area, outgassing rate, pump-down time, size, weight, vibration, cost, and special needs such as hydrocarbon-free or reactive gases.</p>`,
q:"A vacuum pump of speed Sp is joined to a chamber by a pipe of conductance Co. When Co = Sp, what is the speed seen by the chamber?",o:["Sp","Sp / 2","2 Sp"],a:1,e:"1/Ss = 1/Sp + 1/Co = 2/Sp, so Ss = Sp/2. A narrow pipe can waste much of a big pump."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryogenic instrumentation",lead:"Measuring temperature, liquid level and pressure when everything is cold, small and in vacuum.",h:`<h3>Why special instruments</h3><p>Instrumentation monitors the insulating vacuum, shows how much cryogen is left, and records sample temperatures. Ordinary instruments often fail at extreme cold, under thermal and mechanical stress and in vacuum, and are hard to calibrate at low temperature. The lecture lists special requirements: <b>remote</b> operation (vessels are closed), <b>vacuum</b> and <b>cryogen</b> compatibility, behaviour in <b>magnetic fields</b>, good <b>accuracy</b> and calibration, low self-heating (i²R losses), and suitable thermal and mechanical material properties. The properties to be measured include temperature, liquid level, pressure, flow rate, viscosity and density, and conductivity. The lecture covers the first three.</p><h3>Temperature scales</h3><p>The international temperature scale is defined down to the triple point of hydrogen (13.84 K). Lower, special scales are used: a germanium resistance thermometer between 4.2 K and 13.84 K, the ⁴He vapour-pressure scale (T58) between 1.5 K and 4.24 K, the ³He vapour-pressure scale (T62) between 0.8 K and 1.5 K, and the magnetic susceptibility of a paramagnetic salt between 0.006 K and 0.8 K.</p><h3>Sensors</h3><p><b>Thermocouples</b> use the Seebeck effect (Thomas Seebeck, 1821): two different metals joined at two points at different temperatures give a small voltage that depends on the temperature difference. One junction is held at a reference temperature. T type (copper–constantan) works from 3 K to 673 K with about 4.6 µV/K at 20 K; K type (chromel–alumel) from 3 K to 1543 K with about 4.1 µV/K at 20 K; E type (chromel–constantan) from 3 K to 953 K with about 68 µV/K at 20 K, the highest sensitivity. Below about 50 K the sensitivity of T and K types falls and they are undesirable. Other drawbacks: the voltage is very small, lead-wire voltage drops matter, and conduction along the leads adds heat.</p><p><b>Metallic resistance thermometers</b> (RTDs; platinum, copper, indium or lead wires) change resistance with temperature. Platinum wire is wound on a notched mica former inside a sealed sheath; a linear resistance–temperature curve is preferred, and platinum RTDs (PT100, PT1000) are used where that holds. Sensitivity is the change of resistance per kelvin, and for a metal it falls at low temperature, so non-metallic sensors take over there.</p><p><b>Semiconductor and film sensors.</b> A <b>silicon diode</b> run on a constant 10 µA changes voltage with temperature: range 1.4 K to 475 K, accuracy about ±50 mK, sensitivity −33.6 mV/K at 4.2 K and −1.91 mV/K at 77 K, with very little self-heating, but it is affected by magnetic fields. <b>Cernox</b> (a sputtered thin-film resistor) covers 0.3 K to 325 K with about ±5 mK accuracy at 10 K, excellent stability, and is not affected by magnetic fields. <b>Ruthenium oxide</b> is a thick-film sensor. These non-metallic sensors have much higher sensitivity and resistivity than metals, so they suit temperatures below about 30 K. A four-wire connection removes lead resistance.</p><p>Which of these cover a chosen temperature?</p><div class=calc><label for=tps><b>Temperature = <span id=tpv>77</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=tps min=0.5 max=400 step=0.5 value=77><div class=res id=tpo></div><div class=cap>Ranges from the lecture. The platinum range is approximate (the lecture notes its linear behaviour down to about 30 K).</div></div><h3>Liquid level</h3><p>Level must be watched to avoid overfilling and to know the stock. Techniques in the lecture: the <b>dipstick</b> (oldest: a tube dipped in until boiling is heard), <b>hydrostatic gauge</b>, <b>electrical resistance gauge</b>, <b>capacitance gauge</b>, <b>thermodynamic gauge</b> and the <b>superconducting LHe gauge</b>.</p><p>In a closed vessel of height L, with liquid depth L<sub>f</sub> and vapour height L<sub>g</sub>, the pressure difference between the bottom and top taps is</p><p style='text-align:center'><b>Δp = (ρ<sub>L</sub> − ρ<sub>g</sub>) g L<sub>f</sub> + ρ<sub>g</sub> g L</b></p><p>so the gauge reads the liquid level directly, and its sensitivity is proportional to ρ<sub>L</sub> − ρ<sub>g</sub>. Using the lecture's densities (nitrogen 808 and 4.65 kg/m³, hydrogen 70.8 and 1.33, helium 124.8 and 16.7):</p><div class=calc><label for=hgf><b>Cryogen</b></label><select id=hgf><option value=0>Nitrogen</option><option value=1>Hydrogen</option><option value=2>Helium</option></select><label for=hgl><b>Vessel height = <span id=hglv>1.0</span> m</b></label><input type=range id=hgl min=0.2 max=3 step=0.1 value=1><label for=hgh><b>Liquid level = <span id=hghv>50</span>% full</b></label><input type=range id=hgh min=0 max=100 step=1 value=50><div class=res id=hgo></div><div class=cap>Gauge reading in pascals and millimetres of water column. Low-density cryogens give a much smaller signal.</div></div><p>The <b>resistance gauge</b> (Wexler and Cox, 1956) exploits the fact that a heated wire is cooled far better in liquid than in vapour, so the portion in liquid has lower resistance, but it dissipates heat continuously. The <b>capacitance gauge</b> uses the different dielectric constants of liquid and vapour between two probe electrodes; it has no moving parts and gives continuous readout. The <b>thermodynamic gauge</b> has a capillary charged with gas; when the tip is immersed, the gas condenses and the pressure falls. The <b>superconducting gauge</b> uses a filament which is superconducting where it is in liquid helium and resistive where it is in warm vapour, so the voltage across it gives the level continuously (with a small heating current and four-wire connection).</p><h3>Pressure and vacuum gauges</h3><p>Pressure is measured to check the insulating vacuum and to watch pressure rise in a vessel. Vacuum levels span atmosphere down to 10<sup>−12</sup> mbar, so different gauges cover different ranges:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Gauge</th><th>Principle and notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>McLeod (hydrostatic)</td><td>Traps and compresses a gas sample in mercury and uses Boyle's law. Independent of gas type, a reference standard for calibration, no zero correction; but gas must obey Boyle's law and the reading is not continuous</td></tr><tr><td>Diaphragm</td><td>A thin corrugated diaphragm deflects with the pressure difference from a reference side; read mechanically or electrically (capacitance)</td></tr><tr><td>Pirani (thermal conductivity)</td><td>A heated tungsten filament cools by the residual gas; resistance changes with gas conductivity, which falls below about 10<sup>−2</sup> torr. Fast response, cheap</td></tr><tr><td>Thermocouple gauge</td><td>Same idea, with a thermocouple at the filament centre to read temperature; range about 5 to 10<sup>−3</sup> mbar</td></tr><tr><td>Thermionic ionisation</td><td>A hot filament emits electrons that ionise residual gas; the ion current measures pressure. About 10<sup>−3</sup> to 10<sup>−10</sup> mbar</td></tr><tr><td>Cold cathode</td><td>Ionisation without a hot filament, typically 10<sup>−3</sup> to 10<sup>−7</sup> mbar</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`,
q:"Why is a hydrogen hydrostatic level gauge harder to read than one for nitrogen?",o:["Hydrogen is too cold for pressure gauges","The liquid–vapour density difference is much smaller, so the pressure signal per metre of level is smaller","Hydrogen vapour is denser than the liquid"],a:1,e:"Sensitivity is proportional to (ρL − ρg) g. For liquid hydrogen it is about 70 kg/m³ times g, a tenth of nitrogen's 800."},
{t:"Advanced: Measuring flow, pressure and liquid level",lead:"How to put a number on cold flow and level with orifices, Coriolis meters, superconducting wires and a simple pressure difference.",h:`<h3>Pressure: measure it warm if you can</h3><p>Three kinds of reading are common: gauge (relative to the atmosphere), absolute and differential. Most use a thin diaphragm whose flex is sensed capacitively, by strain gauges, or by <b>variable reluctance</b>: a magnetically permeable steel diaphragm sits between two inductive coils, and its displacement unbalances an AC bridge that is converted to a DC voltage. Sensors are usually kept at room temperature, joined to the cold process by a capillary running up through the cryostat. That tube must be leak-tight, its mean free path must be checked for low-pressure use, and, as the thermoacoustic lesson shows, a closed helium capillary can sing, so its size, length and routing matter.</p><h3>What counts as a vacuum</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Range</th><th>Upper (mbar)</th><th>Lower (mbar)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low</td><td>1000</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>10</td><td>10⁻³</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>10⁻³</td><td>10⁻⁶</td></tr><tr><td>Very high</td><td>10⁻⁶</td><td>10⁻⁹</td></tr><tr><td>Ultra-high</td><td>10⁻⁹</td><td>10⁻¹²</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For low and medium vacuum, <i>direct</i> gauges (capacitance manometers, diaphragms) measure force per area and do not care what gas is present. From high vacuum down, only <i>indirect</i> gauges work, which measure a gas property. A <b>Pirani gauge</b> uses the thermal conductivity of the gas (medium and high vacuum); <b>hot-cathode and cold-cathode (Penning)</b> gauges ionise the gas and measure the current (high to ultra-high). Indirect gauges read as if the gas were nitrogen, so other gases need a correction. Ionisation gauges do not like medium vacuum: electrodes burn out and leave soot. Wide-range gauges switch between methods at certain pressures, which can show up as a misleading step in the reading.</p><h3>Flow meters</h3><ul><li><b>Orifice plate.</b> A plate with a hole between two flanges makes a pressure drop that grows with the flow. Pressure taps upstream and at the <i>vena contracta</i> (the point of lowest pressure) feed a differential-pressure transmitter, often with a 4 to 20 mA output. Cheap and compact, but 2 to 5% accuracy and a high permanent pressure loss; discharge coefficient about 0.6.</li><li><b>Venturi.</b> The same principle with a smoothly narrowing throat: much lower loss, accuracy about 0.5% and a discharge coefficient near 1, but dearer and harder to install.</li><li><b>Thermal (mass) meter.</b> A small heater warms one of two resistance sensors; the flow cools it, and the electrical power needed to hold a fixed temperature difference gives mass flow. Fast, good at low flow, about ±1%, but for gas only.</li><li><b>Coriolis.</b> A tube is vibrated at resonance; flowing fluid twists it, and the phase shift between inlet and outlet sensors is proportional to mass flow. Tests at CERN and in Italy covered 0.5 g/s to 1.2 kg/s and temperatures down to 1.7 K. Accuracy can be as good as 0.25%, repeatability 0.1%, independent of fluid properties, no individual cryogenic calibration, remote electronics (radiation tolerant), a leak-tight secondary containment. The cold elastic modulus (about 207.5 GPa below 20 K versus 194.6 GPa at 20 °C) needs a correction factor of 1.066. Downsides: size, pressure drop and price.</li><li><b>Vortex.</b> A bluff body sheds vortices at a frequency f = St V / d (St is the Strouhal number, d the body width, V the speed). Independent of fluid properties, 1:10 turn-down, little loss, but needs turbulent flow, straight pipe, is sensitive to vibration, and has about 2% accuracy for gases. Used for warm low-pressure gas return.</li><li><b>Turbine.</b> A freely spinning rotor whose pulse frequency is proportional to volume flow (f = kQ). High accuracy and a wide temperature range, suited to low flows, but gas readings need pressure and temperature corrections, moving parts must be lubricated, and it needs regular calibration.</li></ul><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=fmf viewBox='0 0 640 168' role=img aria-label='Fluid accelerating through an orifice with the pressure drop measured across it'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=fmr><b>Fluid</b></label><select id=fmr><option value=125>Liquid helium (125 kg/m³)</option><option value=70.8>Liquid hydrogen (70.8)</option><option value=807 selected>Liquid nitrogen (807)</option><option value=1141>Liquid oxygen (1141)</option><option value=1394>Liquid argon (1394)</option></select><label for=fmk><b>Meter</b></label><select id=fmk><option value=0.6 selected>Orifice plate (C<sub>d</sub> 0.6)</option><option value=1>Venturi (C<sub>d</sub> about 1)</option></select><label for=fmd><b>Pipe diameter = <span id=fmdv>25</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=fmd min=10 max=100 step=1 value=25><label for=fmb><b>Throat ratio β = <span id=fmbv>0.50</span></b></label><input type=range id=fmb min=0.2 max=0.8 step=0.01 value=0.5><label for=fmp><b>Pressure drop = <span id=fmpv>10.0</span> kPa</b></label><input type=range id=fmp min=-1 max=2 step=0.02 value=1></div><div class=res id=fmo></div></div><div class=cap id=fmn></div></div><p>For an incompressible fluid the standard relation is ṁ = C<sub>d</sub> A<sub>t</sub> √(2 ρ ΔP / (1 − β⁴)), where A<sub>t</sub> is the throat area and β the throat-to-pipe diameter ratio. (The slide's equation did not extract cleanly; this is the standard form, with the lecture's C<sub>d</sub> values.) Because flow goes as the square root of ΔP, a meter turned down to a tenth of its flow sees a hundredth of the signal: that is why orifice meters have limited range.</p><h3>Liquid level</h3><ul><li><b>Superconducting wire.</b> A heater drives the normal zone along a superconducting wire until it reaches the liquid surface, where better cooling stops it; the wire's resistance reads level. Calibrated in liquid helium at 4.2 K, it behaves differently in He II because heat transfer is so much better. High-temperature superconductor versions exist for liquid nitrogen.</li><li><b>Differential pressure.</b> The pressure difference between the bottom and top of the vessel depends on the liquid and vapour columns: L = (ΔP/g − ρ<sub>gas</sub> L<sub>total</sub>) / (ρ<sub>liquid</sub> − ρ<sub>gas</sub>). Avoid liquid or gas traps in the sensing capillaries. For helium the sensitivity is about 1.06 Pa per mm of level.</li><li><b>Capacitive.</b> The dielectric constant of the liquid changes the capacitance of a probe; mostly custom-made, needs in-situ calibration, works best with high-permittivity fluids such as liquid nitrogen.</li><li><b>Ultrasonic</b> (sound speed and a resonance related to level) and <b>weighing</b> (mass of the vessel).</li></ul><div class=fig><div class=two><svg class=sv id=lvf viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='A tank with a differential pressure gauge across its height'></svg><div><label for=lvr><b>Fluid</b></label><select id=lvr><option value=125>Helium (125 / 16.7 kg/m³)</option><option value=70.8>Hydrogen (70.8 / 1.33)</option><option value=1240>Neon (1240 / 9.4)</option><option value=807 selected>Nitrogen (807 / 4.6)</option><option value=1141>Oxygen (1141 / 4.47)</option><option value=1394>Argon (1394 / 5.77)</option></select><label for=lvt><b>Tank height = <span id=lvtv>1000</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=lvt min=200 max=3000 step=50 value=1000><label for=lvh><b>Liquid level = <span id=lvhv>50</span> %</b></label><input type=range id=lvh min=0 max=100 step=1 value=50><div class=res id=lvo></div></div></div></div><h3>Rules of thumb</h3><ul><li>Do not buy more accuracy and precision than you need.</li><li>Use commercial sensors whenever possible; there are many.</li><li>Mount sensors outside the cryostat at 300 K when you can (pressure transducers, flow meters).</li><li>Use redundant sensors for critical devices inside the cryostat, and plan how to recalibrate them.</li><li>Avoid cold instrumentation feedthroughs if you possibly can.</li></ul>`,
q:"An orifice meter measures 10 L/min at 10 kPa. What differential pressure would it show at 1 L/min?",o:["About 0.1 kPa, because flow goes as the square root of ΔP","About 1 kPa","About 5 kPa"],a:0,e:"ΔP is proportional to flow squared, so a tenfold turn-down gives a hundredfold smaller signal. That is why orifice meters have a limited flow range."},
{t:"Advanced: Safety in cryogenics",lead:"The six basic hazards and how engineers and users protect against them.",h:`<p>Cryogens are liquefied gases stored at very low temperature: helium, nitrogen, argon, hydrogen and oxygen are the commonly used ones. Cold alone changes materials: rubber and organic matter turn brittle, and materials shrink, which can break parts or open leaks. Boil-off in a closed container can raise pressure suddenly. Handling cryogens therefore needs training and precautions.</p><h3>The six basic hazards</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Hazard</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Extreme cold</td><td>Bare skin or eyes exposed to cryogen or cold vapour can be burnt in as little as about 5 seconds; inhaling very cold vapour damages the lungs; prolonged contact with cold surfaces causes frostbite; skin can stick to a cold metal pipe and tear when pulled away</td></tr><tr><td>2. Oxygen deficiency</td><td>Breathing air needs about 19.5% oxygen at minimum; below that, a few minutes can bring choking, unconsciousness or death (asphyxiation). It is the most dangerous hazard because oxygen is odourless, colourless and gives no warning</td></tr><tr><td>3. Oxygen enrichment</td><td>Cold boil-off or cold pipes can liquefy air. Nitrogen has a much lower latent heat than oxygen, so it evaporates first, leaving an oxygen-rich liquid; with a flammable material this is a fire risk</td></tr><tr><td>4. Fire</td><td>Hydrogen and oxygen are flammable; hydrogen is colourless, odourless and explosive in air in the right proportions</td></tr><tr><td>5. Explosion</td><td>Vessels are closed and boil-off is continuous; without venting the pressure rises. Nitrogen and helium have low latent heats and big expansion ratios, so the rise can be sudden</td></tr><tr><td>6. Embrittlement</td><td>Face-centred cubic metals stay ductile at low temperature; body-centred cubic ones become brittle. Thermal stresses plus embrittlement can break pipes or supports</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Oxygen deficiency in a room</h3><p>The expansion ratio of a cryogen warming from its boiling point to ambient is of the order of 1:1000, and one litre of liquid nitrogen displaces about 700 litres of air. Nitrogen and argon vapour are heavier than air, so they pool and do not disperse; hydrogen and helium mix and stratify but still deplete oxygen. Unventilated or closed rooms, such as a small laboratory with a portable dewar, are the risk. Try a spill:</p><div class=calc><label for=sfv><b>Room volume = <span id=sfvv>50</span> m³</b></label><input type=range id=sfv min=5 max=500 step=5 value=50><label for=sfl><b>Liquid nitrogen evaporated = <span id=sflv>20</span> L</b></label><input type=range id=sfl min=0 max=300 step=1 value=20><div class=res id=sfo></div><div class=cap id=sfn></div></div><h3>Protection</h3><ul><li><b>Personal protective equipment:</b> a face shield and safety goggles for any transfer, loose-fitting insulated gloves that cryogen cannot flow into, high-top safety shoes, and overalls or lab coats covering all skin. No rings, watches or jewellery, because metal can freeze to the skin.</li><li><b>Procedure:</b> only trained and qualified personnel handle, transport or store liquefied gases; follow standard operating procedures; never overfill; pour or transfer slowly to limit boiling and splashing; direct all vents away from people and work areas. Placards and, for hydrogen, a flashing blue light warn of the risk.</li><li><b>Oxygen detectors</b> in risk areas read 0 to 25%. A first-stage warning sounds when oxygen falls below 19%, and a second, danger alarm at 17%.</li><li><b>Pressure relief valves:</b> a spring-loaded valve with an adjustable set pressure that opens to vent excess pressure and recloses. They need periodic checks, and electronic versions also monitor the system.</li><li><b>Bursting (rupture) discs:</b> a sacrificial metal diaphragm that bursts at a set overpressure or excess vacuum, used as a backup to relief valves. It works once and must be replaced.</li></ul>`,
q:"Why is oxygen deficiency the most dangerous cryogenic hazard?",o:["Oxygen is odourless and colourless, so depletion gives no warning","Liquid oxygen is always explosive","It only occurs in small laboratories"],a:0,e:"Nothing signals falling oxygen, and below about 19.5% a person can lose consciousness within minutes. Oxygen monitors and ventilation are the defence."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryogenic storage and boil-off",lead:"From a one-litre flask to a 160,000 m³ tank: how big vessels are insulated and why bigger is better.",h:`<h3>Why storage matters</h3><p>Cryogens have almost no shelf life without good containers: an uninsulated vessel can boil dry in minutes. Storage is needed for transport, for buffering inside every liquefaction plant, for rocket propellants on the ground and in flight, and for science. Vessel sizes span one litre to about 42 million US gallons (162,000 m³). A <b>cryostat</b> is a vessel that contains a cryogen, usually to cool a superconducting magnet or experiment; a <b>dewar</b> is the same thing, named for Sir James Dewar; a <b>storage vessel</b> is a dewar for holding and transporting liquid.</p><h3>Insulation, from worst to best</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Notes from the lectures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Solid foam</td><td>Gas-filled, so conductivity is no better than the gas (above about 20 mW/m·K)</td><td>Poor. A 20 mm polystyrene LN₂ vessel of 1 m² (k = 33 mW/m·K) leaks 368 W, boiling off 8.3 L/hour</td></tr><tr><td>Powder (perlite, glass microspheres, aerogel)</td><td>Cheap, easy to install; a soft vacuum is enough</td><td>Good at about 0.1 torr, against 10⁻⁴ torr for super-insulation. Used for LN₂, LNG and large LOX tanks</td></tr><tr><td>High vacuum plus radiation shields</td><td>Removes gas conduction; shields intercept radiation</td><td>Radiation goes as T⁴, so it is dominated by the 300 K wall</td></tr><tr><td>Multi-layer insulation (MLI)</td><td>Many aluminised-Mylar layers approximate a stack of shields</td><td>Recommended loads: 1 to 1.5 W/m² from 300 K to 77 K, and 50 to 100 mW/m² from 77 K to 4 K. Must be in vacuum; wrap in parallel layers, not tightly, or conduction along the layers shorts it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For n shields of emissivity ε the radiation flux is reduced by a factor of about n + 1 when ε is small. With one floating shield between 300 K and 77 K the shield settles at T<sub>s</sub> = [(T₁⁴ + T₂⁴)/2]<sup>1/4</sup> ≈ 252 K, not halfway, because radiation is not linear in temperature. A refrigerated 77 K shield around an LHe or LH₂ vessel cuts the radiant load by about 250 times, and the boil-off it causes is cheap LN₂. Try it in the shield simulator in the insulation lesson. Here we look at what happens to boil-off as the vessel gets bigger.</p><h3>Boil-off rate</h3><p>The heat leak Q turns liquid into vapour at ṁ = Q/h<sub>fg</sub>. The normal evaporation rate, in percent of the contents per day, is NER = (ṁ × 24 / (V ρ)) × 100. <b>Hold time</b> is how long a closed container keeps its contents before pressure builds up and gas must be vented; it ranges from days for storage to months for transport, and ISO tank containers can cross the globe without losing cargo.</p><h3>Why size and shape matter</h3><ul><li><b>Vacuum-insulated vessels of similar shape:</b> heat leak is proportional to area, Q ∝ V<sup>2/3</sup>, so NER ∝ V<sup>−1/3</sup>.</li><li><b>Powder-insulated vessels:</b> the insulation thickness scales with the vessel, so Q ∝ L ∝ V<sup>1/3</sup> and NER ∝ V<sup>−2/3</sup>. Powder wins decisively for the biggest tanks.</li><li><b>Shape:</b> a sphere has the largest volume for its area and the lowest stresses, but is costly to build at scale. A cylinder with dished or elliptical ends is only about 10% worse and is easy to fabricate. Long road and rail tanks have a fixed diameter, so their NER depends on length and radius.</li></ul><p>Set the vessel size, the heat flux and the insulation type below. The curves compare the two scaling laws; the dot is your vessel.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=sgt viewBox='0 0 300 200' role=img aria-label='Insulated spherical tank with boil-off vapour; more vapour means a higher evaporation rate'></svg><div class=calc><label for=sgf>Liquid</label><select id=sgf></select><label for=sgm>Insulation</label><select id=sgm><option value=0>High vacuum and MLI (flux independent of size)</option><option value=1>Powder (thickness grows with size)</option></select><label for=sgv><b>Volume = <span id=sgvv>10</span> m³</b></label><input type=range id=sgv min=-1 max=4 step=0.05 value=1><label for=sgq><b>Heat flux at 1 m radius = <span id=sgqv>2.0</span> W/m²</b></label><input type=range id=sgq min=1 max=100 step=1 value=20><div class=res id=sgo></div><svg class=sv id=sgc viewBox='0 0 340 190' role=img aria-label='Evaporation rate against volume for vacuum and powder insulation'></svg><div class=cap id=sgn></div></div></div><p>Real examples from the lectures: NASA's launch-pad tanks hold 900,000 gal of liquid oxygen and 1,250,000 gal of liquid hydrogen; a 1,000 m³ LNG tank is rated at an evaporation rate of 0.05% per day. Hydrogen's low density and latent heat make it far harder to store than nitrogen, so large LH₂ tanks often combine powder insulation with an LN₂-cooled shield.</p>`,
q:"For vessels of the same shape, which insulation gives the steepest fall of boil-off rate with size?",o:["Powder insulation: NER ∝ V^(−2/3), against V^(−1/3) for vacuum insulation","High vacuum: NER ∝ V^(−2/3)","Neither: boil-off rate does not depend on size"],a:0,e:"With powder, insulation thickness grows with the vessel, so the heat leak grows only as the linear dimension, not the area."},
{t:"Advanced: Piping, valves and transfer lines",lead:"Moving cryogens without freezing the plant, vapour-locking the pipe or losing half the liquid on the way.",h:`<h3>Transfer lines</h3><p>A cryogenic transfer line is a pipe inside a vacuum jacket, usually wrapped in multi-layer insulation and often with a nitrogen-cooled shield for helium. Design rules from the lectures:</p><ul><li><b>Slope the line up toward the warm end</b> so thermal stratification can establish itself, or if it must fall, make it fall after a short rise. Avoid low points and siphons in liquid-carrying lines, where vapour or trapped liquid causes surges.</li><li><b>Allow for thermal contraction</b> with bellows or loops; a cold line shortens by millimetres per metre.</li><li><b>Put a phase separator at the delivery end</b> of a long siphon. Two-phase flow arrives as gas and liquid; a sintered-bronze phase separator vents the gas and delivers liquid. Place such filters where they can be reached, because they clog.</li><li><b>Shields</b> are built as concentric cylinders, brazed cooling pipes, extruded finned elements, or quilted panels; the spacing of cooling pipes is set by the shield's conduction (the greater the sheet conductivity, the further apart the pipes may go).</li></ul><h3>Valves</h3><p>Cryogenic valves must keep the cold end cold and the stem seal warm. The helium plant specification quoted in the lectures is typical: extended-spindle valves with body and stem in line; a welded metal-bellows dynamic seal, designed for 10,000 full-stroke cycles, backed by a safety stuffing box; a static O-ring seal at the warm end (a double O-ring with guard gas in sub-atmospheric service); body of austenitic stainless steel such as 316L; stem and seat arranged so the trim can be changed without breaking the insulating vacuum. Leak-tightness targets are 10⁻⁶ Pa·m³/s to the atmosphere across the seal, 10⁻⁵ across the seat and 10⁻⁹ Pa·m³/s into the insulating vacuum.</p><h3>Sizing a valve: K<sub>v</sub></h3><p>The flow coefficient K<sub>v</sub> is the flow of water in m³/h that gives a 1 bar pressure drop. For a liquid it is K<sub>v</sub> = ṁ / √(1000 ρ Δp), with ṁ in kg/h, density ρ in kg/m³ and Δp in bar. There are separate formulas for gases, in subcritical and critical (choked) flow, because for gas the pressure and temperature at the inlet enter as well.</p><h3>Pipe pressure drop</h3><p>Δp = λ (l/d) (ρ v²/2), with friction factor λ from the Reynolds number Re = ρ v d / μ: for Re below 2300 the flow is laminar and λ = 64/Re; for 2300 < Re < 10⁵ in smooth pipe, Blasius gives λ = 0.3164 / Re<sup>0.25</sup>; for 10⁵ < Re < 10⁸ Nikuradse gives λ = 0.0032 + 0.221 / Re<sup>0.237</sup>. Corrugated hoses have higher friction than smooth pipe. Pick a cryogen and a line:</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=pgp viewBox='0 0 640 190' role=img aria-label='Flow in a pipe: streamlines and velocity profile, laminar or turbulent'></svg><div class=calc><label for=pgf>Liquid</label><select id=pgf></select><label for=pgm><b>Mass flow = <span id=pgmv>0.5</span> kg/s</b></label><input type=range id=pgm min=-3 max=1 step=0.05 value=-0.3><label for=pgd><b>Inner diameter = <span id=pgdv>25</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=pgd min=5 max=200 step=1 value=25><label for=pgl><b>Length = <span id=pglv>20</span> m</b></label><input type=range id=pgl min=1 max=200 step=1 value=20><div class=res id=pgo></div><div class=cap id=pgn></div></div></div>`,
q:"Why is a phase separator fitted at the delivery end of a long cryogenic transfer line?",o:["The liquid arrives as a mixture with vapour, and the separator vents the gas so that mainly liquid is delivered","It heats the liquid before delivery","It reduces the line's heat leak to zero"],a:0,e:"Heat leaking into a long line makes some of the liquid boil; the phase separator vents that gas and delivers liquid."},
{t:"Advanced: Pressure drop, fittings and two-phase flow",lead:"How to add up the losses in a cryogenic line, and what changes when the fluid is half liquid and half vapour.",h:`<h3>Where pressure goes</h3><p>Every component in a cryogenic system (vacuum-jacketed transfer lines, valves, heat exchangers, pumps, expanders) loses pressure, and each loss costs efficiency, because the entropy generated by fluid friction and by heat exchange across a finite temperature difference is the main source of inefficiency. The fluid may be a single-phase liquid, a compressible gas, a supercritical fluid or a two-phase mixture, but most of the fluid mechanics is classical. Pressure is lost to pipe friction, changes of direction, obstructions and changes of cross-section.</p><h3>Pipe friction</h3><p>For incompressible single-phase flow, ΔP = 4 f<sub>F</sub> (L/D<sub>h</sub>) (ρu²/2), where f<sub>F</sub> is the Fanning friction factor (the Darcy factor is four times larger) and the hydraulic diameter is D<sub>h</sub> = 4A/S (flow area over wetted perimeter). In a smooth tube: f<sub>F</sub> = 16/Re for laminar flow (Re &lt; 2000); f<sub>F</sub> = 0.0791/Re<sup>1/4</sup> (Blasius) for turbulent flow up to about 10⁴; and a von Kármán–Nikuradse relation above that. Rough tubes follow Colebrook's formula, which depends on the ratio of roughness to diameter, plotted on the Moody chart. (The previous piping lesson has calculators for these.)</p><h3>Fittings: the loss coefficient</h3><p>Bends, valves, tees, entrances and exits are handled with a resistance coefficient K: ΔP = K ρu²/2. For straight pipe K = f L/D, where f is the friction factor at the flow conditions; fittings are tabulated as an equivalent number of pipe diameters (a standard 90° elbow is 30 f<sub>T</sub>, for example), as in Crane's <i>Technical Paper 410</i>. Control valves are rated with C<sub>v</sub>, the number of US gallons per minute of 60 °F water giving a 1 psi drop. Add K for every component in the circuit and use the total. The lecture's worked example is a 1-inch line (internal diameter 1.049 in, completely turbulent friction factor 0.023): entrance 0.78, three standard elbows at 0.69 each, two 24-inch straight runs at 0.53 each and a sharp exit of 1.0, total K = 4.91.</p><div class=calc><label for=tkf><b>Fluid</b></label><select id=tkf><option value=125>Liquid helium (125 kg/m³)</option><option value=807 selected>Liquid nitrogen (807)</option><option value=1394>Liquid argon (1394)</option></select><label for=tke><b>Standard 90° elbows = <span id=tkev>3</span></b></label><input type=range id=tke min=0 max=12 step=1 value=3><label for=tkl><b>Straight pipe = <span id=tklv>1.22</span> m</b></label><input type=range id=tkl min=0 max=20 step=0.01 value=1.22><label for=tku><b>Flow speed = <span id=tkuv>2.0</span> m/s</b></label><input type=range id=tku min=0.1 max=10 step=0.1 value=2><div class=res id=tko></div><div class=cap id=tkn></div></div><h3>Compressible flow</h3><p>When the density changes along the line (an isenthalpic expansion of a real gas, or a heated channel in which warming accelerates the fluid) the momentum balance gains a term from the changing density: dp/dx = −4(f/D)(ρu²/2) + u² dρ/dx, and the density depends on pressure and temperature through the equation of state. That gives two unknowns, so a second equation is needed: the energy equation in terms of stagnation enthalpy, which brings in the Joule–Thomson coefficient (∂T/∂p at constant enthalpy).</p><h3>Two-phase flow</h3><p>Two-phase flow (liquid and vapour together) is common in cryogenics: the output of a JT valve, boil-off in a heated line, a cooldown. It depends on the fluid, how close the pressure and temperature are to saturation, gravity, heat transfer and the rate of phase change, and it is mostly empirical. The patterns (the <b>Baker</b> map has eight regimes in a horizontal pipe, from bubbly through slug and annular to mist) change with the velocities of the phases. Three definitions:</p><ul><li><b>Void fraction α:</b> the vapour volume fraction of the flow area.</li><li><b>Quality χ:</b> the vapour fraction of the mass flow.</li><li><b>Slip ratio S:</b> vapour speed over liquid speed, S = χ(1 − α)ρ<sub>l</sub> / ((1 − χ)αρ<sub>v</sub>).</li></ul><p>The total pressure drop is the sum of gravity (elevation), acceleration (the vapour speeds up as the liquid boils) and friction terms. For friction, a two-phase multiplier φ² relates the loss to that of a single phase flowing alone. Two standard models:</p><ul><li><b>Homogeneous</b> (S = 1; the phases move at the same speed and are in equilibrium): works best for helium at high Reynolds number. With the Blasius law, φ<sub>lo</sub>² = [1 + χ(ρ<sub>l</sub>/ρ<sub>v</sub> − 1)] [1 + χ(μ<sub>l</sub>/μ<sub>v</sub> − 1)]<sup>−1/4</sup>, relative to the same total flow as liquid.</li><li><b>Lockhart–Martinelli</b> (separated flow, equal static pressure drop in the two phases): works well for nitrogen and hydrogen at moderate flow. The correlating parameter X compares the pressure drops of the pure liquid and pure vapour, X<sub>tt</sub> = ((1 − χ)/χ)<sup>0.9</sup> (ρ<sub>v</sub>/ρ<sub>l</sub>)<sup>0.5</sup> (μ<sub>l</sub>/μ<sub>v</sub>)<sup>0.1</sup> for both phases turbulent, and φ<sub>l</sub>² = 1 + C/X + 1/X² (equivalently (X² + CX + 1)/X²) with C = 20 for turbulent–turbulent flow.</li></ul><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=tpf viewBox='0 0 640 175' role=img aria-label='A pipe carrying liquid and vapour with the void fraction and pressure drop multiplier'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=tpr><b>Fluid</b></label><select id=tpr><option value=0 selected>Nitrogen at 77 K</option><option value=1>Helium at 4.2 K</option></select><label for=tpx><b>Quality χ = <span id=tpxv>0.10</span></b></label><input type=range id=tpx min=0.005 max=0.99 step=0.005 value=0.1></div><div class=res id=tpo></div></div><div class=cap id=tpn></div></div><p>Densities are the saturated values from the lecture (nitrogen 807 and 4.6 kg/m³, helium 125 and 16.7 kg/m³); viscosities are standard handbook values (nitrogen liquid 1.6×10⁻⁴ and vapour 5.4×10⁻⁶ Pa·s; helium liquid 3.3×10⁻⁶ and vapour 1.0×10⁻⁶) and are not from the lecture. Notice how a tiny mass fraction of vapour occupies most of the pipe: 10% quality in nitrogen is about 95% void. The pressure drop of the mixture is a large multiple of the liquid alone, which is why cooldown lines choke and why two-phase lines are sized generously.</p>`,
q:"A line has a sharp entrance (K = 0.78), three elbows (0.69 each), 1.06 of straight pipe and a sharp exit (1.0). The total K and the pressure drop at velocity u are:",o:["K ≈ 4.9 and ΔP = 4.9 × ρu²/2","K ≈ 1 and ΔP = ρu²","K ≈ 2.7 and ΔP = ρu²/2"],a:0,e:"K values simply add: 0.78 + 3 × 0.69 + 1.06 + 1.0 = 4.91. The total pressure drop is K times the dynamic pressure ρu²/2."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryostat design",lead:"The eight parts of a cryostat, the heat-load budget that sizes the cooler, and thirteen rules from people who have built them.",h:`<h3>The main elements</h3><ol><li><b>Vacuum vessel.</b> The outermost shell: contains the insulating vacuum (10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁶ torr, the lower the better), is the main structure, and contains the pressure if a cold line leaks. Designed to the ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code, Section VIII.</li><li><b>Thermal shield and insulation.</b> A shield at 50 to 80 K, sometimes a second at 5 K or 20 K, intercepts radiation and is also the heat sink for supports, current leads and power couplers. Cooled by LN₂, cold helium gas or a cryocooler. Because it is a passive component, keep its cooldown time short with a high-conductivity, low-heat-capacity material such as aluminium. Covered with MLI: 30 to 60 layers on an 80 K shield, 10 to 15 on a colder one, about 50 to 60 layers per inch, and a realistic load of about 1.5 W/m² at 80 K and 0.15 W/m² at 4.5 K.</li><li><b>Cryogenic process piping and cryocoolers.</b> Supply and return lines compatible with the other materials and with codes such as ASME B31.3. Cryocoolers (Stirling, Gifford–McMahon, pulse tube) give 0.5 to 5 W at 4.2 K and a few hundred watts at 80 K, with a 50 K stage for the shield and a 4 K stage for conduction cooling.</li><li><b>Supports for the cold mass.</b> They must hold alignment through shipping and operation, yet insulate heat from 300 K. Strength and low conductivity pull in opposite directions; use materials with low thermal-conductivity integrals, such as stainless steel, titanium alloy and G-10 composites, and intercept the heat at the shield.</li><li><b>Flexibility and vibration isolation.</b> Everything shrinks on cooling. Bellows give axial flexibility, flex hoses lateral flexibility, and flexible thermal straps give flexibility, vibration isolation and a conduction path together.</li><li><b>Magnetic shield.</b> A high-permeability alloy (mu-metal at 300 K, Cryoperm when cold) 1.5 to 2 mm thick keeps the field at a cavity or sensor low, for example under 15 mG. It must not be bent or stressed after annealing.</li><li><b>Instrumentation and wiring.</b> Mount sensors at room temperature outside the vacuum when possible and heat-sink every lead wire.</li><li><b>Over-pressure protection.</b> Relief valves and rupture discs; see the pressure-relief lesson.</li></ol><h3>A worked example: a cryocooler-cooled SRF test cryostat</h3><p>The lectures describe a cryostat built to test an SRF cavity with no liquid helium. Pulse-tube coolers (a Cryomech PT420 gives 2 W at 4.2 K and 55 W at 45 K) cool a 45 K shield and a 4 K cavity; the vessel has four cooler ports. The shield hangs on four stainless-steel threaded rods and is wrapped in MLI; heat estimates are about 7 W from rod conduction and 7 W from radiation through MLI, under 15 W in total against 55 W of capacity. The cavity hangs from four titanium Ti-6Al-4V rods and is joined to the cooler by 5N aluminium flexible links; conduction from 300 K to the cold mass is only 70 mW, the rods are stressed to about 510 psi against a yield strength of 120,000 psi, and the cavity dissipates 2 W at about 4.8 K. Rebuild that heat budget yourself. The conduction integrals use the lecture's tabulated values (4 to 76 K and 76 to 300 K), interpolated to a 45 K shield.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=cyf viewBox='0 0 640 250' role=img aria-label='Cutaway of a two-stage cryostat showing heat flowing in to the 45 K shield and the 4 K cold mass; arrows scale with the heat load'></svg><div class=calc><div class=two><div><label for=cyn1><b>Shield rods (stainless): number <span id=cyn1v>4</span></b></label><input type=range id=cyn1 min=1 max=12 step=1 value=4><label for=cyd1><b>diameter <span id=cyd1v>10</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=cyd1 min=2 max=20 step=0.5 value=10><label for=cyl1><b>length <span id=cyl1v>150</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=cyl1 min=30 max=600 step=5 value=150><label for=cya1><b>Shield MLI area <span id=cya1v>4.5</span> m²</b></label><input type=range id=cya1 min=0.5 max=15 step=0.1 value=4.5></div><div><label for=cyn2><b>Cold-mass rods (Ti-6Al-4V): number <span id=cyn2v>4</span></b></label><input type=range id=cyn2 min=1 max=12 step=1 value=4><label for=cyd2><b>diameter <span id=cyd2v>9.5</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=cyd2 min=2 max=20 step=0.5 value=9.5><label for=cyl2><b>length <span id=cyl2v>210</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=cyl2 min=30 max=600 step=5 value=210><label for=cya2><b>Cold-mass MLI area <span id=cya2v>0.6</span> m²</b></label><input type=range id=cya2 min=0.1 max=5 step=0.1 value=0.6></div></div><label for=cyp><b>Device dissipation at 4 K = <span id=cypv>1.5</span> W</b></label><input type=range id=cyp min=0 max=4 step=0.1 value=1.5><label for=cyc1><b>Cooler capacity at 45 K = <span id=cyc1v>55</span> W</b></label><input type=range id=cyc1 min=10 max=200 step=1 value=55><label for=cyc2><b>Cooler capacity at 4.2 K = <span id=cyc2v>2.0</span> W</b></label><input type=range id=cyc2 min=0.5 max=5 step=0.1 value=2><div class=res id=cyo></div><div class=cap id=cyn></div></div></div><h3>Thirteen design tips from the lectures</h3><ol><li>Define and prioritise requirements first.</li><li>Design safety features in from the start.</li><li>Use only materials shown to be suitable at cryogenic temperature.</li><li>Study the literature and use existing codes and standards.</li><li>Use tested commercial solutions where you can.</li><li>Intercept heat at higher temperatures; remember the second law, since removing a watt at 77 K costs far less work than at 4 K.</li><li>Allow for thermal contraction; do not over-constrain parts as they cool.</li><li>Avoid feedthroughs and demountable seals at cryogenic temperature.</li><li>Heat-sink sensor wires so that leak and reading are both right.</li><li>Install pressure transducers and flow meters at room temperature when possible.</li><li>Analyse the design for thermoacoustic oscillations.</li><li>Hold independent design reviews at conceptual and detailed stages.</li><li>Test a prototype, and when you make many cryostats, series-test the production units too.</li></ol>`,
q:"Why are cold-mass supports always a compromise?",o:["They must be strong enough to hold alignment yet have low thermal conductivity to limit heat leak","They have to conduct heat to the cold mass","They only need to carry the weight of the shield"],a:0,e:"Mechanical strength and low conductivity pull in opposite directions; designers pick materials with a low conductivity integral and intercept the heat at a warmer shield."},
{t:"Advanced: Designing a cryogenic system",lead:"From requirements and interfaces to technology choice, build-or-buy and commissioning, with a worked cost comparison.",h:`<h3>A process, not a calculation</h3><p>The thermodynamics gets you a heat load and a cycle, but delivering a big cryogenic system from start to finish is systems engineering. The ideal order is requirements, interfaces, strategy (make or buy, prototyping, technology), cost and schedule estimate, detailed design, procurement and manufacturing, installation and commissioning. The real process is multidimensional: each step feeds back to the earlier ones, and the main message of the lecture is to keep stakeholders engaged and to separate requirements from wishes.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=dsf viewBox='0 0 640 215' role=img aria-label='Eight stages of a cryogenic project from requirements to commissioning with feedback loops'></svg><div class=cap>The stages run left to right and top to bottom; the dashed loop shows that real projects iterate.</div></div><h3>Requirements</h3><p>Requirements are tiered and traceable up and down: high-level programmatic, scientific, engineering and conventional-facilities requirements, flowing down from stakeholders (scientists, funding agency, laboratory, advisory panels). A requirements database records for each item its ID, type, name, text, rationale, value, status, and its parents and children, so the origin of every number can be followed. They fall into groups:</p><ul><li><b>Process:</b> operating temperature and pressure, allowable heat leak, expected heat load, coolant flow and allowable pressure drop.</li><li><b>Safety and regulatory:</b> loads from pressure and temperature, maximum allowable working pressure (MAWP), minimum design metal temperature (MDMT), size and weight for transport, compliance with design codes (ASME, CGA, API, EN).</li><li><b>Instrumentation:</b> warm or cold; temperature, pressure, flow; how many, where, what range and sensitivity, what readout.</li><li><b>Environment and infrastructure:</b> magnetic fields, radiation, access; electrical power, cooling water, HVAC, compressed air; lifting, rigging and transport.</li></ul><p>Constraints to keep in mind from the start: suitability of materials for cryogenic use, residual magnetism and radiopurity, the effect of materials on purity in a liquid-argon detector (does the material outgas or poison the lifetime, has it been tested?), schedule and cost limits, and logistics (weight, size, transport, underground access).</p><h3>Interfaces and the statement of work</h3><p>An interface is a boundary with another system (detector and cryogenics, cryogenics and cryogenics, or project and vendor) covering electrical, mechanical, instrumentation, controls and utilities, with location, type, size and who supplies what. They are documented with interface control documents, tables, drawings and three-dimensional envelopes. The requirements and interfaces for anything that is bought or built go into a <b>statement of work</b> together with applicable codes and standards, deliverables, quality assurance and environment, safety and health provisions, whatever the acquisition strategy.</p><h3>Strategy: where judgement is needed</h3><p>Technology choice, design in-house or by industry, prototyping, and procurement approach depend on cost, available resources, complexity, experience and what gives the best chance of success. The lecture's example is a large liquid-argon detector programme needing 4 × 100 kW of cooling for about 20 years, running 24/7.</p><div class=fig><div class=two><div><label for=dsq><b>Cooling capacity = <span id=dsqv>400</span> kW</b></label><input type=range id=dsq min=50 max=800 step=10 value=400><label for=dsl><b>Liquid nitrogen price = $<span id=dslv>0.167</span> per kg</b></label><input type=range id=dsl min=0.05 max=0.5 step=0.001 value=0.167><label for=dsk><b>Plant input power = <span id=dskv>7.5</span> kW per kW of cooling</b></label><input type=range id=dsk min=4 max=15 step=0.1 value=7.5><label for=dse><b>Electricity = $<span id=dsev>0.068</span> per kWh</b></label><input type=range id=dse min=0.03 max=0.2 step=0.001 value=0.068><label for=dsw><b>Cooling water and other = $<span id=dsww>325</span> k per year</b></label><input type=range id=dsw min=0 max=1000 step=5 value=325><label for=dsc><b>Extra capital for the plant = $<span id=dscv>20</span> M</b></label><input type=range id=dsc min=0 max=100 step=1 value=20></div><div class=res id=dso></div></div><div class=cap id=dsn></div></div><p>Open-loop liquid nitrogen is simple, but the lecture's numbers show why it is not feasible here: about 2050 g/s of liquid nitrogen, about $10.8 million a year, and 15 tanker trucks a day for 20 years (and, as a safety matter, no cryogens should be piped down a shaft). A refrigeration plant on site draws about 3 MW of electricity at about $0.068 per kWh (about $1.8 million a year) plus cooling water, giving about $2.1 million a year in running cost. The refrigeration plant's capital cost was not disclosed in the lecture, so the slider for it is yours to set; the calculator reproduces the lecture's yearly figures at its defaults, taking nitrogen at 195 kJ/kg (latent heat plus a little sensible heat), a price calibrated to $10.8 million, and 11.8 tonnes per tanker (derived from 15 trucks a day).</p><h3>In-house or industry?</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>In-house</th><th>Industry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design</td><td>36 months</td><td>10 months</td></tr><tr><td>Procurement and manufacture</td><td>24 months</td><td>14 months</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>60 months</td><td>24 months</td></tr><tr><td>Experience</td><td>Highly skilled, but not every day; must research vendors and large-scale start-up</td><td>Do this every day at 10 to 100 times the scale; qualified vendors for start-up; turnkey warranty</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Doing it in-house is possible but, as in the lecture's case, usually not the best choice. Equally, practical layout questions (for example compressors above ground versus underground, with the plant close to the detector) need their own trade study. For procurement, choose among design-build with or without vendor oversight, fixed-price, firm-fixed-price (with or without economic price adjustment) or cost-reimbursable contracts, single-phase or multi-phase, with options. The aim is a partnership with the vendor.</p><h3>Estimates</h3><p>Cost and schedule estimates come from vendor quotes (pipes and valves), independent cost estimates (a refrigeration system), direct industry engagement, online quotes (standard items), engineering judgement and previous projects. Requests for quotation or information, and persistent follow-up, build relationships and good numbers.</p><h3>Design: analyses and drawings</h3><p>Conceptual or detailed, design follows codes and standards and meets the requirements within the interfaces. It typically contains analyses (heat load and heat leak; first- and second-law thermodynamic analysis; sizing for flow, pressure drop and vacuum; thermomechanical analysis of loads and contraction; pressure relief and safety device sizing; failure modes and effects; what-if), the piping and instrumentation diagrams (P&IDs) and 3D models (envelopes, actual models, fabrication drawings). A P&ID shows the key piping and instruments, control and shutdown schemes, safety and regulatory features and start-up and operation information, and is the common language for controls programming, operating documents, cost estimates and discussions with interfacing systems.</p><p>A worked refrigeration-sizing example from the lecture: a bank of four 100 kW refrigerators serves four large cryostats, with three enough to maintain purity and one a full spare. The load to recondense argon in one cryostat is the sum of its heat ingress (48.7 kW), two recirculation pumps (11.0 kW), piping and purification vessel ingress (3.7 kW) and detector electronics (23.7 kW), which gives 87.1 kW; when the only duty is protecting inventory it is just the 48.7 kW heat ingress. Fill loads are much larger (hundreds of kilowatts) which is why the fill time of a cryostat is measured in months.</p>`,
q:"In the lecture's technology comparison, why was open-loop liquid nitrogen rejected for a 400 kW, 20-year, 24/7 load?",o:["About $10.8 million a year, 15 tanker trucks a day and cryogen piping in the shaft, against about $2.1 million a year for on-site refrigeration","Liquid nitrogen is too warm to cool argon","Refrigeration plants have no running cost"],a:0,e:"Consumption of about 2050 g/s made the open-loop option expensive and logistically heavy; on-site refrigeration cost about $1.8 million a year in electricity plus cooling water."},
{t:"Advanced: Pressure relief and overpressure safety",lead:"Why one litre of liquid can burst a steel vessel, and how relief valves, rupture discs and the codes prevent it.",h:`<h3>Energy-based hazard thinking</h3><p>Every injury is the unwanted release of, and contact with, an energy source: mechanical, electrical, pressure or temperature. A cryogenic system holds a lot of the last two. The hazards the lectures list are extreme cold, oxygen deficiency, fire, oxygen enrichment, over-pressurisation or explosion by rapid expansion, high noise and embrittlement of materials. This lesson is about pressure.</p><h3>Why the pressure is so high</h3><p>A cryogen warmed in a closed volume expands enormously: nitrogen's liquid density is about 700 to 800 times its gas density at room conditions. Held in a closed volume, that expansion can only turn into pressure. Compare the volumes and find the pressure if a trapped volume warms to 300 K (an ideal-gas upper estimate; real gas gives less, but still far above any vessel rating):</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=prx viewBox='0 0 640 216' role=img aria-label='Square areas show the volume of one litre of liquid next to the gas it becomes at 300 K'></svg><div class=calc><label for=prf>Cryogen</label><select id=prf></select><label for=prp><b>Fill of the closed volume = <span id=prpv>80</span>%</b></label><input type=range id=prp min=5 max=100 step=1 value=80><div class=res id=pro></div><div class=cap id=prn></div></div></div><h3>What can go wrong</h3><ul><li><b>Sudden loss of vacuum.</b> Air floods the insulating space and condenses on cold surfaces, giving a very high heating rate. This is usually the design case for relief sizing.</li><li><b>Fire.</b> Extreme external heating of the vessel (sometimes not credible, depending on the installation).</li><li><b>Trapped volume.</b> Liquid or cold gas between two closed valves warms slowly from normal heat leak and expands: a relief must be fitted to every such volume.</li><li><b>External pressure sources.</b> Compressors or supply valves feeding the vessel; a room-temperature relief on the supply pipe protects it.</li></ul><h3>Relief devices</h3><p>A <b>pressure relief valve</b> (PRV or PSV) is a spring-loaded valve that opens at its set pressure and recloses, so the system reseals after an upset. A <b>rupture disk</b> is a sacrificial diaphragm: cheaper at high venting capacity and good for rare events, but it works once. For a normally sub-atmospheric volume such as a 2 K system, the inlet flange must be sealed or guarded against in-leaking air; a rupture disk avoids that problem, or a guard-gas volume can sit between disk and valve. Relief valves are needed on every vessel and pipe that contains cryogen, or could under a failure (including the vacuum vessel of a cryostat).</p><p>The procedure: establish the maximum allowable working pressure (MAWP) for every vessel and pipe that may hold cryogen; find the worst-case rate at which fluid must leave to keep the pressure under the MAWP; then show that the relief valves as installed, including pressure drop in the inlet and outlet piping, pass that flow. Codes such as the CGA S-1.3 standard give formulas for loss of vacuum and for fire, and rules for the temperature at the valve inlet: for a liquid relieving below the critical pressure use the saturation temperature at relieving pressure; above it (common for helium, which has the lowest critical pressure of any fluid) the code points to the temperature at which a particular fluid property is maximum.</p><h3>Sizing by heat input</h3><p>When measured heat fluxes exist, the required vent rate is simply ṁ = Q/h<sub>fg</sub> with Q = q × area. The lectures quote experimental peak fluxes for air condensing on a liquid-helium cryostat: about 0.6 W/cm² for a super-insulated vessel and 3.8 W/cm² for an uninsulated one.</p><div class=calc><label for=prg>Liquid</label><select id=prg></select><label for=prh>Insulation case</label><select id=prh><option value=0.6>Super-insulated vessel: 0.6 W/cm²</option><option value=3.8>Uninsulated vessel: 3.8 W/cm²</option></select><label for=pra><b>Exposed cold surface = <span id=prav>2</span> m²</b></label><input type=range id=pra min=0.1 max=20 step=0.1 value=2><div class=res id=prq></div><div class=cap id=prr></div></div><h3>A lesson learned</h3><p>A 50-litre laboratory LN₂ dewar was being filled from a 160-litre dewar. The transfer hose would not fit the neck, so a rubber hose was clamped over the outside of the neck with a slot cut for venting. Cooling of the neck during the fill broke the seal to the vacuum jacket through differential thermal contraction; the dewar was nearly full, the vent was inadequate, and it exploded, badly injuring one person. The lesson: a broken insulating vacuum plus restricted venting can destroy even a small dewar, so use the right fill adapter and never improvise a seal.</p>`,
q:"Why must a volume trapped between two closed valves have its own relief device?",o:["Normal heat leak slowly warms the cryogen and it expands several hundred times, with nowhere to go","The valves would be damaged by cold","Trapped volumes cannot be filled"],a:0,e:"Liquid or cold gas locked between valves warms by ordinary heat leak and can develop pressures far above the vessel rating without a relief."},
{t:"Advanced: Thermoacoustic oscillations in cryogenic systems",lead:"Why a quiet instrumentation tube can sing, shake an accelerator cavity and leak 1000 times more heat than conduction.",h:`<h3>Heat and sound talk to each other</h3><p><b>Thermoacoustics</b> is the interaction of heat and sound in a fluid. Temperature gradients in a compressible gas can make sound waves, and sound waves can make temperature gradients. This is always going on, but we rarely notice: a normal conversation (about 60 dB) produces temperature swings of only about 10⁻⁴ K. In a cryogenic system, however, a narrow gas-filled tube running from room temperature to 4 K can turn this tiny effect into large, self-sustaining pressure oscillations: <b>thermoacoustic oscillations (TAOs)</b>.</p><p>Three ingredients are needed: a compressible gas in a narrow tube with a closed end, a large temperature difference along it, and a gas whose thermal diffusivity is much larger than its viscous diffusivity. Helium between 300 K and 4 K ticks all the boxes. They turn up in instrumentation lines, control valves and valve stems, wherever a stagnant gas column bridges warm and cold.</p><h3>The mechanism, in two pictures</h3><p><b>Simplified:</b> gas at the cold end warms as it moves toward the warm end, expands and its pressure rises; that pressure pushes gas back toward the cold end, which drops the pressure at the warm end; gas then flows back into the lower-pressure space, and the cycle repeats. Under the right tube size and temperature gradient the oscillation grows and sustains itself.</p><p><b>As a mass and a spring:</b> at low temperature the gas is dense but has little viscosity, so the cold gas behaves like a heavy slug of mass. The gas in the warm region acts as a spring (compressible). A mass on a spring that is pumped in step with its motion will oscillate; here the heat does the pumping. The heat flow is the nasty part: the oscillating gas shuttles heat from the warm wall to the cold end, much more than steady conduction through the tube wall would carry.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=taf viewBox='0 0 640 255' role=img aria-label='A gas-filled tube between a warm closed end and a cold open end with the gas oscillating and a pressure trace'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=tah><b>Warm end temperature = <span id=tahv>300</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=tah min=20 max=300 step=1 value=300><label for=tal><b>Tube length = <span id=talv>1.0</span> m</b></label><input type=range id=tal min=0.1 max=3 step=0.05 value=1><button class=btn id=tad type=button>Fit a damping volume</button></div><div class=res id=tao></div></div><div class=cap id=tan></div></div><p>The frequency estimate treats the tube as a quarter-wave resonator (closed at the warm end, open at the cold end) and integrates the sound travel time along the linear temperature profile. It uses the ideal-gas sound speed of helium and is only an estimate, but it lands in the tens to hundreds of hertz range that is seen in practice.</p><h3>A short history of the theory</h3><ul><li><b>1863:</b> Helmholtz calculates the damping of sound in long tubes from wall friction.</li><li><b>1868:</b> Kirchhoff adds heat conduction to the wall.</li><li><b>1969 to 1973:</b> Nikolaus Rott builds the linear theory of thermoacoustics for gas in a channel with an axial temperature gradient. His analysis applies when the channel width is of the order of the gas's thermal penetration depth (about 1 mm for helium), which is much shorter than the sound wavelength (about 1 m). Time-averaged products of the oscillating quantities give the engineering results: heat flux goes with the product of temperature and velocity, work with pressure and velocity, mass flux with density and velocity. Swift's <i>Thermoacoustic Engines</i> (1988) gives the rigorous derivation.</li></ul><h3>When do TAOs appear?</h3><p>Not always. There are two stable regions, and TAOs live between them:</p><ul><li>If the tube is too narrow, viscous friction on the walls damps the oscillation before it can build.</li><li>If the tube is too wide, heat transfer from the wall into the bulk of the gas is too weak to drive it.</li></ul><p>Stability maps use the temperature ratio α = T<sub>hot</sub>/T<sub>cold</sub>, a length ratio ξ = (L − l)/l (L the total length and l the distance from the cold end to where the temperature equals the average of hot and cold) and a dimensionless diameter group that compares the tube radius with the viscous penetration depth in the cold gas (d/2 times the square root of the cold sound speed over the product of the cold length and kinematic viscosity). The slide equations did not extract cleanly, so they are described here in words. Findings from the maps: a longer tube needs a larger minimum diameter to avoid TAOs, and a colder warm end lowers the minimum diameter, so an 80 K thermal intercept helps. Rott's theory says TAOs cannot exist when the warm-to-cold temperature ratio is below about 6.</p><h3>Signs of trouble</h3><ul><li>Heat loads up to about 1000 times conduction.</li><li>Ice balls at the warm end of an oscillating line or valve stem.</li><li>Noisy or disturbed temperature and pressure readings.</li><li>Mechanical vibration.</li></ul><h3>A case study: a superconducting cavity cryomodule</h3><p>An accelerator cryomodule had to hold the 1.3 GHz resonance of its cavities to within 10 Hz of peak detuning (equivalent to about 3 nm of cavity compression), and a static heat load at 2 K under 9.5 W. When first cold, it showed about 102 Hz average peak detuning and a 42 W static load. The detuning spectrum was dynamic and wandered, which suggested a cryogenic source. Ice balls were found on the valves and instrumentation lines. Varying the cryogenic conditions revealed that at a subcritical supply pressure the vibration improved by about a factor of 10 and the flow fell from 4.7 to 1.75 g/s, pointing to TAOs in the valve stems. A theory for why subcritical pressure stops them is not settled; one idea is that the stem changes from a dense supercritical fluid (128 kg/m³ at 5 K) to a low-density vapour (39 kg/m³) that cannot sustain the oscillation.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Mitigation</th><th>Supply pressure</th><th>Static heat load</th><th>Average peak detuning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>None (supercritical)</td><td>35 psig</td><td>42 W</td><td>102 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>None (subcritical)</td><td>13 psig</td><td>11 W</td><td>not measured</td></tr><tr><td>Single wiper on each valve</td><td>35 psig</td><td>11 W</td><td>55 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple wipers plus damping volumes</td><td>35 psig</td><td>11 W</td><td>11 Hz</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Cures</h3><ul><li><b>Reduce the driving force:</b> reduce the temperature gradient, make the warm end colder, change the length ratio.</li><li><b>Add damping:</b> enlarge the cross-section at the cold end and shrink it at the warm end.</li><li><b>Convection brakes (wipers):</b> a barrier that closes the annular space in a valve stem, placed where the local temperature ratio is about 2 to 3 so Rott's criterion is satisfied. A single wiper confined the TAOs above it, so the heat load fell from 42 W to 11 W but vibration remained; several wipers or reversing the valve were needed.</li><li><b>A second oscillator:</b> a damping volume (300 to 1000 mL) connected through a metering valve; adjust it while watching a pressure trace until the oscillation vanishes. If its impedance matches the tube's acoustic impedance, the wave is absorbed. It cannot be used where there is no warm connection.</li><li><b>Design rules:</b> avoid closed lines, remove the helium fill line after transfer, use check valves and buffer volumes with restrictive elements (porous fillers, orifices), fill the tube cross-section with wires, keep a small gas flow, or add a vent hole partway up.</li></ul>`,
q:"A helium instrumentation tube from 300 K to 4 K starts to hum and the cryostat loses far more heat than conduction predicts. What is the most plausible cause and a first fix?",o:["Thermoacoustic oscillations; add a damping volume or restrict the tube so the oscillation is absorbed","Poor insulation vacuum; pump harder","Magnetic hysteresis in the wall"],a:0,e:"Gas in a closed narrow tube with a large temperature gradient can sustain pressure oscillations that pump heat to the cold end. Damping volumes, wires or wipers, and lower warm-end temperature are standard cures."},
{t:"Advanced: Helium II cryogenic systems",lead:"How a 2 K plant is built: pumping the vapour down with cold and warm compressors to make superfluid helium for accelerators.",h:`<h3>Three ways to make He II</h3><p>Above the lambda line the liquid is He I; below it, He II, with very low viscosity and huge heat conductivity, widely used to cool large superconducting magnets and accelerator cavities. Three routes lead there, depending on what you want:</p><ul><li><b>Pump on a bath of He I.</b> Lower the vapour pressure and the liquid follows the saturation line to a <i>saturated</i> He II bath.</li><li><b>Cool a sealed bath at constant pressure</b> (typically above 1 bar) by convection, giving a <i>pressurised</i> He II bath. Large systems such as the LHC cool the pressurised bath with a two-phase He II <b>bayonet heat exchanger</b>.</li><li><b>Joule–Thomson expansion</b> of normal or supercritical helium into two-phase He II, which is saturated. A JT valve drops the supply to about 3.1 kPa, equivalent to 2.0 K, and counterflow heat exchangers use the cold vapour to pre-cool the incoming stream. Pre-cooling matters: the less warm the stream entering the valve, the more of it stays liquid after the isenthalpic expansion.</li></ul><p>A He II plant uses the same warm compressors, oil removal, purifiers, cold box (valves, heat exchangers, turbo-expanders), controls and distribution as a 4.5 K plant; the extra equipment is on the <b>return path</b>, to compress the sub-atmospheric vapour back up to ambient pressure.</p><h3>Common equipment</h3><ul><li><b>Warm compressors:</b> reciprocating or oil-flooded screw machines (twin helical rotors), followed by an oil removal system.</li><li><b>Turbo-expanders</b> in the cold box (gas-bearing turbines) and reciprocating expanders, as in all helium liquefiers.</li><li><b>Recuperative heat exchangers.</b> An ideal one recovers all the enthalpy (the two streams leave at the same temperature as the other stream's inlet, with no axial conduction and no pressure drop). Real ones have an effectiveness below one, conduct heat along the exchanger and drop pressure, and these losses are what set the plant efficiency.</li><li><b>Purifiers and cryogenic control valves.</b></li></ul><h3>Pumping the vapour down</h3><p>Pumping to a low vapour pressure is the heart of a He II plant. Three arrangements:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Weaknesses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Room-temperature pumping (Roots blower plus liquid-ring pump)</td><td>Low capital cost, naturally stable, easy turn-down</td><td>Limited capacity, high maintenance and operating cost, low reliability, limited run time</td></tr><tr><td>Cold compression (stages of cold compressors in series)</td><td>Unlimited cryogenic power, low maintenance, high reliability, low running cost</td><td>Narrow turn-down, delicate stability, higher capital cost</td></tr><tr><td>Hybrid (cold compressors plus warm vacuum pumps)</td><td>Combines both; wider turn-down</td><td>More complex</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A warm Roots blower handles about 20,000 normal m³/h at 10⁻⁴ to 20 torr, and a liquid-ring pump about 4,000 normal m³/h at 20 to 760 torr; oil injection takes away the heat of compression, and the machines are reworked for helium with helium-guarded seals. Cold compressors are high-speed turbo machines (40,000 to 100,000 rpm) with gas, ceramic ball or magnetic bearings, each giving a pressure ratio of about 2 to 4, so several are stacked in series.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=hsf viewBox='0 0 640 215' role=img aria-label='Vapour pumped from a 2 K helium II bath through cold compressor stages and warm pumps up to atmospheric pressure'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=hsq><b>Heat load on the 2 K bath = <span id=hsqv>2400</span> W</b></label><input type=range id=hsq min=1 max=4 step=0.02 value=3.38><label for=hst><b>Bath temperature</b></label><select id=hst><option value=1.8>1.8 K (1640 Pa)</option><option value=1.9>1.9 K (2300 Pa)</option><option value=2.0 selected>2.0 K (3130 Pa)</option></select><label for=hsr><b>Pressure ratio per cold compressor = <span id=hsrv>3.0</span></b></label><input type=range id=hsr min=2 max=4 step=0.1 value=3></div><div class=res id=hso></div></div><div class=cap id=hsn></div></div><p>The load and the mass flow are linked by Q = ṁ × H<sub>vap</sub>. The calculator uses H<sub>vap</sub> = 23 J/g, a typical value for He II near 2 K taken from standard property tables rather than from the lecture. The suction pressures are the lecture's. For a plant like the LHC sector (2.4 kW at 1.8 K) it finds a vapour flow of about 100 g/s and about four cold-compressor stages to reach atmosphere, matching the four-stage cold-compression units shown in the lecture.</p><h3>Cold versus hybrid: turn-down and temperature</h3><p>Because Q = ṁ H<sub>vap</sub>, capacity control is a mass-flow problem. A pure cold-compressor cycle keeps a nearly constant pressure ratio, so the flow can only vary over a narrow band: about 75% to 100% of the design. A hybrid cycle with a variable pressure ratio allows about 50% to 100%. The hybrid also reaches colder:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Bath temperature and suction pressure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cold compressors only</td><td>2.0 K at 3130 Pa; 1.96 K at 2780 Pa; 1.92 K at 2450 Pa</td></tr><tr><td>Hybrid</td><td>2.0 K at 3130 Pa; 1.9 K at 2300 Pa; 1.8 K at 1640 Pa</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In the lecture's comparison a cold-only plant cannot reach 1.9 K, while a hybrid supports any level from 1.8 K to 2.0 K.</p><h3>Large He II plants</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Facility</th><th>Start</th><th>Capacity</th><th>Scheme</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CEBAF (TJNAF)</td><td>1991</td><td>4,600 W at 2.1 K</td><td>Cold compression</td></tr><tr><td>SNS (ORNL)</td><td>2001</td><td>2,400 W at 2 K</td><td>Cold compression</td></tr><tr><td>LHC (CERN)</td><td>2000</td><td>2,400 W at 1.8 K</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>TJNAF 12 GeV</td><td>2008</td><td>4,600 W at 2.1 K</td><td>Cold compression</td></tr><tr><td>FRIB (MSU)</td><td>2013</td><td>4,500 W at 2.0 K</td><td>Cold compression</td></tr><tr><td>ESS</td><td>2013</td><td>2,000 W at 2 K</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>LCLS-II (SLAC)</td><td>2014</td><td>2 × 4,000 W at 2 K</td><td>Cold compression</td></tr><tr><td>Cryomodule Test Facility (Fermilab)</td><td>2010</td><td>500 W at 2.0 K</td><td>Hybrid (three cold compressors in series with five parallel vacuum pumps; can run at 1.8 K or 2.0 K)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>`,
q:"Why does a hybrid (cold plus warm pumping) He II plant allow deeper turn-down and colder baths than a cold-compressor-only plant?",o:["Because the pressure ratio can vary, so the mass flow (and the load Q = ṁ·H_vap) can be reduced to about 50% and lower suction pressures can be reached","Because it uses more helium","Because warm pumps are always more efficient"],a:0,e:"Cold compressors work in a narrow hydrodynamic field with a nearly constant pressure ratio; adding warm pumps lets the plant vary the ratio, giving a wider flow range and the lower pressures needed for 1.8 K."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryogenic controls and plant automation",lead:"From the 1963 first digital loop to today's PLCs and SCADA, and an interactive loop you can tune yourself.",h:`<h3>What a control system is</h3><p>A cryogenic plant is a physical process: compressors, a cold box, valves, dewars, a distribution system. The control system wraps a second, electronic system around it. <b>Sensors</b> convert physical conditions into (noisy) electrical signals. <b>Actuators</b> (valves, heaters, motor drives) convert signals into (imperfect) physical action. The <b>controller</b> uses what is known about the process to decide what the actuators should do, so that the plant approximates the behaviour you want. Sensors and actuators matter more than they seem: they decide what the controller can see and what it can influence.</p><p>Good control design starts with understanding the process, often through a mathematical model. A model quantifies insight and sometimes creates it, but you have to balance its accuracy against the insight it gives, and controller performance against how complicated it is to build and maintain.</p><h3>A short history</h3><ul><li><b>1963:</b> direct digital control at a petrochemical plant.</li><li><b>1970:</b> programmable logic controllers (PLCs) reach the market.</li><li><b>1975:</b> the first distributed control system (DCS), from Honeywell, bringing PID control, flexible software, a networked system with tuning and access from one operator station, and later auto-tuning.</li></ul><p>SCADA (supervisory control and data acquisition) sits on top for operators and data. Today industrial digital control is almost a commodity, so the difficulty is deploying and maintaining the huge number of loops in a big plant, not the algorithms (PID is still the workhorse).</p><h3>Try a PID loop</h3><p>A typical cryogenic loop is a thermal mass cooled through a valve: the process responds slowly (a first-order lag) and with a delay (the dead time for the cold stream to arrive). A PID controller adds a <b>proportional</b> term (react to the error now), an <b>integral</b> term (remove steady offset by accumulating past error) and a <b>derivative</b> term (react to how fast the error is changing). Tune the three below and look at how the process variable follows a step in the setpoint and then a heat-load disturbance at 60 seconds.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=pdf viewBox='0 0 640 235' role=img aria-label='Process variable response of a PID-controlled lag-plus-delay process to a setpoint step and a disturbance'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=pdp><b>Proportional gain K<sub>p</sub> = <span id=pdpv>1.5</span></b></label><input type=range id=pdp min=-1 max=1.3 step=0.02 value=0.18><label for=pdi><b>Integral time T<sub>i</sub> = <span id=pdiv>20</span> s</b></label><input type=range id=pdi min=3 max=200 step=1 value=20><label for=pdd><b>Derivative time T<sub>d</sub> = <span id=pddv>0.0</span> s</b></label><input type=range id=pdd min=0 max=10 step=0.5 value=0><label for=pdq><b>Disturbance at 60 s = <span id=pdqv>0.20</span></b></label><input type=range id=pdq min=0 max=0.4 step=0.01 value=0.2></div><div class=res id=pdo></div></div><div class=cap id=pdn></div></div><p>This simulation is an illustration, not a model of any particular plant: a process with a 20 s time constant and a 3 s dead time, a valve limited between closed and fully open, and a derivative term applied to the error. Too much gain makes the loop ring and eventually oscillate; too little leaves it sluggish; the integral term removes the lasting offset after the disturbance but slows the response if it is too strong.</p><h3>How a plant control system is built</h3><p>A large helium plant such as the cryogenic systems of PIP-II at Fermilab or the LCLS-II linac at SLAC shows the usual layers:</p><ul><li><b>I/O modules</b> interface with the hardware (resistance temperature inputs, analogue in and out, digital in and out) and convert raw readings into engineering units.</li><li><b>PLCs</b> are the main controllers: they run the limits and alarms, interlocks and bypasses, PID loops, a local display and some integrated functions (heater control, cold compressor pump-down). They are built for harsh industrial use, are compact, can be redundant, and have low failure rates. Control loops inside the PLC keep running even if the operator interface or the network goes down.</li><li><b>Input/output controllers (IOCs)</b> on a server publish every sensor value, setpoint, limit, alarm, interlock, bypass and PID parameter as named process variables, and can run automated sequences.</li><li><b>User interfaces</b> and high-level applications on operator machines show status, allow setpoint changes and automate processes through the same variables.</li><li><b>Networks</b> join PLCs to each other (for soft interlocks between subsystems, alarms and coordination) and to the supervisory layer, with field buses (Profibus DP, Profibus PA, EtherNet/IP) reaching valve positioners and temperature monitors.</li></ul><p>Positioners in a particle accelerator tunnel must tolerate radiation: for example a radiation-resistant pneumatic positioner design originally developed for the LHC is used inside the tunnel, with a commercial positioner outside the radiation area.</p><h3>From requirements to operation</h3><p>The lecture's development chain runs from process requirements (process calculations, operating modes) through the process flow diagram and the piping and instrumentation diagram (P&amp;ID) to a functional analysis, then to control loops, alarm, trip and interlock definitions, logic diagrams, PLC programs and operator displays (synoptics), and finally to an operating manual and procedures, then transfer to the operations team. Process engineers, process control engineers and controls engineers each own a stretch of the chain. Operating modes must be defined up front: commissioning modes (leak test, pressure test, controls checkout, purge and fill), normal modes such as plant start-up and cool-down, closed-loop purification, and outage modes for power, cooling water, instrument air, network or controls failure, plus an emergency shutdown. Each piece of equipment is marked against each mode so that nothing is forgotten.</p>`,
q:"Why are PID loops usually implemented inside the PLC rather than only on the operator server?",o:["So the loops keep running and protecting the plant even if the operator interface, server or network is down","Because PLCs are cheaper than servers","Because PID cannot run on a server"],a:0,e:"Local control in the PLC makes the plant robust: limits, interlocks and loops continue to operate regardless of supervisory-layer failures."},
{t:"Advanced: Liquid argon detectors and DUNE",lead:"Hunting neutrinos and dark matter with thousands of tonnes of argon purified to parts per trillion.",h:`<h3>Why liquid argon?</h3><p>Argon is 0.93% of the atmosphere by volume (1.2% by mass), so it is abundant and cheap: the lectures quote about $1.15 per kg for liquid argon, against about $3,393 per kg for xenon gas, roughly three thousand times more. It boils at 87.3 K at 1 atm, so inexpensive LN₂ (77.4 K) can keep it liquid. It is an excellent scintillator that is transparent to its own light, and ionisation electrons can drift metres through it under an electric field with millimetre-scale spreading. And mass matters, because the targets are rare events: neutrino interactions, dark-matter collisions and double-beta decay.</p><p>Liquid argon packs matter densely. A saturated liquid at 87.6 K has a density of 1,393 kg/m³, against 5.965 kg/m³ for the saturated gas and 1.723 kg/m³ for gas at 293 K. Try the volumes:</p><div class=calc><label for=lam><b>Argon mass = <span id=lamv>700</span> tonnes</b></label><input type=range id=lam min=1 max=5 step=0.02 value=2.845><div class=res id=lao></div><div class=cap id=lan></div></div><h3>How a liquid-argon time projection chamber works</h3><p>A charged particle crossing the liquid leaves a trail of ionised atoms and excited atoms. The <b>ionisation electrons</b> drift in an electric field to wire planes at the anode; because the arrival times and wire positions give the track in three dimensions, the detector is a time projection chamber (TPC), a concept proposed by Rubbia in 1977. The <b>scintillation light</b> is flashed at the moment of the interaction and seen by photon detectors, which give the time stamp. The trouble is that electrons drift for about a millisecond. In that time an electron makes about 10¹³ collisions per second, so any electronegative impurity (oxygen, water) will capture it; the resulting negative ion moves so slowly that its signal is lost in noise. Light is spoiled differently: nitrogen quenches the scintillation, and methane or CO₂ absorbs it.</p><h3>The purity budget: parts per trillion</h3><p>The charge that survives a drift of time t is Q = Q₀ e<sup>−t/τ</sup>, where τ is the electron lifetime. For electric fields below a few hundred V/cm the lectures give τ(µs) ≈ 300 / [O₂ in ppb], so 1 ppb of oxygen means a 0.3 ms lifetime, and a 3 ms lifetime needs just 0.1 ppb, i.e. 100 parts per trillion. Move the oxygen level up and down and watch electrons get captured on their way to the anode. Surviving electrons arrive at the wires; red rings mark captures.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=laf viewBox='0 0 640 260' role=img aria-label='Ionisation electrons drifting across a liquid argon detector; some are captured by oxygen impurities depending on the purity'></svg><div class=calc><label for=lao2><b>Oxygen-equivalent impurity = <span id=lao2v>0.1</span> ppb</b></label><input type=range id=lao2 min=-2 max=2 step=0.05 value=-1><label for=lat><b>Drift time = <span id=latv>2.0</span> ms</b></label><input type=range id=lat min=0.2 max=6 step=0.1 value=2><div class=res id=lar></div><svg class=sv id=lac viewBox='0 0 340 190' role=img aria-label='Electron lifetime against oxygen-equivalent impurity, with experiment markers'></svg><div class=cap id=lai></div></div></div><h3>What was achieved</h3><ul><li>The ICARUS detector at Gran Sasso (two 600-tonne modules) reached lifetimes above 12 ms.</li><li>ProtoDUNE-SP at CERN (740 tonnes) consistently exceeded 30 ms, about 10 ppt of oxygen-equivalent. Pump stoppages degraded purity every time, and it recovered when circulation resumed.</li><li>The DUNE requirement is under 100 ppt, about a 3 ms lifetime.</li></ul><h3>Keeping it pure</h3><ul><li><b>Start with good argon.</b> Purchased liquid argon contains about 1 ppm of oxygen, nitrogen and water (specifications allow 5 ppm O₂ and 10 ppm N₂ and water), often ten times better. Nitrogen is left alone; water and oxygen are removed.</li><li><b>Remove water</b> with 4A molecular sieve, about 22 g of water per kg of sieve (bulk density 705 kg/m³); regenerate above 450 K but below 500 K.</li><li><b>Remove oxygen</b> with activated copper pellets, 0.5 to 0.7 g of O₂ per kg (about 200 m²/g surface). The copper oxidises (2Cu + O₂ → 2CuO) and is regenerated at 450 to 500 K in 1 to 2% hydrogen in argon (CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O, ΔH = −84.5 kJ/mol). Too much heat from that reaction is a risk, and regeneration temperature profiles in practice must be watched.</li><li><b>Circulate the liquid</b> at first about one volume per ten days, then slow down once the argon is pure. <b>Never stop circulating the gas</b>: contaminants outgas into the warm argon vapour at the top and dissolve straight back into the liquid, so the gas is recondensed outside the cryostat and purified, not vented.</li></ul><p>Size a purifier with the lecture's method: oxygen mass equals the argon gas volume at STP times the impurity fraction times oxygen's density, and media mass equals oxygen mass divided by the per-kilogram capacity.</p><div class=calc><label for=lap><b>Argon to purify = <span id=lapv>100</span> tonnes</b></label><input type=range id=lap min=0 max=3 step=0.05 value=2><label for=lax><b>Oxygen in the argon = <span id=laxv>1.0</span> ppm</b></label><input type=range id=lax min=0.1 max=10 step=0.1 value=1><div class=res id=lau></div><div class=cap id=lae></div></div><h3>Materials, cooldown and watching it happen</h3><ul><li><b>Materials.</b> In the cold liquid almost any material that survives the temperature is acceptable, because the vapour pressure of everything is tiny at 87 K; what matters is floating (PTFE at 2,200 kg/m³ sinks, PEEK at 1,260 kg/m³ floats), peeling and cracking from contraction (metals shrink about 2.5 to 4 mm/m, polymers 25 to 30 mm/m). In the warm gas, avoid materials with high water content such as G-10 and nylon. The Fermilab Materials Test Stand measured the outgassing and lifetime effect of over seventy components.</li><li><b>Temperature uniformity.</b> The electron drift speed changes by about −2% per kelvin, so the argon must stay within about 0.1 K of uniform. Hydrostatic head matters: 20 cm of argon adds about 26 mbar, enough to superheat the liquid by about 230 mK, so a thermometer coming out of the liquid reads higher. ProtoDUNE kept the temperature gradient to a few millikelvin when circulation was on.</li><li><b>Cooldown</b> no faster than 50 K/hour and under 50 K between any two points, because the detector mixes stainless steel, G-10 and fine copper-beryllium wires (150 µm, held at 5 N against 25.6 N breaking tension) with different contraction. Liquid is sprayed in at the top; computational fluid dynamics predicted ProtoDUNE's cooldown closely.</li><li><b>Cameras</b> inside the liquid, in evacuated polycarbonate or stainless housings with heated windows and LED lighting, verify alignment during cooldown and fill and check for bubbling near ground planes.</li></ul><h3>DUNE: the largest argon plant</h3><p>The Long-Baseline Neutrino Facility will send a beam from Fermilab to the Deep Underground Neutrino Experiment, a mile underground at the Sanford Underground Research Facility in South Dakota. The far detector is four modules of 17,450 tonnes of liquid argon each (the project builds two first). The lectures give these numbers per cryostat:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cryostat</td><td>Membrane type: a 1.2 mm 304L stainless membrane inside 800 mm of polyurethane insulation, about 65.8 m × 17.9 m × 18.9 m</td></tr><tr><td>Static heat leak</td><td>48.7 kW</td></tr><tr><td>Electronics heat</td><td>23.7 kW</td></tr><tr><td>Total with 2 / 4 circulation pumps running</td><td>87.1 / 98.2 kW</td></tr><tr><td>Condensers per cryostat</td><td>3 × 100 kW, cooled by LN₂ refrigeration units</td></tr><tr><td>Nominal LAr circulation</td><td>0.43 m³/min (10 kg/s) with one pump; up to 1.73 m³/min with four (a five-day turnover)</td></tr><tr><td>Required purity</td><td>100 ppt oxygen equivalent (about 3.2 ms); ProtoDUNE achieved under 10 ppt</td></tr><tr><td>Argon receiving</td><td>4 × 50 m³ storage tanks (280 tonnes), offloading pumps and vaporisers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For scale: 87.1 kW divided by argon's latent heat of about 161 kJ/kg is roughly 0.54 kg/s of boil-off, about 47 tonnes a day or 0.27% of the inventory a day, all of which must be recondensed and purified. The design deliberately has no cryogens in the shaft: argon is vaporised and moved as gas, then recondensed underground. The cryostat pressure is protected by an inline safety valve whose seat sits inside the cryostat, below the liquid level.</p>`,
q:"Why is purity measured in parts per trillion for a liquid argon TPC?",o:["Electrons drift for milliseconds and any electronegative impurity such as oxygen captures them, so even 0.1 ppb cuts the signal","Argon is toxic at higher purity","The detector wires dissolve in impurities"],a:0,e:"Electron lifetime is about 300 µs divided by the oxygen-equivalent ppb, so a drift of a few milliseconds needs sub-ppb oxygen."},
{t:"Advanced: Superconducting RF cavities for accelerators",lead:"How a niobium cavity at 2 K loses a hundred-millionth of the power of a copper one, and what that costs in refrigeration.",h:`<h3>A refresher on superconductivity</h3><p>Kamerlingh Onnes liquefied helium in 1908 and found the superconductivity of mercury in 1911. Below a <b>critical temperature</b> T<sub>c</sub> the DC resistance drops to zero, and the material expels magnetic fields (the Meissner effect) because screening currents flow in a thin layer, the London penetration depth. The material stays superconducting up to a critical magnetic field H<sub>c</sub>(T) = H<sub>c</sub>(0)[1 − (T/T<sub>c</sub>)²]. Type II superconductors let field in as vortices between a lower critical field H<sub>c1</sub> and an upper critical field H<sub>c2</sub>; each vortex carries one flux quantum, and moving vortices dissipate power. For RF fields a perfect surface can stay in the Meissner state up to a superheating field above H<sub>c1</sub>, the fundamental limit on the gradient of a cavity.</p><h3>How a cavity accelerates</h3><p>A particle of charge q crossing a voltage V gains energy qV; with an accelerating gradient E<sub>acc</sub> across a gap of length L the gain is qE<sub>acc</sub>L. An RF cavity stores electromagnetic energy in a resonant mode, oscillating between the electric and magnetic fields. To give maximum acceleration, a particle must cross a cell in half an RF period, so cell length is βλ/2. In the π-mode of a multi-cell cavity, neighbouring cells have opposite field; as the bunch moves one cell, the field has reversed, so it always sees an accelerating field. Watch it, and slow the clock with the controls:</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=srf viewBox='0 0 640 230' role=img aria-label='A nine-cell cavity with alternating electric field; a bunch crosses a cell every half RF period and always sees an accelerating field'></svg><button class=btn id=srb>Pause</button><div class=cap>Arrows show the on-axis electric field in each cell (blue up, red down). The bar shows the energy gained by the bunch as it passes through the cavity.</div></div><h3>Why superconducting?</h3><p>Power is dissipated by surface currents driven by the magnetic field: P = ½ R<sub>s</sub> ∮ H² dS. The <b>quality factor</b> Q₀ = G/R<sub>s</sub> is 2π times the stored energy over the energy lost per cycle; G is a geometry factor and the typical Q₀ is about 10⁴ for a copper cavity and about 10¹⁰ for a niobium one at 2 K. The cavity voltage and power are linked by the shunt impedance R/Q (set by the shape alone): P<sub>c</sub> = V<sub>c</sub>² / ((R/Q) Q₀). So power falls in proportion to R<sub>s</sub>, and shape optimisation tries to maximise G·(R/Q).</p><p>The surface resistance of a superconductor is not zero in an RF field. It has a temperature-dependent part from thermally excited quasiparticles (BCS theory) and a residual part from impurities, trapped flux and hydrides. For niobium below about T<sub>c</sub>/2 an approximate formula is R<sub>s</sub> ≈ 2×10⁻⁴ (f/1500)² (1/T) exp(−17.67/T) + R<sub>res</sub>, in ohm with f in MHz, and R<sub>res</sub> is typically 1 to 10 nΩ. Only about 100 nm of the surface matters, which is why final surface preparation, electropolishing, baking and nitrogen doping decide the performance. Explore what temperature and surface quality do for a TESLA-type 1.3 GHz nine-cell cavity, and what that means at the wall socket.</p><div class=fig><div class=two><div class=calc><label for=srt><b>Bath temperature = <span id=srtv>2.00</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=srt min=150 max=450 step=5 value=200><label for=srr><b>Residual resistance = <span id=srrv>5</span> nΩ</b></label><input type=range id=srr min=1 max=20 step=0.5 value=5><label for=sre><b>Accelerating gradient = <span id=srev>20</span> MV/m</b></label><input type=range id=sre min=5 max=40 step=1 value=20></div><svg class=sv id=src viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='Q0 against temperature for a niobium cavity'></svg></div><div class=res id=sro></div><div class=cap id=srn></div></div><p>Assumptions: f = 1300 MHz, G = 270 Ω and R/Q = 1036 Ω (typical values for a nine-cell TESLA cavity, quoted from standard references, not the lectures), length 1.038 m; cryoplant efficiency 25% of Carnot, similar to the 4×10⁻³ coefficient of performance quoted for 4.5 K in the lectures.</p><h3>Why niobium, and what could replace it</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>T<sub>c</sub> (K)</th><th>H<sub>c</sub> (Oe), or H<sub>c2</sub> in brackets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nb</td><td>9.25</td><td>2,060</td></tr><tr><td>Pb</td><td>7.19</td><td>803</td></tr><tr><td>Ta</td><td>4.47</td><td>829</td></tr><tr><td>Sn</td><td>3.72</td><td>305</td></tr><tr><td>In</td><td>3.41</td><td>281</td></tr><tr><td>Al</td><td>1.18</td><td>105</td></tr><tr><td>NbTi</td><td>10.2</td><td>(120,000)</td></tr><tr><td>NbZr</td><td>10.8</td><td>(110,000)</td></tr><tr><td>Nb₃Sn</td><td>18.1</td><td>5,200 (240,000)</td></tr><tr><td>MgB₂</td><td>39.0</td><td>4,290</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Niobium has the highest critical temperature of any element, high H<sub>c1</sub> and H<sub>c</sub>, a theoretical Meissner breakdown field near 240 mT, low residual resistance, and is easy to form. Nb₃Sn reaches Q₀ above 10¹⁰ at 4.2 K but has so far been limited to about 20 MV/m; alloys like NbTi are too lossy for cavities, and high-temperature superconductors show very high surface resistance. For high Q at 2 K or the highest gradient (above 45 MV/m) nothing yet beats niobium.</p><h3>Making and cooling a cavity</h3><p>Cavities are made from niobium sheets several millimetres thick, deep-drawn or spun into half-cells, electron-beam welded into cells and then into a full structure, then cleaned and etched; at least 150 µm is removed by electropolishing for state-of-the-art performance. A recipe such as a 120 °C bake, nitrogen doping or low-temperature baking tunes the surface, and the performance test is a measurement of Q against E<sub>acc</sub>, which shows three regions: a low-field slope, a medium-field slope and a high-field slope. The finished cavity is welded into a titanium or stainless-steel helium vessel. Elliptical cells serve particles with β near 1; quarter-wave, half-wave and spoke resonators serve slower ones. The helium-filled cavities sit inside a <b>cryomodule</b>: a cryostat with thermal shield, MLI, magnetic shield, power coupler, tuner and higher-order-mode dampers. Long linacs such as the European XFEL and LCLS-II use a continuous string of cryomodules with an internal cryogenic transfer line; others use separate modules fed from outside.</p><h3>The cost of cold: normal against superconducting</h3><p>Copper cavity losses grow as the square of the voltage, so for continuous-wave operation at high gradient a superconducting cavity wins even though cold power is expensive. The lectures compare two 500 MHz RF systems for a storage ring that must give 300 kW to the beam:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Normal-conducting</th><th>Superconducting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cavity wall power</td><td>278 kW</td><td>65 W dynamic + 30 W static</td></tr><tr><td>Total RF power needed</td><td>578 kW</td><td>300 kW</td></tr><tr><td>Cryoplant COP at 4.5 K</td><td>none</td><td>4×10⁻³</td></tr><tr><td>Total wall-plug (AC) power</td><td>1,156 kW</td><td>624 kW</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The superconducting system needs fewer cavities and less power; its larger beam aperture also lowers the beam impedance, which reduces disruption of the beam, and its design is free from the pressure to minimise wall losses.</p>`,
q:"Why does Q₀ rise so sharply when a niobium cavity is cooled from 4.2 K to 2 K?",o:["The thermally excited (BCS) part of the surface resistance falls exponentially with temperature","Niobium becomes a normal conductor","The cavity gets shorter"],a:0,e:"R_BCS falls roughly as exp(−17.67/T), so below about 2 K it is dwarfed by the residual resistance, and Q₀ reaches 10¹⁰ or more."},
{t:"Advanced: Cryogenics for dark matter and the cosmic microwave background",lead:"Thermometers that sit on the edge of a superconducting transition, nested cans at 15 millikelvin, and detectors counting heat from single particles.",h:`<h3>Why cryogenics reaches the edge of physics</h3><p>The sky and the lab need the same trick: heat from a single particle or a few photons is tiny, so make the detector so cold that its own thermal noise is lower still. Two science cases from the lectures show the engineering.</p><h3>Dark matter: waiting for a few events a year</h3><p>Galaxy rotation curves stay flat far beyond the visible disc; colliding galaxy clusters (the Bullet Cluster) show mass, inferred from gravitational lensing, passing straight through the hot gas; and simulations show that structure forms in the right way only if most matter is invisible. A favoured candidate is a weakly interacting massive particle (WIMP) that scatters off a nucleus like a billiard ball, depositing energy as charge, light or heat. Expected rates are a few events per year in current experiments, so the design must: detect very small energies, reject backgrounds, and have large mass and patience.</p><ul><li><b>Go deep.</b> Cosmic-ray muon flux falls by orders of magnitude with depth: the Soudan mine is about half a mile down, SNOLAB in Ontario 1.5 miles. The whole SNOLAB laboratory is a clean room, because dust carries radioactivity.</li><li><b>Layered shielding.</b> The CDMS shield used, from outside in: plastic-scintillator muon veto, 40 cm polyethylene, 18 cm lead, 4.5 cm ancient lead, and 10 cm polyethylene; the cryostat adds about 1.9 cm of copper.</li><li><b>Cryogenic detectors.</b> Germanium and silicon crystals (76 mm diameter, 25 mm thick) are cooled so that a particle recoil releases both electric charge and crystal vibrations (phonons, i.e. heat). Aluminium fins 300 nm thick absorb the phonons; the energy passes as quasiparticles into tungsten transition-edge sensors (TES), read out by SQUIDs. CDMS ran at about 50 mK at Soudan from 2003 to 2015; SuperCDMS SNOLAB, planned for 2020 to 2030, runs at about 15 mK.</li></ul><p>Other cryogenic dark-matter experiments use liquid xenon (162 K), liquid argon (85 K), bubble chambers, silicon CCDs (140 K), scintillating crystals and an RF-cavity axion search at 130 mK.</p><h3>Getting to 15 millikelvin</h3><p>A home refrigerator condenses a gas and expands it to carry heat away. A <b>dilution refrigerator</b> works on the same principle, but with a mixture of the two helium isotopes, and reaches about 0.01 K. In the experiments here, large copper cans, each colder than the last, nest one inside another and hang from the next outer can on Kevlar ropes, which have little conduction. Look at the stack:</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=dmc viewBox='0 0 640 300' role=img aria-label='Nested cryostat cans from 300 K down to 15 millikelvin with the detector tower in the centre'></svg><div class=cap>The 50 K and 4 K stages are cooled by Gifford–McMahon cryocoolers; the 1 K still, 250 mK cold plate and 15 to 30 mK mixing chamber belong to the dilution refrigerator. Every layer is suspended from the next outer layer on Kevlar ropes; all materials must be radio-pure.</div></div><p>The cryogenic challenges named in the lectures: wet fridges need daily helium and nitrogen refills underground, which led to recapture and recondensation; impurities in the circulating mixture must be trapped in cold traps that need regular cleaning; long stems and many joints make it hard to hold detectors below 50 mK, and joint conduction degrades with air exposure unless gold-plated; thermal contraction can make cans touch during cooldown; and pumps and mechanical coolers shake the detectors. CDMS at Soudan ran for 12 years, once for 1.5 years continuously at base temperature.</p><h3>The cosmic microwave background</h3><p>The microwave background is light from about 400,000 years after the Big Bang, and holds more than 90% of the energy in light in the universe; COBE found it smooth to one part in 100,000 and a near-perfect 3 K blackbody. Mapping its faint temperature and polarisation patterns tells us about inflation, neutrinos and dark energy. The South Pole Telescope sits at the South Pole because its winter water vapour is about four times lower than at Chile's Atacama plateau (and six times lower than at Mauna Kea), and the sky is roughly 30 times more stable than in Chile during the six-month night. Progress has come from detector technology: 16 detectors in 2001, 960 in 2007, 1,600 in 2012 and about 16,200 in the SPT-3G camera of 2017, with the proposed CMB-S4 reaching 500,000.</p><p>The detector is a <b>bolometer</b>: an absorber of heat capacity C heated by incoming power P<sub>opt</sub>, joined to a cold bath by a weak thermal link G, with a thermistor reading its temperature. The temperature rise is P<sub>opt</sub>/G, and the time constant is C/G. The design goal is thermal noise below photon noise. The first bolometers used a hand-made bismuth absorber on sapphire, cooled to 300 mK; modern ones use a superconducting <b>transition-edge sensor</b>, a metal bilayer (for example Al/Ti or Mo/Au) 30 to 100 nm thick whose superconducting transition, near 500 mK, is only about 10 mK wide. Biased in the middle of the transition, a tiny temperature rise makes a big change in resistance. Try it:</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=tsf viewBox='0 0 640 250' role=img aria-label='Resistance against temperature across a superconducting transition showing the operating point with and without electrothermal feedback'></svg><div class=calc><label for=tsg><b>Thermal link G = <span id=tsgv>80</span> pW/K</b></label><input type=range id=tsg min=20 max=200 step=2 value=80><label for=tsp><b>Absorbed optical power = <span id=tspv>6</span> pW</b></label><input type=range id=tsp min=0 max=25 step=0.5 value=6><label for=tsl><b>Electrothermal loop gain = <span id=tslv>100</span></b></label><input type=range id=tsl min=0 max=3 step=0.05 value=2><div class=res id=tso></div><div class=cap id=tsn></div></div></div><p>Loop gain is the key advantage over germanium thermistors (loop gain about 1 to 5, a resistance of megaohms and a high sensitivity to microphonics): a TES has loop gain of about 20 to 1000, a resistance near an ohm (so low microphonics), and can be fabricated directly on the bolometer. Currents are read by SQUIDs, with 0.2 nV/√Hz of voltage noise, and many detectors share one line by frequency-domain multiplexing: each detector sits at the resonance of its own LC filter in the 300 to 1000 kHz range, and the LC quality factor keeps crosstalk below 1%. In SPT-3G, each pixel has an antenna in the centre that sends power to six TES bolometers, measuring three colours (95, 150, 220 GHz) and two polarisations, all cooled to about 0.3 K inside a camera of roughly one tonne.</p>`,
q:"Why does a transition-edge sensor work as a thermometer?",o:["It is biased in the middle of a very narrow superconducting transition, so a tiny temperature change gives a large resistance change","It is made of a semiconductor","It is cooled below the transition so its resistance is zero"],a:0,e:"A 10 mK transition width means a few millikelvin moves the resistance from near zero to the normal value, so the sensor is extremely sensitive, and feedback keeps it in the transition."},
{t:"Advanced: Below 1 kelvin and pumped helium refrigerators",lead:"Why one kelvin is the line between industrial and laboratory cryogenics, and how a pump on a pot of helium gets you to 0.3 K.",h:`<h3>Why one kelvin is a boundary</h3><p>Helium-4 has the lowest boiling point of the common cryogens, 4.21 K at one atmosphere, and the only practical way to go colder with a liquid is to lower its vapour pressure. The large superfluid plants met earlier run at about 10 mbar, roughly 2 K, and can pump hard enough to lift around 100 W. A laboratory cryostat with its own pump reaches about 1 K, but the pump is small, so the cooling power is only about 10 mW. One kelvin is a soft boundary, but below it you are no longer doing industrial-scale cryogenics: you are doing small, delicate laboratory work where every microwatt matters.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Range</th><th>Name</th><th>How you get there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.1 K to 1 K</td><td>Sub-kelvin</td><td>Pumped helium-4 or helium-3 cryostats, magnetic cooling</td></tr><tr><td>1 mK to 100 mK</td><td>Millikelvin</td><td>Dilution refrigerators, magnetic cooling</td></tr><tr><td>1 µK to 1 mK</td><td>Microkelvin</td><td>Nuclear magnetic cooling</td></tr><tr><td>Below 1 µK</td><td>Nanokelvin</td><td>Atom traps and laser cooling (these cool the atoms themselves, not a physical object)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>A short history</h3><ul><li><b>1908:</b> helium is first liquefied, but sub-kelvin temperatures had to wait for pumps. In 1922 Kamerlingh Onnes reached 830 mK with an enormous battery of pumps.</li><li><b>1926 to 1933:</b> Debye and Giauque propose magnetic cooling; it is demonstrated in 1933 at 530 mK, then 270 mK.</li><li><b>1951 to 1966:</b> London proposes the dilution refrigerator; it first works in 1965 to 1966.</li><li><b>1972:</b> nuclear demagnetisation goes below 1 mK; by the late 1990s equilibrium temperatures of 1 µK are routine.</li><li><b>1995:</b> trap cooling below 1 µK reveals the Bose–Einstein condensate.</li></ul><p>Why bother? Cold means quiet. Sensors for faint far-infrared and millimetre light, dark-matter crystals, and superconducting quantum bits all need thermal noise pushed far below anything a 4 K machine can offer.</p><h3>The two helium isotopes</h3><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>⁴He</th><th>³He</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Boiling point at 1 atm</td><td>4.21 K</td><td>3.19 K</td></tr><tr><td>Critical temperature</td><td>5.20 K</td><td>3.32 K</td></tr><tr><td>Superfluid transition</td><td>2.1768 K</td><td>about 2.5 mK</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid density</td><td>0.1451 g/cm³</td><td>0.082 g/cm³</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat at the normal boiling point</td><td>20.9 kJ/kg</td><td>8.49 kJ/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Molar volume (actual)</td><td>27.58 cm³/mol</td><td>36.84 cm³/mol</td></tr><tr><td>Gas-to-liquid expansion ratio</td><td>866</td><td>662</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Helium-3 keeps a higher vapour pressure than helium-4 over the whole range, because it does not turn superfluid until a couple of millikelvin. That is exactly why it can reach lower temperatures when you pump on it: about 0.3 K, against about 1 K for helium-4. The price is steep: helium-4 costs roughly $1 per standard litre of gas, helium-3 about $1000 per standard litre from the US Department of Energy isotope programme and up to $3000 on the open market, so helium-3 machines are always sealed and recirculating.</p><h3>The pumped helium-4 pot</h3><p>The simplest design connects a bath of liquid helium-4 to a pump and lets the liquid cool as it boils. It works, but about half of the liquid evaporates during the pump-down from 4 K to 1 K, and a big pump is needed. The better design uses a small <b>pot</b> fed from an atmospheric helium bath through a narrow <b>flow impedance</b>. The pump only has to handle the pot, and the cooling power is the balance between the heat that reaches the pot (mainly down the pumping tube) and the experiment's own load. The pot is also <b>self-regulating</b>: if the load rises, the liquid level falls, and less heat creeps in down the wetted tube.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=sbp viewBox='0 0 640 235' role=img aria-label='A pumped helium-4 pot fed from a 4 K bath through a flow impedance, with the pump removing vapour'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=sbq><b>Heat load on the pot = <span id=sbqv>5.0</span> mW</b></label><input type=range id=sbq min=-1 max=1 step=0.02 value=0.7><label for=sbv><b>Liquid in the pot = <span id=sbvv>2.0</span> cm³</b></label><input type=range id=sbv min=0.5 max=10 step=0.1 value=2></div><div class=res id=sbo></div></div><div class=cap id=sbn></div></div><p>The evaporation rate follows from the latent heat: V̇ = Q̇ / (ρ L). For 5 mW at 1.5 K the lecture finds about 5.5 cm³ of liquid per hour, or about 5 standard litres of gas per hour; this calculator uses L = 22.5 J/g and ρ = 0.145 g/cm³, which are the values implied by that example. Note how small the gas flow is. That is why a modest pump will do: at 1.5 K the vapour pressure is only about 5 mbar, easy for a mechanical pump.</p><h3>Choosing the impedance</h3><p>The impedance Z is the pressure drop divided by the volume flow rate and the viscosity. If Z is too large the pot runs dry; if it is too small, too much warm liquid arrives and the pot never gets cold. A long capillary does the job, and for laminar flow in a round tube Z = 8 l / (π R⁴). Because of the fourth power, small errors in radius matter enormously, and measured values typically agree with the formula only to within a factor of two.</p><div class=calc><label for=sbl><b>Capillary length = <span id=sblv>3.0</span> m</b></label><input type=range id=sbl min=0.5 max=10 step=0.1 value=3><label for=sbr><b>Capillary radius = <span id=sbrv>0.100</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=sbr min=0.05 max=0.3 step=0.005 value=0.1><div class=res id=sbz></div><div class=cap>The lecture quotes a typical impedance of about 10¹¹ cm⁻³, equivalent to a few metres of capillary. Taking 0.1 mm as the radius reproduces that. Double the radius and Z drops sixteen-fold.</div></div><h3>Pumps that live in the cold: sorption coolers</h3><p>You do not need a room-temperature pump. A cryogenic <b>getter</b> pump, usually activated charcoal, adsorbs helium at low temperature and releases it when warmed. Heat the charcoal to 20 to 40 K and it drives the gas off, which condenses in the cooler; cool it below about 10 K and it pumps the pot. At the pressure of a 1 K refrigerator (about 0.1 torr) one gram of charcoal at 4 K holds about 5 litres of helium gas. The example calculator above uses that figure to tell you how much charcoal your pot needs.</p><ul><li><b>Pros:</b> compact and self-contained, mechanically simple, and no heat from a warm pump line, so the base temperature can be lower.</li><li><b>Cons:</b> usually one-shot (a continuous sorption cooler needs several pumps and valves and becomes very complicated), and low pumping speed limits the cooling power.</li><li><b>Where they shine:</b> places where nobody can top up a pump, such as stratospheric balloons, sounding rockets and space telescopes. Multi-stage versions put one or more helium-3 stages after a helium-4 stage.</li></ul><h3>Helium-3 refrigerators</h3><p>A helium-3 refrigerator has the same idea, one temperature lower. Since helium-3 is too precious to vent, the gas must be condensed from a pumped helium-4 pot at 1 to 2 K. Three layouts are common:</p><ul><li><b>Single-shot:</b> a pumped helium-4 bath plus a one-shot helium-3 pot. Simple, but wasteful of helium-4 and not continuous.</li><li><b>Atmospheric bath plus pumped pot:</b> better use of helium-4, but the helium-3 stage is still discontinuous.</li><li><b>Fully recirculating:</b> a helium-3 pot that is continuously condensed and pumped. Continuous cooling, at the cost of a complicated condenser and a warm pump system.</li></ul><p>Typical performance for a helium-4 and helium-3 pair in a pulse-tube-cooled (<q>cryogen-free</q>) machine is about 10 mW at 1.5 K and about 1 mW at 0.4 K. Each of these fridges shows an <b>overload state</b> (the liquid is being used up and the pot runs for a while above its rating) and a <b>stationary state</b> (when the liquid has gone the pot can still hold a little load, but at a higher temperature). Below roughly 0.3 K, a different physical process is needed, and the next lessons describe it.</p>`,
q:"Why can a helium-3 pot reach a lower temperature than a helium-4 pot at the same pumping speed?",o:["Helium-3 stays at higher vapour pressure at low temperature because it has no superfluid transition until about 2.5 mK","Helium-3 is heavier so it flows faster","Helium-3 has a higher boiling point"],a:0,e:"Helium-4's vapour pressure collapses below its lambda point; helium-3's stays higher down to the millikelvin range, so pumping on it can reach about 0.3 K."},
{t:"Advanced: Dilution refrigerators",lead:"The only machine that gives continuous cooling into the millikelvin range, and the workhorse behind quantum computing.",h:`<h3>Cooling from dissolving one isotope in another</h3><p>A dilution refrigerator (DR) is the workhorse of millikelvin science: the only technology that reaches millikelvin temperatures continuously, with high cooling power even at the upper end of the sub-kelvin range. Its cooling comes from the <b>heat of mixing</b> of helium-3 and helium-4, which behave differently because of quantum statistics. Helium-4 (two protons, two neutrons) is a boson and condenses into the ground state; helium-3 (one fewer neutron) is a fermion and obeys the Pauli exclusion principle. Helium-3 is rare, produced mostly by tritium decay (12-year half life) from the weapons programme, and costs of the order of $1000 per standard litre.</p><h3>The strange phase diagram</h3><p>Write the concentration as x = n₃/(n₃ + n₄). Dissolving helium-3 in helium-4 lowers the superfluid transition temperature, and the transition disappears altogether above x = 67.5%. A mixture richer than 6.6% helium-3 separates on cooling into two phases:</p><ul><li>a <b>concentrated phase</b> that approaches pure helium-3 (x → 100%), which is lighter and floats on top;</li><li>a <b>dilute phase</b> that approaches x = 6.6% helium-3 and does not fall to zero even at absolute zero. This finite solubility is the key to the whole machine.</li></ul><p>Why does helium-3 dissolve in helium-4 at all at T = 0? A helium-3 atom has larger zero-point motion than a helium-4 atom (it is lighter), so it is more strongly bound in liquid helium-4 than in liquid helium-3. As more helium-3 is added, the Pauli principle forces extra atoms into higher and higher energy states, up to the Fermi energy (the Fermi temperature of the dilute helium-3 is about 1 K, compared with 10,000 K for electrons in a metal), and the binding energy falls until, at 6.6%, the chemical potentials of the two phases match. In the dilute phase, the helium-4 is an inert superfluid background with zero heat capacity: it adds to the volume and the effective mass of the helium-3 but nothing else.</p><h3>How the machine works</h3><p>The simplest picture is a U-tube. One side holds the concentrated phase, the other the dilute phase. Pumping helium-3 away from the surface of the dilute phase pulls it below its equilibrium 6.6%, so helium-3 atoms cross the phase boundary from the concentrated phase to restore the balance. Each atom that crosses goes from a lower-entropy, ordered state to a higher-entropy dilute state, and this absorbs heat just as evaporation does, but without needing a vapour space. Osmotic pressure drives the flow of helium-3 up the dilute column to the <b>still</b>, where it is evaporated, and a room-temperature pump returns it, condensed, to the cold concentrated side. Move the sliders to see the loop run.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=dlf viewBox='0 0 640 305' role=img aria-label='Circulation loop of helium-3 in a dry dilution refrigerator through the cold stages to the mixing chamber and back through the still'></svg><div class=two><svg class=sv id=dlc viewBox='0 0 330 175' role=img aria-label='Cooling power against mixing chamber temperature compared with the heat load'></svg><div><label for=dln><b>³He circulation ṅ₃ = <span id=dlnv>300</span> µmol/s</b></label><input type=range id=dln min=1 max=3.5 step=0.02 value=2.48><label for=dlt><b>Mixing chamber T = <span id=dltv>20</span> mK</b></label><input type=range id=dlt min=5 max=100 step=1 value=20><label for=dlq><b>Heat load on the plate = <span id=dlqv>1.0</span> µW</b></label><input type=range id=dlq min=-2 max=2 step=0.05 value=0><div class=res id=dlo></div></div></div><div class=cap id=dlk></div></div><h3>The still</h3><p>The still pumps the helium-3 away. Its best operating temperature is about 600 to 700 mK; left to itself the vapour pressure would drop and pull it toward 400 mK, so a heater makes up the difference. Helium-3 arrives at the temperature of the condenser and leaves at the still temperature, so the still power is set by the enthalpy balance: roughly 40 times the circulation rate in watts, with the rate in mol/s, a few milliwatts for typical flows. A baffle suppresses the superfluid film that would otherwise creep up the pumping line and carry helium-4 away.</p><h3>Heat exchangers, and Kapitza resistance</h3><p>Incoming concentrated helium-3 must be pre-cooled by the cold dilute stream leaving the mixing chamber, otherwise the warm inflow would swamp the cooling. Above about 50 mK a continuous coil-in-tube exchanger works. Below that the boundary resistance between liquid helium and metal, the <b>Kapitza resistance</b>, which rises roughly as 1/T³, takes over, and continuous exchangers alone stall at about 30 mK. The solution is a <b>step exchanger</b>: a set of sintered-metal blocks with huge surface area (often silver sponge, tens of m² per block) connected in series at progressively lower temperatures, with channels that widen as the liquid becomes denser and slower to avoid viscous heating.</p><h3>The cooling power of the mixing chamber</h3><p>The cooling is the difference in enthalpy between a helium-3 mole in the dilute phase and in the concentrated phase. Both phases are Fermi liquids, so their specific heats rise linearly with temperature, but with different slopes. The numbers used in the lecture: concentrated phase C = 22 T J/mol·K (so H₃ = H₃(0) + 11 T²); dilute phase at 6.6% C = 106 T J/mol·K (effective mass about 2.5 times the bare mass, Fermi temperature 0.38 K), which gives H = H₃(0) + 95 T². Therefore:</p><p><b>Q̇ = ṅ₃ (95 T² − 11 T²) = 84 ṅ₃ T²</b> (watts, with ṅ₃ in mol/s and T in kelvin).</p><p>The T² is the whole story: halve the temperature and the cooling power falls by a factor of four, and double the circulation to double the power. It does not tell you the lowest temperature of a real machine, because viscous heating of the flowing fluid, the Kapitza resistance of the mixing-chamber exchanger and the heat leaks all add to the load. For a heat load Q̇, the idealised limit with no boundary resistance is T<sub>min</sub> = √(Q̇ / 84 ṅ₃), and in practice there is an optimum flow rate beyond which viscous heating wins. The lecture's full boundary-resistance formulas are not reproduced here because the slide equations did not extract cleanly; the shape of the result is that a larger exchanger area lowers the minimum temperature.</p><h3>From wet to dry</h3><p>The first demonstrations were in 1965 to 1966 (220 mK, then 80 mK, then 25 mK). Early machines sat in a bath of liquid helium and used a pumped 1 K pot to condense the helium-3. The big change came in 1977 with the <b>Joule–Thomson heat exchanger</b> (independently by Kraus and by de Waele), which cools and partly liquefies the helium-3 with the gas leaving the still and an expansion valve, removing the 1 K pot. Removing the pot cuts vibration, cuts helium use and removes a common blockage point. Pulse-tube coolers then replaced the helium bath: Pari (1990) used a 12 K Stirling cooler, Uhlig (from 1993) used 10 K Gifford–McMahon machines and later small 4 K pulse tubes, which only became available around 1999 to 2000. Today's <q>dry</q> or cryogen-free refrigerators are the standard.</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Plate in a dry refrigerator</th><th>Typical temperature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pulse tube first stage</td><td>50 K</td></tr><tr><td>Pulse tube second stage</td><td>4 K</td></tr><tr><td>Still</td><td>about 1 K (0.7 K)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate (cold) plate</td><td>0.1 K</td></tr><tr><td>Mixing chamber</td><td>0.01 K</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The lowest temperature of a DR is around 2 mK. To go colder you need nuclear demagnetisation, covered in a later lesson. Variants exist, such as cold-cycle fridges that use a cryopump instead of a warm pump, but the dry DR is the workhorse for quantum science, particle detectors and astrophysical instruments.</p>`,
q:"What produces the cooling in a dilution refrigerator mixing chamber?",o:["Helium-3 crossing from the concentrated phase into the dilute phase, whose enthalpy is higher","Evaporation of liquid helium-4","Expansion of helium-3 gas through a valve"],a:0,e:"Q = ṅ₃(H_dilute − H_concentrated) = 84 ṅ₃ T². The finite 6.6% solubility of helium-3 in helium-4 at T = 0 keeps this process going down to millikelvin temperatures."},
{t:"Advanced: Magnetic cooling: ADR, Pomeranchuk and nuclear demagnetisation",lead:"Cooling with no pumps and no fluids: cleverly ordering and disordering tiny magnets, and freezing helium-3 to cool it.",h:`<h3>Cooling without any fluid</h3><p>Adiabatic demagnetisation refrigeration (ADR) cools entirely electronically: no pumps, no circulating fluid. It is therefore well suited to space instruments and small experiments where a dilution refrigerator would be impractical. It was proposed independently by Debye (1926) and Giauque (1927) and first shown in 1933 (530 mK, then 270 mK), long before helium-3 was available. It dominated sub-kelvin research until the 1950s and 1960s, fell from favour when helium-3 and dilution machines arrived, and returned in the 2000s as helium-3 became scarce and costly and as space and suborbital missions needed compact cold sources. It is most commonly used around 100 mK because that is reachable from a 4 K start, though it can go as low as 2 mK.</p><h3>Entropy in a magnet</h3><p>A paramagnetic ion with total angular momentum J has 2J + 1 possible orientations in a field, so its disordered state carries an entropy S = R ln(2J + 1) per mole. Two things lower that entropy:</p><ul><li><b>Lower temperature:</b> the moments interact with each other and eventually order spontaneously, and by the third law S goes to zero.</li><li><b>An applied field:</b> the moments partly line up with it, creating order. The ratio of magnetic energy to thermal energy B/T decides how much.</li></ul><p>Here is the cycle, drawn on an entropy–temperature diagram:</p><ol><li><b>A to B, magnetise isothermally.</b> A heat switch connects the salt pill to the precooling bath (at T<sub>i</sub>). The field is raised to B<sub>i</sub>. The entropy falls, and the heat of magnetisation, Q = n T<sub>i</sub> [S(0, T<sub>i</sub>) − S(B<sub>i</sub>, T<sub>i</sub>)], flows into the bath: several joules per mole.</li><li><b>B to C, isolate.</b> Open the heat switch.</li><li><b>C to D, demagnetise adiabatically.</b> Ramp the field down. The entropy is constant, and for free moments it depends only on B/T, so T<sub>f</sub>/B<sub>f</sub> = T<sub>i</sub>/B<sub>i</sub>. The pill cools.</li></ol><p>The temperature cannot be pushed to zero by lowering B<sub>f</sub> to zero, because the moments then order spontaneously. After demagnetisation the refrigerant absorbs heat as it warms along the low-field curve; that cooling power is far smaller than the heat of magnetisation, because it is taken in at temperatures below T<sub>i</sub>. Low final temperature and large cooling power are always a compromise.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=adf viewBox='0 0 640 275' role=img aria-label='Entropy against temperature for a paramagnet showing magnetisation and adiabatic demagnetisation'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=ads><b>Salt</b></label><select id=ads><option value=2>CMN (orders at 2 mK)</option><option value=9>CPA (9 mK)</option><option value=16>CMA (16 mK)</option><option value=26 selected>FAA (26 mK)</option><option value=173>MAS (173 mK)</option><option value=182>GS (182 mK)</option></select><label for=adt><b>Start temperature = <span id=adtv>1.5</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=adt min=0.5 max=4 step=0.05 value=1.5><label for=adi><b>Magnet field B<sub>i</sub> = <span id=adiv>2.0</span> T</b></label><input type=range id=adi min=0.5 max=8 step=0.1 value=2><label for=adg><b>Final field B<sub>f</sub> = <span id=adgv>50</span> mT</b></label><input type=range id=adg min=0.7 max=2.7 step=0.02 value=1.7></div><div class=res id=ado></div></div><div class=cap id=adn></div></div><p>This calculator uses the textbook spin-½ formula for free moments, S/R = ln(2 cosh x) − x tanh x with x = μ<sub>B</sub>B/k<sub>B</sub>T. The lecture shows measured entropy curves for real salts rather than this formula, so the numbers are a model of the trend. It reproduces the order of magnitude the lecture states (a few joules per mole of heat of magnetisation).</p><h3>Choosing and building the pill</h3><p>The salt sets the limit. Water of crystallisation spaces the magnetic ions apart, which lowers the ordering temperature. Each salt has a low-temperature or high-temperature range:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Salt</th><th>Abbrev.</th><th>Orders at</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cerium magnesium nitrate</td><td>CMN</td><td>2 mK</td><td>Low-temperature range</td></tr><tr><td>Chromium potassium alum</td><td>CPA</td><td>9 mK</td><td>Low-temperature range</td></tr><tr><td>Chromium methylammonium alum</td><td>CMA</td><td>16 mK</td><td>High-temperature range</td></tr><tr><td>Ferric ammonium alum</td><td>FAA</td><td>26 mK</td><td>High-temperature range</td></tr><tr><td>Manganese ammonium sulphate</td><td>MAS</td><td>173 mK</td><td>High-temperature range</td></tr><tr><td>Gadolinium sulphate</td><td>GS</td><td>182 mK</td><td>High-temperature range</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li><b>Thermal contact.</b> These salts are poor conductors. Crystals are grown around a bus of copper or gold wires, or the canister is filled with a little helium-3 exchange gas.</li><li><b>Hermetic can.</b> The salts contain a lot of water; a welded stainless-steel canister stops them drying out in vacuum.</li><li><b>Suspension.</b> The pill is hung on tensioned Kevlar thread, rigid but a poor conductor. Kevlar expands on cooling, so springs maintain the tension. Space designs use two different supports at each end for a kinematic mount.</li><li><b>Heat switch.</b> Either a mechanical switch (a motor pressing contact surfaces together) or a helium-3 gas-gap switch with no moving parts.</li></ul><h3>Real machines</h3><ul><li><b>Laboratory ADR:</b> typically two stages (1 K and 100 mK) on a pulse-tube cooler, with about 1 J of capacity at 1 K and about 120 mJ at 100 mK.</li><li><b>HAWC+</b> (the far-infrared camera on the SOFIA airborne telescope): holds 100 mK from a 1.5 K bath with a very large pill of nearly 2 mol of FAA.</li><li><b>ASTRO-H</b> (a space X-ray telescope): a three-stage continuous ADR, with two gadolinium-gallium-garnet stages and one CPA stage, cooling a superconducting X-ray detector array to 50 mK. Helium-3 gas-gap switches join the stages.</li></ul><p>An ADR is inherently one-shot, because it must be recycled when the pill warms up. A <b>continuous ADR</b> uses several independent stages with separate magnets, in series or in parallel (one pill is recycled while another is cold). It works but is very complex.</p><h3>Pomeranchuk cooling: freeze helium-3 to cool it</h3><p>Normally freezing a liquid gives off heat, because the solid is more ordered than the liquid. Pomeranchuk predicted in 1950 that helium-3 below about 0.3 K does the opposite: its melting curve has a minimum (at 29.3 bar and 315 mK) and below that the <b>solid has more entropy than the liquid</b>, because the solid's nuclear spins are still disordered. By the Clausius–Clapeyron equation, the negative slope of the melting line means that compressing liquid helium-3 so that part of it solidifies <i>absorbs</i> heat. The cooling power is Q̇ = ṅ<sub>sol</sub> T (S<sub>solid</sub> − S<sub>liquid</sub>) with S<sub>solid</sub> = R ln 2 and, for the liquid below about 30 mK, S<sub>liquid</sub> = 36 T J/mol·K. Anufriev first did it in 1965, cooling from 50 mK to below 18 mK; Wheatley and co-workers reached 2 mK. It was the only route to a few millikelvin until strong dilution refrigerators appeared in the late 1970s.</p><div class=calc><label for=pmi><b>Start temperature = <span id=pmiv>30</span> mK</b></label><input type=range id=pmi min=6 max=30 step=1 value=30><label for=pmf><b>Final temperature = <span id=pmfv>2</span> mK</b></label><input type=range id=pmf min=2 max=29 step=1 value=2><div class=res id=pmo></div><div class=cap id=pmn></div></div><h3>Nuclear demagnetisation: into the microkelvin range</h3><p>Nuclei carry magnetic moments too, so the same trick can be used with nuclear spins. The advantage is that nuclear moments order only at extremely low temperatures, so the refrigerant can be cooled into microkelvin territory. The catch is that nuclear moments are weak: the nuclear magneton is 5.1 × 10⁻²⁷ J/T against 9.3 × 10⁻²⁴ J/T for the Bohr magneton, about 1800 times smaller. A pure metal such as copper is used, because it conducts heat well and has many moments. Starting from 8 T and 6 mK, demagnetising copper removes only about 9% of the nuclear spin entropy. The cooling power Q = n ∫ T dS is many orders of magnitude smaller than in the salt, and the nuclear spins must then cool the conduction electrons and the lattice. Nuclear stages are launched from below 1 K, usually from a dilution refrigerator, and are used to study materials and gases, not to cool real apparatus. Summing up, the useful ranges are: pumped helium refrigerators 1 K to 0.3 K; salt demagnetisation 1 K to 30 mK; dilution refrigerators 1 K to 2 mK; Pomeranchuk cooling 0.3 K to 2 mK; nuclear demagnetisation 1 mK to 1 µK.</p>`,
q:"In an ADR, the final temperature after an ideal adiabatic demagnetisation from (Tᵢ, Bᵢ) to field B_f is:",o:["T_f = Tᵢ × B_f / Bᵢ, but never below the ordering temperature of the salt","T_f = Tᵢ × Bᵢ / B_f","T_f = 0 once B_f is zero"],a:0,e:"The entropy of free moments depends only on B/T, so constant entropy means constant B/T. Spontaneous ordering sets the practical floor."},
{t:"Advanced: Materials and thermometry below 1 kelvin",lead:"At millikelvin temperatures the cooling power is tiny, so even aged stress, hydrogen in copper and a thermometer's own heating matter.",h:`<h3>When cooling power is measured in microwatts</h3><p>Sub-kelvin refrigerators deliver many orders of magnitude less power than machines at 4 K, so four things you might ignore at higher temperature become critical: thermal conduction, thermal isolation, thermal contact and time-dependent heat sources inside the materials themselves.</p><h3>Conduction</h3><p>The conductivity integral from earlier lessons applies again, but the physics changes. Phonons freeze out, strongly cutting the conductivity of insulators and leaving electrons as the main carriers in metals. Superconductors become <i>good insulators</i> well below their critical temperature, because the Cooper pairs carry no heat; the drop is gentler than for electrical resistance and mostly complete by T<sub>c</sub>/10. Pure copper has a linear conductivity at low temperature and is excellent, while high-purity (high residual resistivity ratio) grades such as RRR 150 conduct better still.</p><p>For notional supports with the same stiffness, the heat conducted from 1 K to 0.1 K was:</p><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Support material</th><th>Heat conducted, 1 K to 0.1 K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>G-10 fibreglass epoxy</td><td>11.5 mW</td></tr><tr><td>Stainless steel 304</td><td>12.0 mW</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon-fibre reinforced polymer</td><td>3.5 mW</td></tr><tr><td>Titanium 15-3-3-3 (superconducting, T<sub>c</sub> about 3 K)</td><td>2.8 mW</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The carbon-fibre composite wins because it is very disordered and scatters phonons; the titanium alloy wins because it is superconducting, and therefore insulates.</p><h3>Joints</h3><p>Joints are demountable (bolted or compression fits) or permanent (welds and braze). Beware solders and filler metals that are superconductors, which insulate the joint. Electron-beam welding of copper is very effective. Contact conductance depends on the tiny asperities that touch, so tricks such as thermal grease or indium foil that help at room temperature often stop working below 1 K, and can even degrade the joint. Gold (or silver or platinum) plating is effective. For bolted copper joints a design curve from the literature (Schmitt et al., 2015) is of the form K(T) = 0.1161 T^1.11 for a 10-32 screw, scaled up by tensile area for bigger screws and multiplied by the number of screws; check the units in the original paper before using it, because the slide does not state them.</p><h3>Heat that appears on its own</h3><p>Cold metal and plastic are not inert. Three processes release energy slowly, over days:</p><ul><li><b>Ortho–para hydrogen conversion.</b> Commercial copper contains 10 to 100 ppm of hydrogen, trapped as small gas pockets. On cooling it liquefies and slowly converts from ortho to para, releasing 1.063 kJ per mole of ortho hydrogen converted. The conversion follows x(t) = x₀ / (1 + x₀ k t) with k = 0.019 per hour, so the heat rate falls as 1/t². Annealing the metal first lets the hydrogen escape.</li><li><b>Tunnelling in amorphous solids and crystals.</b> Atoms hop between nearly equal energy states by quantum tunnelling and release energy at a rate that falls as 1/t. Typical amorphous materials release about 100 pW/g after 24 hours; the rate is strongly material dependent.</li><li><b>Stress relaxation.</b> Thermoelastic stresses built up when the grains in a metal contract at different rates relax with a time constant of about 100 hours, Q̇ = W₀ e^(−t/τ), with W₀ about 500 pW/g for copper (a model gives about 700 pW/g, in agreement with experiment). It starts the moment stress develops, so cooling slowly helps.</li></ul><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=tdf viewBox='0 0 640 262' role=img aria-label='Time-dependent heat release per gram of copper from hydrogen conversion, tunnelling and stress relaxation'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=tdm><b>Mass of copper = <span id=tdmv>500</span> g</b></label><input type=range id=tdm min=0 max=4 step=0.05 value=2.7><label for=tdt><b>Time since cooldown = <span id=tdtv>24</span> h</b></label><input type=range id=tdt min=0 max=3 step=0.02 value=1.38></div><div class=res id=tdo></div></div><div class=cap id=tdn></div></div><p>The curves use the time dependences from the lecture, scaled to the lecture's <q>after one day</q> values (20 pW/g for hydrogen conversion and for tunnelling) and to W₀ = 500 pW/g for stress relaxation. They are order-of-magnitude guides, since each real sample differs. The practical defences are the same: use less mass, anneal before cooling and cool slowly.</p><h3>Measuring temperature</h3><p>Temperature is defined by the Carnot cycle, which gives only ratios, so a scale needs fixed points. The Kelvin is 1/273.16 of the triple point of water. The standard scale, ITS-90, is defined by the triple points of hydrogen (13.8033 K), neon (24.5561 K), oxygen (54.3584 K), argon (83.8058 K), mercury (234.3156 K) and water (273.16 K), plus melting and freezing points up to copper (1357.77 K) and, in the helium range, the vapour pressure of helium between 3 K and 5 K. Platinum resistance interpolates between the fixed points.</p><ul><li><b>Primary thermometers</b> need no calibration because the theory is well known: a gas thermometer (PV = nRT, with corrections for dead volume and non-ideal gas), or the thermal (Johnson) noise in a resistor. They are slow and fiddly, so they are mainly used at the very lowest temperatures and to calibrate others.</li><li><b>Secondary thermometers</b> must be calibrated, but are easy to use. A good one is sensitive, quick to reach equilibrium, easy to read, insensitive to magnetic fields, with a simple temperature law and minimal self-heating.</li></ul><div class=sc><table><thead><tr><th>Sensor</th><th>Range (K)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ruthenium oxide</td><td>0.01 to about 40</td><td>Interchangeable above 0.05 K; the sub-kelvin workhorse</td></tr><tr><td>Cernox (zirconium oxynitride)</td><td>0.1 to 420</td><td>Individual calibration</td></tr><tr><td>Germanium</td><td>0.05 to 100</td><td>Individual calibration</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon-glass</td><td>about 1 to 500</td><td>Individual calibration</td></tr><tr><td>Rhodium-iron</td><td>about 1 to 500</td><td>Individual calibration</td></tr><tr><td>Platinum</td><td>14 to 900</td><td>Interchangeable</td></tr><tr><td>Silicon diode</td><td>2 to 500</td><td>Cheap, easy to read, poor sensitivity above about 30 K</td></tr><tr><td>Capacitance</td><td>wide</td><td>Insensitive to magnetic field, but drifts on thermal cycling: good for control only</td></tr><tr><td>Thermocouple</td><td>mainly above 20 K</td><td>The Seebeck coefficient falls to zero as T goes to zero</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Not heating the thermometer</h3><p>A resistance thermometer dissipates P = I²R. At 4 K you can ignore this; at 20 mK the sensor's own power can warm it above its surroundings. Recommended maximum powers for a ruthenium oxide sensor are about 10⁻¹⁴ W at 20 mK, 10⁻¹⁰ W at 0.1 K, 10⁻⁹ W at 1 K and 10⁻⁸ W at 10 K. Use the highest excitation that does not cause self-heating; lower than that and the signal vanishes in noise.</p><div class=calc><label for=tht><b>Temperature = <span id=thtv>0.020</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=tht min=-1.7 max=1 step=0.02 value=-1.7><label for=thr><b>Sensor resistance = <span id=thrv>10.0</span> kΩ</b></label><input type=range id=thr min=-1 max=2 step=0.05 value=1><div class=res id=tho></div><div class=cap id=thn></div></div><ul><li><b>Four-wire measurement</b> separates the current leads from the voltage leads so lead resistance does not matter, at the price of twice as many wires and twice the conducted heat.</li><li><b>Shielding and filtering.</b> Use twisted pairs, make the cryostat a Faraday cage with an explicit ground, and filter every line (RC, RL or RLC with a cutoff above the excitation frequency) in a grounded box that is well heat-sunk to a cold plate. Radio-frequency pickup shows up as heating in the sensor and gives a false reading.</li><li><b>Choose sensors by the temperature, accuracy and resolution you need</b> and by whether there is a magnetic field or radiation. Often two sensors are used: platinum for the cooldown and ruthenium oxide at base.</li></ul>`,
q:"Why must a ruthenium-oxide thermometer at 20 mK be excited with far lower power than at 4 K?",o:["Its own dissipation can heat the sensor above the experiment, because the thermal contact and the surrounding cooling power are tiny","The sensor changes polarity","Lower power makes the reading faster"],a:0,e:"A sensor at 20 mK is recommended to dissipate about 10⁻¹⁴ W; the same current that is harmless at 4 K would warm it noticeably."},
{t:"Lab: Building a cryostat, step by step",lead:"From a pile of parts to a vacuum-tight cryostat: what goes in, in what order, and how to estimate the heat load before you cut any metal.",h:`<p><b>About this module.</b> The lessons so far explained the physics. This module is the bench side: how an experiment is actually built, tested and run. It is general laboratory practice, not taken from the lectures, so treat the numbers as planning values and follow your own institution's safety rules and the maker's manuals for every pump, sensor and cryogen.</p><h3>Start with the heat budget, not the drawing</h3><p>A cryostat is a cold object kept apart from a warm room. Everything you build is an answer to one question: how much heat reaches the cold part, and can your cooling source remove it? Heat gets in by three routes, and a good design lists all three before any metal is cut.</p><ul><li><b>Solid conduction</b> down supports, fill tubes and wires. It is set by the area, the length and the thermal conductivity integral of the material between the warm and cold ends, so thin, long, low-conductivity parts win.</li><li><b>Radiation</b> from the warm walls. It falls with surface emissivity and with the number of reflective layers or intercepting shields between the two surfaces.</li><li><b>Residual gas.</b> Below about 10<sup>-5</sup> mbar this is usually negligible; above it, gas molecules carry heat across the gap in proportion to the pressure (see the gas conduction lesson).</li></ul><p>Write down the target first: the temperature, the size of the sample, how many wires you need, and how long you want it to hold. The heat-budget calculator further down turns those choices into watts and into litres of cryogen per hour.</p><h3>The assembly order</h3><p>Most laboratory cryostats are built from the inside out, hanging from the top flange so that everything can be inspected and then enclosed. Press Play, or drag the slider, and watch the build.</p><div class=fig><div><button class=btn id=asg>Play</button></div><svg class=sv id=asf viewBox='0 0 640 350' role=img aria-label='Animated build of a cryostat: vessel, sensors, wiring, radiation shield, vacuum can, pumping and fill'></svg><label for=ass><b>Build step</b></label><input type=range id=ass min=0 max=700 step=1 value=0><div class=cap id=asn></div></div><ol><li><b>Hang the vessel.</b> The inner vessel hangs from the top flange on a thin-walled stainless-steel tube, which doubles as the fill and vent line. Stainless steel conducts heat poorly, and a thin wall means a small cross-section.</li><li><b>Fit the sensors and the heater.</b> Put thermometers where you want to know the temperature, in a copper block or plate for good thermal contact. Use a four-wire connection, a non-inductive heater, and a thin film of vacuum grease or varnish for contact. Log the room-temperature resistance of every device now; it is your first check later.</li><li><b>Route the wires.</b> Use fine wire of a low-conductivity alloy on the long runs (manganin or constantan), copper where you need low resistance, and wrap each run around a copper post at every temperature stage, the <b>thermal anchor</b>. Leave a little slack for thermal contraction.</li><li><b>Wrap the radiation shield.</b> Bolt the shield to a cold anchor and wrap it in multilayer insulation: many thin aluminised polyester layers kept apart by a loose spacer. Do not press the layers tight, because contact between layers carries heat. Perforate or vent the layers so the gas between them can pump out.</li><li><b>Close the vacuum can.</b> Clean the sealing faces, fit a fresh O-ring or indium wire, and tighten the bolts in a star pattern, a little at a time. Check that no part touches the can wall: a single contact is a thermal short.</li><li><b>Pump and leak test.</b> The next lesson covers this in detail. Do not fill with cryogen until the vacuum is good.</li><li><b>Fill.</b> Precool, then fill slowly, as described in the cooldown lesson.</li></ol><h3>Heat-budget calculator</h3><p>Set up your own cryostat. The calculator uses power-law fits to the thermal conductivity of each material, k = k<sub>300</sub>(T/300)<sup>n</sup>, and the radiation formula with floating shields, plus the free-molecular gas conduction formula from the gas conduction lesson. All material constants are rough handbook values from outside the lectures and are good to about ±30%: enough to size a design, not to replace a measured value.</p><div class=calc><div class=res id=bco></div><label for=bcc><b>Cold temperature</b></label><select id=bcc><option value=0>77 K (liquid nitrogen)</option><option value=1>4.2 K (liquid helium)</option></select><label for=bcm><b>Support tube material</b></label><select id=bcm><option value=0>Stainless steel 304</option><option value=1>G-10 fibreglass</option><option value=2>Copper (for comparison)</option></select><label for=bcl><b>Support length = <span id=bclv>30</span> cm</b></label><input type=range id=bcl min=5 max=100 step=1 value=30><label for=bcd><b>Tube outer diameter = <span id=bcdv>12</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=bcd min=4 max=40 step=1 value=12><label for=bct><b>Tube wall = <span id=bctv>0.30</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=bct min=0.1 max=2 step=0.05 value=0.3><label for=bcw><b>Wires = <span id=bcwv>20</span></b></label><input type=range id=bcw min=0 max=100 step=1 value=20><label for=bcg><b>Wire diameter = <span id=bcgv>0.10</span> mm</b></label><input type=range id=bcg min=0.05 max=0.5 step=0.01 value=0.1><label for=bcy><b>Wire material</b></label><select id=bcy><option value=0>Copper</option><option value=1>Manganin</option></select><label for=bca><b>Surface area of the cold part = <span id=bcav>1000</span> cm²</b></label><input type=range id=bca min=100 max=5000 step=50 value=1000><label for=bce><b>Surface emissivity = <span id=bcev>0.05</span></b></label><input type=range id=bce min=0.02 max=0.3 step=0.01 value=0.05><label for=bcn><b>Layers of insulation or shields = <span id=bcnv>10</span></b></label><input type=range id=bcn min=0 max=40 step=1 value=10><label for=bcf><b>Real-world penalty on radiation = <span id=bcfv>3</span>×</b></label><input type=range id=bcf min=1 max=10 step=0.5 value=3><label for=bcp><b>Residual pressure = 10<sup><span id=bcpv>-5</span></sup> mbar</b></label><input type=range id=bcp min=-8 max=-2 step=0.25 value=-5><label for=bcv><b>Liquid volume = <span id=bcvv>10</span> L</b></label><input type=range id=bcv min=1 max=100 step=1 value=10><div class=cap id=bcx></div></div><p>Notice the pattern. At 77 K the radiation usually dominates until you add shield layers; at 4.2 K the same wires and supports cost far less per watt but each watt boils away about 60 times more liquid, so the budget is much tighter. Try the copper support tube: its conduction dwarfs everything else, which is exactly why no cryostat hangs from copper tubes.</p><h3>Check before you close the can</h3><ul><li>Everything is clean and free of oil and finger grease: gloves, then a solvent wipe.</li><li>Every sensor and heater has been checked at room temperature and its value logged; every wire is checked for shorts to ground.</li><li>No wire, support or shield touches the vacuum can. Look all round with a torch.</li><li>Screws that end in blind holes are vented (slotted or drilled), or gas trapped behind them will leak out slowly for weeks.</li><li>Photograph the assembly and write the build into the logbook. When something misbehaves at 4 K, you will want to know exactly how it was built.</li></ul>`,
q:"Why is the support tube made of thin-walled stainless steel instead of copper?",o:["Stainless steel conducts heat poorly and the thin wall keeps the cross-section small, so little heat flows down to the cold part","Copper is not strong enough at low temperature","Stainless steel has higher emissivity"],a:0,e:"Conduction heat load scales as area times the thermal conductivity integral divided by length. Stainless steel in a thin-walled tube minimises both the area and the conductivity, while copper would carry orders of magnitude more heat."},
{t:"Lab: Vacuum and leak checking in practice",lead:"How to pump down a cryostat, find a leak with a helium detector, and tell a real leak from outgassing by watching the pressure.",h:`<p>A cryostat's insulating vacuum is the first thing to get right. A poor vacuum adds heat load, and a leak can freeze gas onto cold parts, block a fill line or ruin a measurement. The good news is that vacuum problems are diagnosed with a simple routine, and most of it is patience and good habits. This lesson is general laboratory practice, not taken from the lectures.</p><h3>Four kinds of gas load</h3><ul><li><b>Real leak.</b> A path from outside into the vacuum: a poor seal, a cracked weld, a pinhole. It adds gas at a constant rate for ever.</li><li><b>Virtual leak.</b> A trapped pocket of gas inside, such as a blind tapped hole, a double seal or a gap between two welded plates. It bleeds out slowly and looks like a leak until the pocket has emptied.</li><li><b>Outgassing.</b> Gas and water vapour released from the surfaces. It is large at first and falls roughly as 1/time, so it gets better the longer you pump.</li><li><b>Permeation.</b> Gas slowly diffusing through elastomer seals and polymers. It is small, but it sets the limit for O-ring-sealed systems.</li></ul><p>The unit of a gas load is the <b>throughput</b>, pressure times volume per second, in mbar·l/s. At steady state, the pressure you reach is p = Q/S, where Q is the total gas load and S the pumping speed at the chamber. So a leak of 10<sup>-5</sup> mbar·l/s into a 50 l/s pump leaves you at 2×10<sup>-7</sup> mbar, and the same leak into a sealed chamber raises the pressure at a rate Q/V.</p><h3>The pump-down routine</h3><ol><li>Clean the seal faces and fit the seals. Use as little grease as possible on O-rings, none on indium.</li><li>Start the <b>roughing pump</b> and open the valve to the chamber. The pressure falls from atmosphere to about 10<sup>-2</sup> mbar.</li><li>When the pressure is low enough for the turbomolecular pump, as stated in its manual, start the <b>turbo pump</b> and open to it. Do not start a turbo against atmospheric pressure.</li><li>Leave it pumping while you check for leaks. For cryostats a few hours of pumping before cooling is common, and overnight is better.</li></ol><h3>Find the leak with helium</h3><p>A <b>helium leak detector</b> is a mass spectrometer tuned to helium (mass 4), connected to the pumping port. Helium is chosen because it is inert, light enough to pass through very small holes, and rare in air, so a trace is easy to see. The routine:</p><ul><li>Pump until the detector reaches its background level and the pressure is stable.</li><li>Spray helium on one joint at a time in short, gentle bursts. Helium is lighter than air, so <b>start at the top and work down</b>.</li><li>Watch the detector's signal. A rise that follows your spray on a particular joint points to the leak, but the signal lags by several seconds, so move slowly. Mark the suspect joint, then check again.</li><li>Repair, re-pump and re-test. When the total leak rate is below your target, the system is leak-tight.</li></ul><p>Try it below. Choose which joint leaks and how large the leak is, then move the gun along the joints, or press Auto-sweep. The signal responds slowly after you pass the leak, which is exactly why real leak hunting needs patience.</p><div class=fig><div><button class=btn id=lka>Auto-sweep</button></div><svg class=sv id=lkf viewBox='0 0 640 300' role=img aria-label='Helium leak test: a gun sprays helium on joints of a vacuum vessel, and a detector signal trace shows the response'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=lkg><b>Helium gun position</b></label><input type=range id=lkg min=0 max=300 step=1 value=0><label for=lkj><b>Which joint leaks?</b></label><select id=lkj><option value=4>Random: find it</option><option value=0>Flange seal</option><option value=1>Feedthrough</option><option value=2>Weld seam</option><option value=3>Valve</option></select><label for=lks><b>Leak size</b></label><select id=lks><option value=-9>10⁻⁹ mbar·l/s (tiny)</option><option value=-8>10⁻⁸ mbar·l/s</option><option value=-7 selected>10⁻⁷ mbar·l/s</option><option value=-6>10⁻⁶ mbar·l/s (large)</option></select></div><div><div class=res id=lko></div></div></div><div class=cap id=lkn></div></div><h3>Rate-of-rise: real leak or outgassing?</h3><p>The helium detector tells you <b>where</b>. The <b>rate-of-rise test</b> tells you <b>how much</b>, and which kind of load it is. Pump to a good vacuum, close the valve to the pump, and record the pressure against time. The slope gives the throughput: Q = V × dp/dt. A <b>straight line</b> means a real leak, because it keeps adding gas at a constant rate. A line that <b>bends over and flattens</b> means the load is dying away, so it is outgassing or a virtual leak. Set the leak and the outgassing yourself and see how the curves differ.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=rrc viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='Pressure rise after isolating the pump for a real leak and outgassing'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=rrv><b>Chamber volume = <span id=rrvv>20</span> L</b></label><input type=range id=rrv min=1 max=200 step=1 value=20><label for=rrq><b>Real leak = 10<sup><span id=rrqv>-7</span></sup> mbar·l/s</b></label><input type=range id=rrq min=-10 max=-4 step=0.25 value=-7><label for=rro><b>Initial outgassing = 10<sup><span id=rrov>-6</span></sup> mbar·l/s</b></label><input type=range id=rro min=-9 max=-4 step=0.25 value=-6><label for=rrt><b>Outgassing decay time = <span id=rrtv>10</span> min</b></label><input type=range id=rrt min=1 max=120 step=1 value=10><label for=rrm><b>Test length = <span id=rrmv>30</span> min</b></label><input type=range id=rrm min=5 max=120 step=1 value=30></div><div class=res id=rrr></div></div><div class=cap id=rrx></div></div><h3>How long to pump down?</h3><p>The pump-down is governed by the volume, the pumping speed and the gas loads. For a chamber of volume V and a pump of speed S, the pressure obeys V dp/dt = −S p + Q, where Q is the sum of outgassing and leaks. The roughing stage falls exponentially, with a time constant V/S. Then outgassing takes over and sets the pace, falling slowly as the surfaces clean up. The calculator uses a roughing pump up to 0.1 mbar and a turbo pump after that, and an outgassing rate that follows 1/time. The outgassing rate for a clean unbaked stainless-steel surface varies widely, from 10<sup>-9</sup> to 10<sup>-7</sup> mbar·l/s per cm² after an hour of pumping, depending on cleanliness and history, so treat the result as a guide.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=puc viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='Pump-down curve of pressure against time on logarithmic axes'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=puv><b>Chamber volume = <span id=puvv>20</span> L</b></label><input type=range id=puv min=1 max=500 step=1 value=20><label for=pus><b>Roughing pump speed = <span id=pusv>5</span> l/s</b></label><input type=range id=pus min=0.5 max=30 step=0.5 value=5><label for=put><b>Turbo pump speed at the chamber = <span id=putv>50</span> l/s</b></label><input type=range id=put min=5 max=500 step=5 value=50><label for=pua><b>Inner surface area = <span id=puav>3000</span> cm²</b></label><input type=range id=pua min=100 max=30000 step=100 value=3000><label for=puq><b>Outgassing at 1 h = 10<sup><span id=puqv>-8</span></sup> mbar·l/s·cm²</b></label><input type=range id=puq min=-10 max=-6 step=0.25 value=-8><label for=pul><b>Real leak = 10<sup><span id=pulv>-9</span></sup> mbar·l/s</b></label><input type=range id=pul min=-12 max=-5 step=0.25 value=-9></div><div class=res id=puo></div></div><div class=cap id=pun></div></div><h3>Tips from experience</h3><ul><li><b>Cold leaks.</b> A joint can be tight at room temperature and leak when cold, because metals contract by different amounts. If the vacuum worsens on cooling, find it with the detector while the cryostat is cold, or with a quick helium test as it warms.</li><li><b>Seals.</b> A hair, a scratch or a dry O-ring causes most seal leaks. Wipe, inspect, replace. Indium needs clean, flat, parallel faces.</li><li><b>Vent screws.</b> A screw in a blind hole is a virtual leak. Use vented screws, or leave a small slot.</li><li><b>Gauges lie.</b> An ion gauge reads differently for different gases, and a hot filament outgasses. Be careful about comparing readings of two different gauges.</li><li><b>Write it down.</b> Log the pressure and the time of every pump-down. A change from the usual curve warns you before a problem becomes serious.</li></ul>`,
q:"After closing the valve to the pump, the pressure rises linearly with time and does not flatten. What does that suggest?",o:["A real leak adding gas at a constant rate","Outgassing from the surfaces","A faulty pressure gauge reading zero"],a:0,e:"Outgassing and virtual leaks decay with time, so the pressure curve bends over. A straight line means a constant gas load, which is a real leak. The slope times the volume gives its size."},
{t:"Lab: Cooling down safely",lead:"The cooldown procedure step by step, how much cryogen a cooldown really uses, and why the cold vapour is worth more than the liquid.",h:`<p>Cooling an experiment down is the most expensive and the riskiest routine step in a cryogenic laboratory. Done well, it takes a predictable time and a predictable amount of cryogen. Done badly, it wastes litres of liquid helium, cracks joints and freezes moisture into valves. This lesson is general laboratory practice, not taken from the lectures. Follow your own institution's procedures and safety rules for every cryogen.</p><h3>The routine, step by step</h3><ol><li><b>Check the vacuum.</b> Never cool a cryostat whose insulating vacuum is poor. Confirm the pressure, and log it.</li><li><b>Check the instruments.</b> Confirm that every thermometer reads room temperature, the heater resistance is right, and the logging works. It is much easier to fix a wire now than at 77 K.</li><li><b>Purge the lines.</b> Evacuate or flush the fill and vent lines with dry gas so that no air or moisture is left to freeze. Air in a helium space freezes solid and blocks the path.</li><li><b>Precool with liquid nitrogen.</b> For a system going to 4 K, bring it to about 77 K first, with liquid nitrogen in a jacket or by direct fill. This is vastly cheaper than using helium for the same job, as the calculator shows.</li><li><b>Remove the nitrogen.</b> Liquid nitrogen must be completely removed from the helium space before helium goes in; any left would freeze and block the line.</li><li><b>Transfer the cryogen slowly.</b> Start with a low flow so the cold gas does most of the cooling, and open up as the system cools. Watch the temperatures and the pressure.</li><li><b>Respect the cooling rate.</b> Large temperature differences between parts cause thermal stress. Fragile parts (ceramics, epoxy joints, glass-to-metal seals, superconducting magnets) have rate limits in their manuals, often a few kelvin per minute.</li><li><b>Hold and log.</b> When the temperature is reached, record the time, the cryogen used, and the final temperatures. They are your baseline for next time.</li></ol><h3>Why the vapour matters</h3><p>When a liquid cryogen evaporates, it first absorbs its latent heat, then the cold vapour absorbs more heat as it warms up towards room temperature. The vapour's sensible heat is often far bigger than the latent heat. For nitrogen, warming the vapour from 77 K to 300 K absorbs about 230 J/g, against 199 J/g of latent heat. For helium the gap is huge: the vapour absorbs about 1500 J/g warming from 4.2 K to 300 K, against only 20.9 J/g of latent heat, about 70 times more. So the cheapest cooldown is the one that makes the cold gas flow past everything on its way out, and the most expensive is the one that dumps liquid and lets the cold vapour escape.</p><h3>Cooldown simulator</h3><p>The simulator treats the cold mass as a single lump. Its heat capacity comes from the Debye model, using Debye temperatures of 343 K for copper, 428 K for aluminium and 470 K for steel. The model is accurate for copper and aluminium to within a few percent; for stainless steel it is rougher (about 15% low), because of magnetic and electronic contributions it leaves out. You choose how hard the cooling source works and the safe cooling-rate limit. The lump cools according to dT/dt = −hA(T − T<sub>bath</sub>)/(mc), capped at your rate limit.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=cdc viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='Cooldown curve of the cold mass with the rate limit'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=cdm><b>Material</b></label><select id=cdm><option value=0>Copper</option><option value=1>Aluminium</option><option value=2>Steel</option></select><label for=cdw><b>Cold mass = <span id=cdwv>5</span> kg</b></label><input type=range id=cdw min=0.1 max=100 step=0.1 value=5><label for=cdh><b>Heat transfer hA to the bath = <span id=cdhv>20</span> W/K</b></label><input type=range id=cdh min=1 max=500 step=1 value=20><label for=cdr><b>Allowed cooling rate = <span id=cdrv>3</span> K/min</b></label><input type=range id=cdr min=0.2 max=20 step=0.2 value=3><label for=cdu><b>Share of the vapour's cold used = <span id=cduv>50</span>%</b></label><input type=range id=cdu min=0 max=100 step=1 value=50></div><div class=res id=cdo></div></div><div class=cap id=cdn></div></div><h3>What a cooldown costs</h3><p>The cryogen needed is the heat to be removed divided by what each gram can absorb. With only the latent heat used, that is the enthalpy change divided by the latent heat. Using a share of the vapour's cold as well, the number of grams falls sharply. The simulator reports both figures for liquid nitrogen from 300 K to 77 K, and for liquid helium from 77 K to 4.2 K. It also shows what it would cost to go straight from 300 K to 4.2 K with helium, without a nitrogen precool, so you can see why laboratories nearly always precool.</p><h3>Common ways a cooldown goes wrong</h3><ul><li><b>Moisture and air.</b> Water vapour and air freeze in the lines. Purge with dry gas and check for leaks before cooling.</li><li><b>Cooling too fast.</b> Parts of different mass cool at different rates, so stress builds at joints. Slow the transfer if the temperature differences grow.</li><li><b>Transferring against a warm line.</b> The first liquid evaporates on the warm transfer line and wastes cryogen. Cool the line first and let the flow settle.</li><li><b>Pressure.</b> Cold gas and boiling liquid raise the pressure. Keep the vent path open and the relief valve in place.</li><li><b>A thermal short.</b> If the system warms or cools unevenly, something is touching that should not. Re-check the supports and wires at the next warm-up.</li></ul>`,
q:"For helium, the cold vapour can absorb about 70 times more heat than the latent heat of vaporisation. What does this mean for cooldowns?",o:["It is far cheaper to make the cold gas flow past the warm parts on its way out, and to precool with liquid nitrogen first","Liquid helium should be poured directly onto warm parts to use its latent heat","Helium vapour should be vented immediately to save time"],a:0,e:"Most of the cooling power in a helium cooldown is in the vapour's sensible heat. Using that cold gas, and precooling with nitrogen, cuts the helium consumption by orders of magnitude."},
{t:"Lab: Calibrating thermometers",lead:"How to turn a resistance or a voltage into a trustworthy temperature: the comparison method, fixed points, how many points you need, and the errors that creep in.",h:`<p>A cryogenic thermometer does not tell you the temperature; it gives you a resistance or a voltage. A <b>calibration</b> is the table or curve that turns that signal into a temperature, built by comparing the sensor with something you trust. Everything you later conclude about your experiment rests on it. This lesson is general laboratory practice, not taken from the lectures.</p><h3>What to compare against</h3><ul><li><b>A calibrated reference thermometer.</b> This is the usual method in a laboratory. A reference sensor with a certified calibration is put in the same isothermal block as the sensor under test, and the two are compared at a series of temperatures.</li><li><b>Fixed points.</b> Pure substances have temperatures that are the same everywhere in the world. At the <b>triple point</b> a pure substance holds solid, liquid and vapour in equilibrium at one temperature. Some defining fixed points of the international temperature scale are hydrogen at 13.8033 K, neon at 24.5561 K, oxygen at 54.3584 K, argon at 83.8058 K, mercury at 234.3156 K and water at 273.16 K. These are used in standards laboratories.</li><li><b>Boiling baths.</b> Liquid nitrogen boils at 77.35 K and liquid helium at about 4.2 K at atmospheric pressure. They are handy checks, but the temperature depends on the pressure, so read the barometer. Liquid nitrogen that has taken up oxygen from the air boils warmer, which can shift a 77 K check by a degree or more.</li></ul><h3>The comparison method in practice</h3><p>The set-up is a small, solid copper block, thermally anchored to the cold stage through a controlled thermal link, with a heater on it. The reference thermometer and the sensors under test are fitted into drilled holes or clamped on, with a thin film of vacuum grease for good thermal contact. A radiation shield surrounds the block. A temperature controller holds the block at each set point in turn. The routine has five parts:</p><ol><li>Step to a set point and <b>wait for equilibrium</b>. Look at the reference reading; it should stop drifting.</li><li>Take the readings of the reference and every sensor, with <b>four-wire</b> measurements and a low excitation current.</li><li>Move to the next set point. Cover the range with <b>more points where the sensor changes fastest or most unevenly</b>.</li><li>Repeat some points on the way back, going up as well as down, to check for hysteresis and drift.</li><li>Fit a smooth curve through the points. It is the curve, not the table, that you will use between the points.</li></ol><p>Watch the animation. The block steps through a series of set points. At each one it waits until the temperature has settled, and only then does the system record a point.</p><div class=fig><div><button class=btn id=calg>Restart</button></div><svg class=sv id=calf viewBox='0 0 640 340' role=img aria-label='Animated comparison calibration: a copper block steps through set points and calibration points are recorded once equilibrium is reached'></svg><div class=cap id=caln></div></div><h3>How many points do you need?</h3><p>The curve you fit is an interpolation, and it can only be as good as the points that define it. Too few points and the curve wanders between them; too many for the degree of the fit and it chases the noise. The simulator calibrates a generic negative-temperature-coefficient resistor, of the kind used from room temperature down to a few kelvin. Its resistance follows R = R<sub>0</sub> exp(√(T<sub>0</sub>/T)), with R<sub>0</sub> = 40 Ω and T<sub>0</sub> = 78 K, giving about 67 Ω at 300 K and about 3 kΩ at 4.2 K. This is an illustrative model, not a specific product. The fit is a Chebyshev polynomial of log T in log R, by least squares. Try changing the number of points, the degree and the noise on the reference readings.</p><div class=fig><svg class=sv id=fitc viewBox='0 0 330 190' role=img aria-label='Calibration residual against temperature for the chosen number of points and degree'></svg><div class=two><div><label for=fitn><b>Calibration points = <span id=fitnv>20</span></b></label><input type=range id=fitn min=3 max=30 step=1 value=20><label for=fitd><b>Polynomial degree = <span id=fitdv>10</span></b></label><input type=range id=fitd min=1 max=12 step=1 value=10><label for=fits><b>Point spacing</b></label><select id=fits><option value=0>Even in temperature</option><option value=1 selected>Even in log T (more at low T)</option></select><label for=fitr><b>Range</b></label><select id=fitr><option value=0>4.2 to 300 K</option><option value=1>4.2 to 50 K</option><option value=2>50 to 300 K</option></select><label for=fitw><b>Noise on the reference = <span id=fitwv>1</span> mK</b></label><input type=range id=fitw min=0 max=50 step=0.5 value=1><button class=btn id=fitb>New noise</button></div><div class=res id=fito></div></div><div class=cap id=fitx></div></div><p>The residual is the difference between the fitted curve and the true curve at 300 test temperatures. Note how bad an even spacing in temperature is for this sensor: it changes by orders of magnitude at low temperature, so it needs points that are dense there. If the degree is too low, the curve cannot follow the shape. If the degree is close to the number of points, the curve passes through the noise and can swing between the points. A good calibration has a few more points than the degree, spread where the sensor is most sensitive and where it changes most rapidly.</p><h3>Errors that creep in</h3><p><b>Self-heating.</b> The measuring current dissipates power I²R in the sensor. At low temperature the thermal contact to the surroundings is poor, so the sensor warms above the thing it is measuring. <b>Lead resistance.</b> A two-wire measurement adds the resistance of the leads to the sensor's; a four-wire measurement avoids it. The calculator below shows both errors for the same generic sensor.</p><div class=calc><label for=shwt><b>Sensor temperature = <span id=shwtv>4.2</span> K</b></label><input type=range id=shwt min=1 max=300 step=0.1 value=4.2><label for=shwi><b>Excitation current = <span id=shwiv>10</span> µA</b></label><input type=range id=shwi min=-1 max=3 step=0.05 value=1><label for=shwg><b>Thermal conductance to the surroundings = <span id=shwgv>100</span> µW/K</b></label><input type=range id=shwg min=-1 max=4 step=0.05 value=2><label for=shwl><b>Lead resistance in a two-wire measurement = <span id=shwlv>5</span> Ω</b></label><input type=range id=shwl min=0 max=20 step=0.5 value=5><div class=res id=shwo></div><div class=cap id=shwn></div></div><h3>Good habits</h3><ul><li>Use the <b>lowest excitation</b> that gives a clean signal. Lower the current until the reading stops changing.</li><li>Anchor the leads thermally at each stage, so heat does not flow along them into the sensor.</li><li>Keep the sensor in good thermal contact with the thing you are measuring: a bolted copper block, not a free-hanging bead.</li><li>Wait for equilibrium. A thermometer that reads 2 K off during a fast cooldown is not wrong, just late.</li><li>Check the calibration regularly against a fixed point: liquid nitrogen at 77 K, and liquid helium at 4.2 K. Record the date and the result.</li><li>Be careful in magnetic fields: many sensors change their reading in a field.</li></ul>`,
q:"Why must a four-wire measurement be used for a low-resistance sensor, such as a platinum resistor?",o:["It removes the resistance of the leads from the reading, which would otherwise be a large error","It reduces the noise in the heater","It makes the sensor reach equilibrium faster"],a:0,e:"In a two-wire measurement the lead resistance adds to the sensor's. For a low-resistance sensor, or one that changes slowly with temperature, that adds a large temperature error. Four-wire measurement sends current down one pair and senses the voltage with another."}
];
